      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0001" n="art0001">
        <head>--- Parliamentary Notice.</head>
        <p>--- Parliamentary Notice.</p>
        <p>BOARD OF TRADE.—SEssioN 1915. WARRINGTON CORPORATION ELEC- TRIC LIGHTING- (EXTENSION). (The supply of electricity by the Corporation of War- rington in certain parishes or townships in the rural districts of Runcorn and Warrington and the extension of the powers of the Corporation to and over those parishes; purchase of land; com- pulsory area; the repeal or amendment of the Runcorn Urban and Runcorn Rural Electric Lighting Order, 1910, and the incorporation and amendment of other Acts or Orders. 'IV/TICE IS HEREBY GIVEN that the Mayor, Aldermen r.id Burgesses of the County Borough of Warrington (herein referred to as the Corporation), and whose address is the Town Hall, Warrington, intend to apply to the Board of Trade on or before, the 21st day of December next for a Provisional Order (herein referred to as the Order) under the Electric LightiNg Acts, 1882 to 1909, for all or some of the following purposes (that is to say):- 1. To extend the area of supply of the Corporg- tion under the Warrington Electric Lighting Order, 1898 (confirmed by the Electric Lighting Orders Confirmation (No. 11) Act, 1898), so as to include therein the following parishes or townships, namely: Stockton Heath, Latchford Without, Thelwall, Grappenhall, Lower Walton t Higher Walton and Appleton, all in the rural district of Runcorn in the County of Chester; and Penketh and Great Sankey. in the rural dis- trict of Warrington, in the County Palatine of</p>
        <p>tili,---;ll,_—________ -STREET PRIMITIVE METHODIST ki.mon. CHURCH. I,i'llONV (SUNDAY), NOVEMBER 22, 1914. 1,4"17,ing, 10.30: Mr. B. T. PARKES. 11,1;CY-!Lling, 6.30: Mr. A. E. HOYLE, B.A.</p>
        <p>Lancaster; and to authorise the Corporation to supply, distri- bute and sell electrical energy for public and private purposes within the parishes or townships above- named (herein referred to as the extended area of supply). 2. To extend and make applicable to the extended area of supply all or some of the provisions con- tained in the Warrington Electric Lighting Order, 1898, and the Warrington Corporation Act, 1911, and to apply such provisions to the undertaking to be authorised by the Order, subject to such varia- tions and exceptions as may be contained therein. 3. To incorporate some or all of the provisions contained in the Schedule to the Electric Lighting (Clauses) Act, 1899, with such modifications and exceptions as may be indicated in the Order.</p>
        <p>4. To confer upon the Corporation all or some of the powers of the Electric Lighting Acts, 18.82 to 1909, and the enactments incorporated therewith. and to alter, vary, or extinguish all rights and privileges which would or might interfere with any of the objects of the Order, and to confer all other rights and privileges necessary for carrying such ot)erf=. into .rect. 5. To empower the Corporation to supply elec- trical energy for purposes incidental to the working of any railway, tramway, river or canal situate partly within and partly without the extended area of supply.</p>
        <p>_ 6. To alter, extend, repeal or amend the provi- sions of the 'Warrington Electric Lighting Order, 1898, the Warrington Corporation Act. 1911, and the Runcorn Urban and Runcorn Rural Electric Light- ing Order, 1910, and the Acts confirming the same. - -</p>
        <p>7. The streets in which it is proposed that elec- tric lines shall be laid down in the extended area of supply within a specified time are as follows: In the rural district of Runcorn: Old London-road from the borough boundary to the Police Station, Stockton Heath. In the rural district of Warrington: Liverpool-road from the borough boundary to the western boundary of the Sankey Wire</p>
        <p>KGlls. 8. The following are the streets not repairable 14 the local authority and railways which the Corpora- tion propose to take powers to break up:— Borough of Warrington: Greenbank-street and the culvert belonging to the Manchester Ship Canal Company under the said Canal and the approaches thereto. Parish of Walton Superior: Walton Bridge and approaches carrying Hatton and Walton-road over the Bridgewater Canal; Hough's Bridge and approaches carrying Hough's-lane over the Bridgewater Canal. Parish of Stockton Heath: Stockton Bridge and approaches carrying the Northwich-road over the Bridgewater Canal; Red-lane Bridge and approaches carrying Red-latie over the Bridge- water Canal. Parish of Thelwall: Shepherd's-bank Bridges and approaches carrying the Warrington-road over the London and North-Western Railway (Warrington and Stockport branch) and over the old railway siding. Parish of Grappenhall: Bridge and approaches carrying Bell house-lane over the Bridgewater Canal.</p>
        <p>9. To confer upon the Corporation all or some of the powers of the Lands Clauses Act, 1845, to take land otherwise than by agreement for the purpose of a generating or transformer station in respect ' of all that piece or parcel of land situate on the south side of West Avenue, in the parish of Stock- ton Heath in the County of Chester, adjoining the passage. on the west side of the cycle shop in the occupatym of the Royal Alfred Cycle Company, containing by admeasurement 343 square yards or thereabouts, and now in the occupation of Joseph Taylor and Jonathan William Welsby, which lands are more particularly delineated, coloured and described upon a plan and book of reference de- posited at the offices of the Corporation, and in connection therewith to lay a main or mains along Wilderspool-causeway. Greenbank-street, through the north bank of the Manchester Ship Canal, under the said canal by the existing culvert, across Elles- mere-road, and along Old London-road to the trans- former station before mentioned in West-avenue.</p>
        <p>AND NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN that the draft of the Order will be deposited at the Office of the Board of Trade on or before the 21st day of December next, and printed copies of the draft Order when deposited and of the Order when made may be obtained at the price of one shilling for each cepy from the Sub-Postmaster. Great Sankey. and the Sub-Postmaster, Stockton Heath. and from the Town Clerk of Warrington and the Parliamen- tary Agents, whose names are subscribed hereto. AND NOTICE IS HEREBY FURTHER GIVEN that a map showing the boundaries of the proposed extended area of supply. and the streets in which it is proposed that electric lines shall be laid down within a specified time and a plan of the land pro- posed to be taken, with a book of reference thereto and a copy of this advertisement as published in the " London Gazette." will be deposited on or before the 30th day of November, 1914, for public inspection as follows: • _ _</p>
        <p>With the Clerk of the Peace for the County Palatine of Lancaster at his office at Preston ; with the Clerk of the Peace for the County of Chester at his office at Chester: with the Clerks to the Rural District Councils of Runcorn and Warrington respectively at their respective offices at Runcorn and Warrington and with the Town Clerk of War- rington at the Town Hall, Warrington. AND NOTICE IS FURTHER GIVEN that every Local or other Authority, Company, or person de- sirous of bringing before the Board of Trade any objection respecting the application must do so by letter addressed to the Board of Trade marked on the outside of the cover enclosing it " Electric Lighting Acts " on or before the 15th day of January, 1915. and alcony of such objection must also be forwarded to the Town Clerk of Warrington or the Parlia- mentary Agents whose names are subscribed hereto. Dated this 12th November, 1914. J. LYON WHITTLE, Town Clerk, Warrington. BAKER &amp; SONS, 35, Parliament-street, Westminster, Parliamentary Agents.</p>
        <p>Name of Applicant (in full)</p>
        <p>Communications to be addressed to—</p>
        <p>Name</p>
        <p>Address</p>
        <p>Date November, 1911. (a) Applications must be for not less than .1300, and must be for multiples of ,:elOO. (b) Cheques should be made payable to "Bearer," not "To order." and should be crossed " Bank of England."</p>
        <p>PICTUREDRO E TheDa u glhOttehr ol t h the TrCommandmentibe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <head>WAR LOAN,</head>
        <p>WAR LOAN,</p>
        <p>1925-1928. Issue of £350,000,000, (of which £100,000,000 has already been placed in the terms of this prospectus).</p>
        <p>In stock or bonds, bearing interest at 31 per cent, per annum, payable half- yearly on the Ist March and the Ist September.</p>
        <p>Price of issue fixed by HM. Treasury at £95 per Cent. Dividends representing Interest accrued after the 7th December, 1914, on the various Instal- ments as they severally fall due will be paid as follows :</p>
        <p>On the Ist March, 1915 ..80 4 6 percent. On the Ist September, 1915 £1 10 11 per cent. The Stock is an investment authorized by " The Trustee Act, 1893," and Trustees may invest therein notwithstanding that the price may at the time of investment exceed the re- demption value of £lOO per cent. Applications, which must be accompanied by a deposit of .62 per cent., will be received at the Bank of England, Threadneedle-street, London, E.C., and may be forwarded either direct or through the medium of any Banker or Stock- broker in the United Kingdom. Applications must be for even hundreds of pounds. Further payments will be required as follows: ..£l3 per cent. on Monday, the 7th December, 1914 £lO per cent. on Monday, the 21st December, 1914 4:10 per cent. on Thursday, the 7th January, 1915 £lO per cent. on Thursday, the 21st January, 1915 £lO per cent. on Thursday, the 4th February, 1915 .110 per cent. on Monday, the 22nd February, 1915 ..810 per cent. on Thursday, the 11th March, 1915 £lO per cent. on Thursday, the 25th March, 1915 £lO per cent. on Monday, the 12th April, 1915. .810 per cent. on Monday, the 26th April, 1915.</p>
        <p>- 11 THE GOVERNOR AND COMPANY OF THE BANK OF ENGLAND are authorized to receive , applications for this Loan, which will take the form either of Inscribed Stock, or Bonds to Bearer, at the option of the Subscribers. If not previously redeemed, the Loan will be repaid at par on the Ist March, 1928, but His ' Majesty's Government reserve to themselves the right to redeem the Loan at par at any time on, or after, the let March, 1925, on giving not less than three calendar months' notice in the "London Gazette." Both Capital had Interest will be a charge on the Consolidated Fund of the United Kingdom. The books of the Loan will b 4, kept at the Bank of England and at the Bank of Ireland. Dividends will be paid half-yearly on the Ist March and Ist September. Dividends on Stock will be paid by Warrant, which will be sent by post. Dividends on Bonds will be paid by Coupon. Inscribed Stock will be convertible into Bonds to Bearer at any time without payment of any fee; and Bonds to Bearer will be exchangeable for Inscribed Stock on payment of a fee of one shilling per Bond. In case of partial allotment, the balance of the amount paid as deposit will be applied to- wards the payment of the first instalment. Should there be a surplus after making that payment, such surplus will be refunded by cheque. The instalments may be paid in full on or after the 7th December, 1914, under discount at I the rate of 3 per cent, per annum. In case of default in the payment of any instalment by its proper date, the deposit and the instalments previously. paid will be liable to forfeiture. Scrip Certificates to Bearer, with Coupon at- tached for the dividend payable on the Ist March, 1915, will be issued in exchange for the provisional receipts. As soon as these Scrip Certificates to Bearer have been paid in full they can be inscribed (i.e., can be converted , into Stock); or, they can he exchanged for Bonds to Bearer (as soon as these can be pre- pared) in denominations of .tlOO, £2OO, £5OO and .tl,OOO. Inscribed Stock will be transfer- able in any sums which are multiples of a penny. Application Forms may be olitained at the Bank of England and the Bank of Ireland; at any Bank or Money Order Office in the United Kingdom; • of Messrs. Mullens, Marshall and Cd., 13, George-street, Mansion House, 8.C.; and of the principal Stockbrokers. 'The LIST OF APPLICATIONS will he CLOSED on, or before, Tuesday, the 24th November. 1914. Bank of England, London, 17th November, 1914.</p>
        <p>THIS FORM OF APPLICATION MAY BE USED. WAR LOAN, 1925-1928. Issue of £350,000,000. (of which ..t100,000.000 has already been placed in the terms of the prospectus).</p>
        <p>In stock or bonds, bearing interest at 31 per</p>
        <p>cent: per annum. Price of Issue £95 per Cent.</p>
