      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>4›.).).4.44)+,4,)</head>
        <p>4›.).).4.44)+,4,)</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORI4),</p>
        <p>'CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>(instry, for none of our competitors can beat us in production, except in the cheaper 'varieties.</p>
        <p>The Return of the Pocket. Pockets are gradually coming into general use again; at present they are restricted to walking skirts and coats. On the former they are placed at the side, and stitched on the outside, just below the hips. They are quite ornamental ad- ditions to the skirt, and are often braided. The yoke skirt, gathered slightly at the back, with pleated skirt beneath, is a favourite design for renovations. The front and back are quite plain, with a broad box pleat, the fulness being at the sides. Sashes and belts vary very much, and some are exceedingly quaint. Many of the waistless dresses have a superfluity of drapery that enlarges the figure to a degree that is any- thing but graceful. One some models, narrow black silk ribbon takes the place of the usual sash. This is wound round and round the figure, like a soldier's puttee, extending from bust to hips, and ties in bows under the arm, the ends , falling thence to considerably below the waist.</p>
        <p>The Popular Medici Collar and the New Choker. Tailor-made suits of the severe type are fur- nished with the old-fashioned stock collar, very similar to that worn by men in 1830, but less voluminous. A high linen collar that fastens at the back goes round the neck; below this a long stock of black or coloured satin is wound round twice, and tied .in a bow in front, the long ends falling half-way at least to the waist. It is a clumsy contrivance, and altogether in- artistic, but it may find favour on account of its novelty. Another choker collar, generally</p>
        <p>_ 4,4 seen on plainly-made shirt blouses, of crepe de chine, silk, or delaine, is less cumbersome and much more practical, since it is innocent of wire, and made to turn back with a small or large V-shaped opening and revers, or to button up tightly, keeping the neck warm. The medici collar, which seems to suit all types—except of course, the woman whose heck is abnormally short—is still deservedly popular. It is high at the back and sides, and open in front.</p>
        <p>A New Motor Veil. The veil is an important detail of the toilette, and to be effective must be chosen with great care. Shadow-late patterns in tulle are most effective, and a veil that is said to improve any complexion is made of chalk-white tulle, upon which irregularly spaced spots have been worked 1 in blue thread. Adjustment, of course, is a matter of vast importance, and it is an art many women fail to master. Many a dainty hat loses its chic because its wearer is incapable of arranging the veil worn with it, neatly and smartly. Very beautiful as works of art(are the Brussels lace veils shown this year, but some of the patterns on them are so heavy, and the designs are so close together, that they horribly disfigure the face behind them. Much discretion is necessary when making a choice. A new motor veil of the Yashmak type is emi- nently practical and cosy. The upper part that covers the face is of net, the lower of ninon. The latter goes round the neck, giving the warmth required when a collar hi gfi at the neck is worn, for then the cold wind circles round, to the discomfort of the motorist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>By MARJORIE.</head>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Homely Clubs for Women. The success which has attended the opening of the first Women's Cafe and Recreation Rooms at Hammersmith, has encouraged an extension of the movement to other parts of London. The scheme was inaugurated by Mrs. Juson Herr,' and the idea is to provide a meeting-place for women whose men folk are on active service, where they cart enjoy social intercourse, read papers, write letters, and be entertained on certain days of the week. A piano, comfort- able furniture, and necessary kitchen utensils are provided by friends interested in the move- ment, and tea and light refreshments can be obtained at cost price. Altogether it is a very practical effort to combat that evil of which we have heard so much lately—the resort of work- ing women to the public-houses—and one would be glad, to see the opening of these homely clubs for womenfolk in all the large industrial centres.</p>
        <p>Women Toy Makers. • The Women's Emergency Corps is doing much that is useful in finding work for girls and women thrown out of employment by the war, and is making a very practical attempt to cap- ture the toy-making industry of Germany. The peasants who hive in and around the Black Forest, and make the cheap wooden toys that Germany sells to the whole world, work long hours for a mere- pittance, but there is no reason why the better class toys should not be produced in our own villages. It is proposed to send out skilled women instructors to various parts of the country, and where this already has been done, it is encouraging to find that women have rapidly become proficient in the work. Most of the old arts and crafts of the country districts have been killed by machinery, and in places not reached by the factory system there is much unemployed available labour, which only wants organisation and instruction to direct it into useful channels.</p>
        <p>Galalith Buttons. British manufacturers should succeed in cap- turing a great part of the trade in foreign buttons, of which immense quantities are, in the ordinary way, imported from Germany and Austria. The linen button still remains a Brit- ish speciality, but foreign competition is very keen in the manufacture of most other kinds. A new substance obtained from milk, and called " galalith," has lately been invented on the Continent. It is very light, resembles horn, and vegetable ivory, and makes very handsome buttons. British manufacturers are now ex- perimenting with the hope of discovering the method of making galalith, and thus capturing some of the enemy's trade. Brass and gilt but- tons are made almost entirely i Germany, also those of steel, and all ornamental kinds used for blouses. Trouser buttons also come now from Germany. It is to be hoped that in future the public will support the British button in-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>iil 1: gYAreN2sOS6"M",2IOFNVA244244SI(' Mrat(ra CHILDREN'S CORNER. i t= Conducted by UNCLE NED. k. , $ i s)ztgsOvtka-qsos6tot2AY-tCcO4S2.sCk</head>
        <p>iil 1: gYAreN2sOS6"M",2IOFNVA244244SI(' Mrat(ra CHILDREN'S CORNER. i t= Conducted by UNCLE NED. k. , $ i s)ztgsOvtka-qsos6tot2AY-tCcO4S2.sCk</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,— Soon after my last week's letter was in your hands our Enipire was plunged into mourn- ing through the death of that veteran soldier, Lord Roberts. , You will probably know that he died in France after inspecting the Indian troops, so that it is not necessary for me to go into details about his death. Instead I am collecting a few true stories about him, and these I hope to give you next week. He was a man to be proud of, and every Britisher will always remember " Bobs " as a true soldier and a true friend of soldiers. He died as he would have wished—whilst serv- ing the country he loved so well. Throughout his life he tried to help others, and this is what I have been trying to impress upon all my nephews and nieces. Last week I gave two examples of how the boys and girls from the Lancaster-street and Piekmere-street districts have done their share. Were there any of my young friends amongst them? If there were I would be pleased to hear from them, for I would like them to tell me how they made their arrangements. Last week I stated that I would announce a new competition to-day, and it will take the form of an essay on "How should I spend this Christmas?" The closing date will be Tues- day, December 15th. Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TERRIBLE TOOTHACHE.</head>
        <p>A TERRIBLE TOOTHACHE.</p>
        <p>. If any of you should have a toothache—and it's very likely that you will—just look into the mirror and notice how small is the little white tormentor that is causing so much pain. Then —by way of comfort—reflect how much worse it would be if this tiny white thing were an elephant's great tusk, with toothache all through it. At Exeter Change, in London, there was, many years ago, a menagerie in the second story of the building., Here the elephant—" Chunee" by name—a very quiet, well-trained beast—was confined in a cage, under which the floor had been strengthened to support his weight. Chunee never came out, but seemed very happy. Suddenly he became raving mad, and screamed and trumpeted, and endeavoured vigorously to tear away the iron bars of his cote. Now, if he had succeeded in getting out upon the floor, Mr. Chunee would have immediately dropped through into an apothecary-shop below. If he had fallen into the scales, his exact weight might have been ascertained, after a fashion; but, in other respects, a mad elephant in a drugstore would have been far worse than a bull in a china-shop. If he had been sane, he might have had a nice time eating the licorice and cough lozenges and sugar-coated pills and candy, but as he wasn't sane the accident was not to be desired. _ _</p>
        <p>Chunee -g-r-eNV-more and more wild and dan- gerous, until, at last, the "Beefeaters," who are the keepers of the Tower of London, were called upon to destroy the poor beast. They discharged many balls from their old-fashioned muskets into his body, but loss of blood seemed to increase his fury, and not lessen his strength. There were no rifle teams in those days to reach his brain with a single shot, so a piece of artillery was actually brought up, and poor Chunee, obeying his keeper's voice, even in his rage, kneel d down and was shot to death with a cannon ball. Now just think of what an awful thing six feet of toothache must have been, and pity poor Chunee!</p>
        <p>THE WELL-BELOVED. (By TEssig SINN.) Thou Well-beloved, a cause is won By thy strong service given. Death's silent barque now drifteth Towards the shore of Heaven. " Bobs!" rest in peace. Thy duet is dear ; We lay thee 'mid the great: Falls on thy tomb a splendid tear, From Empire as from State.</p>
        <p>But who shall follow in thy train, From out the eternal throng? None from thy dust shall rise, again To vanquish Empire wrong! Sleep on. The mighty task of earth Was well performed by thee: Then wake, in higher realms, to birth, And lead to victory.</p>
        <p>Xmas Puddings for the Forces. Make puddings for your soldier friends with ATORA Shredded Beef Suet. They will be more delicious than if made with raw suet. Requires no chopping. Ask your grocer for it. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SPIRITUALISM'S ANTIQUITY.</head>
        <p>SPIRITUALISM'S ANTIQUITY.</p>
        <p>Lecture on a Cult that is as "Old as the Hills."</p>
        <p>" We owe a greater debt to Spiritualism than many of us think!" To justify this statement of his, Mr. Walter Howell, of Birmingham, delivered an instructive lecture at the Druids' Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. Councillor E. Osborne presided over a good attendance.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Stout, president of the Warrington Spiritualist Society, said that his reason for inviting Councillor Osborne to preside was that it had been thought desirable to have a chair- man who, whilst outside their movement, was sympathetically disposed towards it. He would like to point out a significant statement made on Sunday by a local Nonconformist minister, in reference to the death of Lord Roberts: "We know that, although he is dead, his spirit still lives; and that he will be able to carry on his work more effectively now than if he had been alive !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WHY HE WAS IN THE CHAIR.</head>
        <p>WHY HE WAS IN THE CHAIR.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he would like to explain why he was in the chair. It was his desire that people should listen to lectures even if they did not agree with what the lecturer had to tell them. He had a keen desire for know- ledge. They were apt to be perturbed, at pre- sent, about things; but, if they would attend more meetings similar to that one, they would be helped to combat those evil influences which were disturbing their minds and thoughts. Whilst some of them were concerned about the municipal debt, that was not their only debt; and he was afraid that many of them spent their time considering only mundane matters, and neglected the spiritual ones. They had to realise that they were not bodies alone, nor spirits alone, but both. Whilst their bodies isolated them, their spirits united them. What- ever opponents might say in criticism, if they would only grasp their own personal situations and desires and attributes, and if they would only realise that they had a divinity in them, which could be presented in their own homes, he was satisfied that the citizenship of their town would be nobler, better and purer. (Ap- plause.) All were critics, and the most ignor- ant man was very often the keenest critic! He could not, however, stand those people who never said anything, and pretended they were clever; he would rather they spoke, even if they made mistakes. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SPIRITUALISM AS OLD AS THE HILLS.</head>
        <p>SPIRITUALISM AS OLD AS THE HILLS.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said he did not want anybody to think that the fundamental principles of Spirit- ualism were modern, for they were as old as the hills. If they could say that God was a spirit; then all His manifestations were spiritual phenomena; and they must realise themselves in a world that was infinite in an infinite variety of ways. Spiritualism had been there long before the revival of modern Spiritualism, but for some centuries it was not spoken about very much, because it was regarded as an offence against the individuals concerned. They owed many debts to modern Spiritual- ism. It had, for one thing, converted millions of people who would otherwise have remained in doubt as to man's possible survival of death, and would therefore have come to fear death; and it gave those who remained strength in the ordeal of bereavement. It had also stimulated scientific research, and had led to the discovery of many things. It had done much towards the emancipation of negro-slavery. History had been vindicated from Genesis to Revelation by it, whilst mental healing had done untold good through it. (Applause.) The Chairman, in thanking Mr. Howell for his lecture, said much as man knew, there was a great deal beyond his ken. Our trying to criticise the acts of God was like an ant trying to see things as man sees them.</p>
        <p>A formal vote of thanks was passed to the Lecturer and the Chairman, on the motion of Mr. W. E. Stout.</p>
        <p>Little Girl's Terrible Fate. Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an inquest at the Police Buildings on Monday afternoon concerning the death of Catherine Kelly, aged three years, who resided with her Parents at 93 Athol-street Earlestown. From the evidence Of the mother 'it appeared that she was bringing in clothing from the yard on TlilirsdaY of last week when she saw the child on the sofa with her clothing on fire. She called for assistance and the child was taken to the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary, where death p.m. on Friday. The fire- d occurred d moved from its place, and there ogcucaurra t been eight was burnt paper on the floor.—The doctor's re- port stated that the child was suffering from "extensive burns" when admitted.—A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE COURTS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Furness, W. Roberts, G. Pilling and T. Locker. A " F ishy " Story.—John Towey, 3, Gas-street, was charged with having been found on the enclosed premises of a warehouse in St. Aus- tin's-lane.—The Chief Constable said that Mr. McCabe, the owner of the Warehouse, went to it at 6.40 p.m. on Saturday and discovered that the padlock had been broken. Prisoner was inside and pretended to be asleep. Mr. McCabe secured the door and went for P.C. Taylor. When they got to the warehouse again pris- oner had decamped with about one shillings- worth of fish, having effected his escape by breaking a window. When arrested prisoner tried to make out that it was a drunken freak. —Prisoner: I fell through the door and I did not know where I was, so I broke the window to get out.—The Chief: And the fish walked down his throat, your Worships, without assist- ance. (Laughter.)—Towey was committed to prison for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Messrs. Hay- ward, C. Jackson Holmes, and W. T. Williams. Said She Was "An Old Customer."—Sarah Rawlinson, a married woman, of 12, Morris- court, was charged with obtaining by false pre- tences, on November 11th, two pairs of boots, valued at 14s. 6d., the property of Thomas Broadhurst, boot dealer, of 76, Mersey-street.— Superintendent Williamson asked for a remand, stating that the woman went into the shop whilst Mr. Broadhurst was out, representing that she was an old customer; and obtained, from the assistant the boots in question. These were afterwards pledged at two pawnshops. It was subsequently found that the woman was not known at all to Mr. Broadhurst. —The re- mand was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>DOCTORS AND HEALTH.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS AND HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Fish and Chips, Pheasant and</p>
        <p>Cham- Pagne.</p>
        <p>Health topics seem to have a great fascination for Adult Schools. _ _</p>
        <p>Dr. Manson's lecturette to the Stockton Heath School attracted a large and interested audi- ence. The Doctor began by traversing the doc- trine of Mr. Chesterton, that the ordinary medical man knows little about health, because he is constantly in an atmosphere of disease. Statistics had been produced in years gone by to show how far down the doctors came in the list of occupational mortality. This seemed to show that the medical man had not much idea of keeping his own health. The public could turn round and say, "Physician, heal thyself!" On the other hand, there were other statistics to show that the infantile death-rate in doc- tors' families was extraordinarily low, indicat- ing that at least medical men could look after their own offspring with remarkable care. Chesterton's idea seemed to be that the jour- nalist was a safer guide to health than the doctor. But unfortunately the journalist had not even the few facts which usually sufficed for his professional work. Dr. Manson pointed out how almost all the great religions of the world had tried to take the question of health under their wing. From the Ganges, the sacred cleansing river, to the miracles of Lourdes and Holywell, there were evidences of the interest shown by religion in health. It was important to remember that health was not an end in itself, but a means to an end. As a famous English judge had said, "Doctors are the men who help people to carry on their work." It was quire posdible for people to think too much about their own health. Sometinns it became an obsession. Hence arose those modern mock-religions, based upon a false philosophy. Questions in quick succession showed how keen the members were to receive further en- lightenment. Food reform, inoculation, sani- tation, and various methods of burial came up for discussion. As illustrating the modern divorce from Nature, which has come about through industrialism, the lecturer mentioned .the case of an old woman, who now lives on fish and chips from a shop, who well remembers how some kind of porridge used to be the staple breakfast dish in most working class homes. He was careful to point out that the condemna- tion of fish and chips ought to extend to the pheasant and champagne dinners, which often became the life standard in other strata of humanity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
        <p>, 4.),•:**:•.+4•40.4,* 4. 4) 0, •:,• •:,• •:”:”:4, •:'• •:•.:”:,..:. 4. 4,70. ..) • 4). , .:.4,4.4..04 4.1. ..:. .:..&gt; .0,4 4.:,..:,.. 0, .:. 44 ......),......"...) . 4,..</p>
        <p>o 4. 4,••:• .4* •:• 4, 40,1,</p>
        <p>PHILOMATIIS AND CONSCRIPTION</p>
        <p>Ridicule for " Saturday Afternoon Soldiering." CASE FOR VOLUNTARISM.</p>
        <p>"Should conscription be adopted in this country?" This was the subject of a debate held under the auspices of the Philomathic Society in their rooms on Wednesday evening. The affirmative view was taken by Mr. C. S. Woods and the negative by Mr. H. Dutton. Mr. N. Nield (the president) presided over a good attendance.</p>
        <p>OUR UNPREPARED "TERRIERS." In his opening Mr. Woods said it was his intention to go as fully 'as he could into the question of the Territorial Force and explain how inadequate it was for the purpose for which it was formed. When the force was created it was supposed to consist of 315,000 men, but the actual numbers were only 265,000 in 1913. Almost half of the force had not qualified even in the preparatory stage of rifle shooting. It was ludicrous to think that we had been plac- ing the defence of our shores into the hands of men who had not fired 100 rounds of aminuni- tion. Then again the small amount of train- ing given to the men did net tend to bring about discipline. The whole thing had savoured too much of Saturday afternoon soldiering. Now that war had broken out the force was under- going training, instead of. being an efficient force ready to repel any invasion which might have taken place. Supposing the rising in Egypt had come off, the Indian Army could not have left India, and our own men would have had to go and leave England at the mercy of the enemy, for our only me-alia of defence in addition to the Navy would be .the Territorial Force, which had just started training. One of the arguments against his idea was that we could not be invaded, but supposing the, Ger- man fleet did away with our fleet, they Would either blockade or invade. He was of the opinion that invasion would be fTie more likely. In order to deal effectively with an,invasion we ought to have a proper army, fully equipped, and, under a voluntary system, we had no such army. • Of course, it had been saTil that if an invasion took place the nation would spring to arms. It would be a bad thing to do, because there would be none of the discipline and energy which was absolutely essential to suc- cess, and there would be none of the necessary training. The courage of the nation would only hasten its destruction. The men in the worthy and excellent training corps which had ' recently sprung up would acknowledge that if they were against trained men it would be a case of butchery pure and simple. The advan- tages to a nation with compulsory service were immense—physically, morally and socially. The cost of such an army would be about 100 per cent, more than the cost of the present army. but there would be a diminution in the naval .expenditure. The expenditure on the present army was out of propiirtion to the results achieved. Compulsory service would be of great advantage to the country all round. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>~•</p>
        <p>INVASION MORE REMOTE THAN EVER. Mr. Dutton said the burden of Mr. Woods' argument seemed to be based on the supposition that if our fleet were defeated. Germany would be able to land, say, 70,000 men. If the 'fleet were beaten neither conscription nor the Ter- ritorial Force could save us. That would never happen, but supposing it did, the German lines of communicition would be cut off, and it would be practically suicidal for them to try it, A permanent system of con- scription was unnecessary in this coun- try. "The Day" had now come; • ' our fleet had swept the seas, as was expected, and the much-talked-of invasion was further away than ever. We had proved recently that one volunteer was worth ten pressed men. The present war was one against excessive militar- ism, from which the German people would be glad to be freed. Those who advocated conscrip- tion were running contrary to the essential characteristics of the country: Conscription bred the spirits of servility and submission, which were mis-called obedience and punctual- ity. Such a spirit was incompatible with the watchword of liberty. Conscription would double our expenditpxe for national defence. It was argued that it would break down class distinctions, but they all knew of the arrogance of the German officers. Zabern was well within their recollection. Conscription, considered as a means of elevating the social, physical and moral sides of life, was not to be trusted. Phy- sical benefits could be obtained by reforms, which the vast expenditure that conscription would necessitate would render impossible. As a means of breaking down class distinction, conscription was doubtful, in view of the aris- tocracy and plutocracy in the country; and, , again, it would render wars of aggression more likely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>BRITISH COMPROMISE.</head>
        <p>A BRITISH COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Chadwick said he believed that a form of conscription on common-sense lines would meet our requirements. Dr. Manson said that instead of voting for one side or the other, they should all be British and vote for a compromise. The degrees of voluntary service were things to be considered, for at the present time there were men who did not join out of a pure spirit of patriotism, but in many cases were forced to join by various reasons. A spirit of compromise, whereby every man could be called upon to give some service to the country would be advantageous. Mr. H. Sherburn argued that the men in the Army and in the Territorials were not ade- quately dealt with as regards payment: He was of the opinion that unless they exhausted all the possibilities of the voluntory system, con- scription should be kept in the background.</p>
        <p>SATISFACTORY VOLUNTARY SYSTEM. Mr. Rathbone said that so far as he could see it was not economically sound nor expedient to introduce conscription into this country. All military expenditure was recognised as unpro- ductive, and under conscription the men would be taken from productive employment and made non-producers. Therefore, the wealth of the country would be reduced and taxation in- creased. People had argued, "If you will have peace, prepare for war." Every nation. in Europe had been preparing for war, and now there was the greatest war in history taking place. The present voluntary system was quite satisfactory, for we had the largest overseas army in the world. A splendid example of the efficacy of the voluntary system was to be seen at the present time. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>lEr. P. S. Dunn said that the conflict had come about because we were not prepared for war and Germany was. If we had had a system .4f conscription there would not have been any war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CONSCRIPTION ON SWISS LINES AS AN</head>
        <p>CONSCRIPTION ON SWISS LINES AS AN</p>
        <p>ALTERNATIVE.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. Bamforth said that if we had a system of Imperialism we should not need to go on in- creasing armaments. _ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman expressed himself in favour of a compromise, and agreed with Dr. Bamforth.l He was of the opinion, however, that so far as the defence of the country was concerned, there was no sensible argument to be brought against conscriptiom on the lines adopted in Switzerland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>VOTING.</head>
        <p>THE VOTING.</p>
        <p>In reply to the various speakers, Mr. Dutton said that Germany had absolutely made up her miud to dominate Europe, and conscription might have delayed the present war, but could never have prevented it. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Woods said that men were now rolling up behind their time. They ought to have been prepared before. Those who were against con- scription did not say what they would do, but stood by without doing anything. On a vote being taken the majority agreed with Mr. Woods. Several members, however, abstained from voting on the direct question.</p>
        <p>Like Poetry.</p>
        <p>A reporter asked John J. Hayes, the winner of the Marathon race, if long distance running was difficult.</p>
        <p>Running, said the young man, "is a good deal like writing poetry. And you know what Victor Hugo said abolit that."</p>
        <p>"No. What did he say?" asked the reporter. " A visitor," said Mr. Hayes, "asked Hugo if it was hard to make good poetry. "'Hard?' said Hugo, with a laugh. 'Hard? Bless me, no. It is either quite easy or—im- possible.' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PRESBYTERIAN DIAMOND JUBILEE</head>
        <p>PRESBYTERIAN DIAMOND JUBILEE</p>
        <p>Sixtieth Anniversary Services Continued</p>
        <p>AT ST. JOHN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Diamond jubilee celebration services in con- nection with St. John's Presbyterian Church— the first of which were held the previous week -L-were continued on Sunday, when the special preacher for the day was the Rev. Wm. McNeill, of Birkenhead (and late of Dublin), and when large congregations attended each service. In the morning the preacher look as his text Luke xxi. 19: " In your patience possess ye your souls."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EVERY MAN'S DIVINE HUNGER.</head>
        <p>EVERY MAN'S DIVINE HUNGER.</p>
        <p>This text, said the preacher, gave at the same time both a precept and a promise. For the disciples there was a life of anguish and trial, but God kept watch over His own. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ They could gather their Lord's general teach- ing as to the soul, the existence of which He assumed. In all men there was a Divine hunger for purity and peace. Jesus knew this, and appealed to it. Every man had, and was, a soul; when he was stripped of all the circum- stances that had gathered around him during his progress through life, there remained the man himself—his soul. That was what a man was worth, no more and no less. And Jesus said quite distinctly that it was possible for a man to lose his soul, through carelessness and sin and easy-going indifference. There was also the opposite and corresponding truth—a man could win his soul!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CONVERSION AND SALVATION.</head>
        <p>CONVERSION AND SALVATION.</p>
        <p>Now the winning of the soul took time; it was k progressive affair. It had been said that con- version and salvation were the same thing; but the former, though the first and deciding step. Was not salvation. It started the winning of the soul, and that Meant a great deal; but it took time to win the soul—probably it took eternity! How fared it with them in that business of soul-winning? When the shock of death came, would it find in them something that death could not destroy? He supposed there was no reason why a man's soul should not thrive in Prosperity, -sunshine and happiness. The.char- acters of some people were so thankful and generous that no prosperity could alter them. But, by most, the soul was won through trial and by patience; and had they made as much of their trials as they might have done?</p>
        <p>Then, welcome each rebuff That turns earth's smoothness rough, Each sting that bids not sit Nor stand, but go! Be our joys three parts pain! Strive, and hold cheap the strain; Learn, nor account the pang; dare, • Never grudge the throe! The winning of the soul was a matter of trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SOUL OF THE NATION.</head>
        <p>THE SOUL OF THE NATION.</p>
        <p>This, true of individuals, was true also of nations; for nations had souls, which could be lost and won. They would agree that, during the past years of prosperity, all had not been well with their country—she had achieved material things and forgotten spiritual ones. Who had not trembled for the soul of England? Now the whole nation was passing under a great wave of anxiety, and in that trial life's values would undergo a change. They would see the worth of the pure; they would be encouraged to take up their Lord's teirching—and not only to profess it, but to live up to it. Chastened and humbled, they would emerge victorious—in that England had won, not a great war, but a great soul, which, but for that trial, she might have lost!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>STRENGTH THROUGH SUFFERING</head>
        <p>STRENGTH THROUGH SUFFERING</p>
        <p>"Our Victory Is The Victory God Wants."</p>
        <p>In the evening the reverend gentleman took for his text, '" Father, if Thou be willing, re- move this cup from Me; nevertheless, not My Will, but Thine, be done.' And there appeared an angel unto Him from heaven strengthening Him," St. Luke xxii. 42, 4.3. Those words were precious to them, said the preacher, for they assured them that our Lord's prayer was not unanswered. Although the cup was not re- moved, He was made strong. There were many people who found Christ through suffering, and it was there, in the Garden of Gethsemane, that they met Him when He was most human. In these days of trial people were drawing nearer to Christ through suffering. Perhaps in the modern stress and strain they would find affinities with Christ which they never ex- pected. Perhaps a look of recognition would pass between them and Christ at last ! He (the preacher) had no doubt but, that during the trials and anguishing days of the war, a large number of iken and women would come to know Christ ..an the felfowship of His suffering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PRAYER LIKE A BOAT HOOK.</head>
        <p>PRAYER LIKE A BOAT HOOK.</p>
        <p>----• ••--• Dealing with the power of prayer, the preacher said that every prayer was bpunded by submis- sion. The beginner's idea of "prayer was that of the child, "Ask for whatever you want. -Try to bring God's will down to your own !" Prayer, however, was like a boat-hook. They did not use a boat-hook to draw the land to the boat, but to draw the boat to the land. Prayer was man's boat-hook. He could not use it to draw God's will down to his, but to draw his will up to God. They should prdy for what was right, and in connection with the war pray for the victory of the Allies; the triumph of right- eousness and peace. Their prayers would be answered, becausb the victory they wanted was the victory God wanted. They ought to be en- couraged to look for, and expect, indirect an- swers to prayer. God did not take the cup from His Son, but sent an angel to strengthen Him. They had all been praying for a prosperous issue of the war, and, because the end was still far out of sight, some people were speaking as though their prayers had not been answered. That was a great mistake. He knew scores of men and women who had gone from a prayer meeting, or private devotion, carrying a burden of anxiety, but who had gone with brave step, steeled to quiet indifference, to patience, and to steadfastness, which had not been the case with them before. Was not the courage and pluck which the men and women of the country were showing an answer to prayer? That was the angel from heaven come to strengthen them. They had asked.for victory and trusted to have it in God's good time, but, in, the meantime, they must not forget that God had sent them strength_ in this time of stress and strife.</p>
        <p>Special hymns and psalms were sung by the choir, under the leadership of Mr. A. G. Lewsey, organist, whilst at the evening service the Misses Mounfield and Smith rendered the duet, "Our Blessed Redeemer" (Mee Pattison). The anthem, "Lift up your heads, 0 ye gates !" (Hopkins) was rendered in a creditable manner by the choir.</p>
        <p>TO-MORROW'S COMMEMORATIVE SERVICES. I The special services commemorative of the Act which sixty years ago made St. John's an integral part of revived Presbyterianism in England will be brought to a close to-morrow (Sunday), when the minister of the congrega- tion (the Rev. William Reid) will occupy the pulpit, taking for a morning subject "The Vision of Habakkuk," while in the evening the I topic of the occasion, "The Year of Jubilee," will find a prominent place. Large congrega- tions waited upon the ministry of Dr. Monro Gibson and the Rev.. William McNeill on the past two Sundays, and it is pretty certain that to-morrow's services will prove no less a fea- ture in the special season. The congregation at the commencement set itself to the high ideal of uniting with the celebration of this sixtieth birthday the realisation of a £6O offering, and rejoice in having so far reached within .tl5 of their goal. The minister and office-bearers are very anxious it should be known that a most cordial welcome will be extended to all with any past connection, or present sympathy, with the con- gregation, who are able tiO attend the social on Thursday evening of nest week.</p>
        <p>Misunderstood. The Kaiser is reported to have received a nice letter from his old friend Abdul ("the D d "), pointing out that it is the fate of some kind and gentle souls to be misunderstood. —" Punch."</p>
      </div>
