      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>AT SMALL EXPENSE BY FITTING ONE OF OUR BARLESS FRONTS.</head>
        <p>AT SMALL EXPENSE BY FITTING ONE OF OUR BARLESS FRONTS.</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND 51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE ST. , WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sole Agent for the celebrated "Herald" Cooking Ranges and "Peveril" Fire Grates—still ahead of all others.</p>
        <p>retters fa the ebftfl NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Tap EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites CO spondence on all subjects of public interese LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Thni, object must be to impart or seek inforinabe not abuse. LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied bi</p>
        <p>the name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the Ice! except urgent matters—can always be Pc' the best attention.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL AND WAR. Sir,—Permit me, through the mediunl„•Oi] your columns, to state that the defeat of rington on Saturday, was a very good tlvri for the town. In future the absurd £ootb.3c excitement will relax, and perhaps we will glo more men to the Army, whose daily need forces is obvious. . • 'Ol I trust that I shall find per Sons of my 01,'111, in your paper, whose feelings are more patriotism than of games. " cumtempore," let us hope will be the faro* thought of these football maniacs in future._ j, BOW'</p>
        <p>" Trelawny," Latchford, November 15th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"AFTER 11111 WAR."</head>
        <p>"AFTER 11111 WAR."</p>
        <p>Christadelphian Interpretation of Biblical Prophecy.</p>
        <p>The third lecture in the "After the War" series was delivered at the Christadelphian Meeting Room, Bold-street, on Monday evening, when Mr. A. S. Wadsworth, of Keighley, spoke upon "The Kingdom of God." The outline of the lecture was "a divine, political kingdom to be established upon the earth, with Christ as King, sitting upon the throne of David." Mr. Joseph P. Jennings, who presided 'over a good attendance, read, by way of introduction, Matthew vi.-7-the second portion of the Sermon on the Mount. GOD'S APPROACHING GLORY.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said that there were kingdoms upon the earth, at present, which were very evidently not divine. Man had come to see that the world was not ruled by wisdom, Mit by greed. In .Revelation iv. 11, it was said: " Thou art worthy, 0 Lord, to receive glory and honour and power, for Thou hait created all things, and for Thy pleasure they are and were created." Had God found pleasure in the things they saw? No, but He had made it clear that the earth shall be filled with His glory, and that there should be. " peace and good-will toward men." That was what Jesus had been born for.</p>
        <p>The term "the Kingdom of God" had been, greatly• misunderstood—men said it was either a state of the heart, or a state a felicity some- where beyond the skies. But what was it? They had been told to pray: "Thy Kingdom come; thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven." The divine kingdom was to be estab- lished on earth—" Thine, 0 Lord, is . . . all that is in the heavep and in the earth . . . thine is the kingdom . . . and thou art exalted as head above all" (1 Chron. xxix. 11). In 2 Chron. ix. it was acknowledged by the other nations that Israel was the chosen early land: "Happy are thy men, and . . thy servants, which stand continually before thee and hear thy wisdom. Blessed be the Lord thy God, which . . . set thee on His throne, to be king for the Lord thy God: because thy God loved Israel, to establish them `for ever, . . to do judgment and justice." Even after the children of Israel had been permitted to enter the land, God reserved it for Himself—" The land shall. not be sold for ever: for the land is Mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with Me" (Leviticus xxv. 23). ISRAEL'S DESTINY. —•-</p>
        <p>Besides providing the land for His chosen people (Deuteronomy xiv. 2—" Thou art an holy people unto the Lord thy God, and the Lord hath chosen thee to be a peculiar people unto Himself, above all the nations that are upon the earth") God had nominated their future king. "When thou art come unto the land which the Lord thy God giveth thee," ran Deuteronomy xvii. 14-15, ". . and shalt possess it . . thou shalt set him king over thee whom the Lord thy God shalt choose." God also gave them a law, as shown in Deuteronomy xxxi. 9: "And Moses wrote this law, and delivered it unto the priests the sons of Levi, which bare the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and unto all the elders of Israel." In chapter xvii. of the same book, He gave Israel many instruc- tions as to their laws. As to the foundation of God's earthly kingdom, it was stated that "it shall come to pass, when thy days be expired, that thou must go to be with thy fathers, that I will raise up thy seed after thee, which shall be of thy sons; and I will establish his king- dom" (1 Chron. xvii. 11). \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>"KING OF THE JEWS."</head>
        <p>"KING OF THE JEWS."</p>
        <p>Who was to rule over Israel in the coming days? Luke i. 30 told them that "the angel said unto her, Fear not, Mary: for thou bast found favour with God,'" and continued: "Thou shalt bring forth a son . . Jesus, . . . and the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David: and he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WAR GOD'S PUNISHMENT.</head>
        <p>WAR GOD'S PUNISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Nation Must Turn From Luxury and __ Pleasure-Loving.</p>
        <p>-The annual meeting of the Warrington branch of the English Chuich Union was held in Fairfield Schools on Saturday afternoon. The Rev. W. J. Stanton presided, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. J. 11. G. Hollingworth (secretary).--The Chairman was re-elected. President of the Warrington branch and of the South-West District Union.—ln ack- nowledgment the reverend gentleman said they required a large number of members. The following officials for the Warrington branch were also elected :—Vice-chairman and treasurer, Mr. C. S. Burgess; secretary, Rev. J. H. Cii.•Hollingworth; agent, Rev. J. Bell Cox; committee, Rev. J. Holden, Mr. E. Hall Coates, Mr. F. M. Darby, and Miss White. PROGRESS MAINTAINED LOCALLY.</p>
        <p>The Secretary presented the Treasurer's- re- port, which showed that the year was com- menced with a sum of lie. 11d, and that there was now .13 Is. 4d. in hand.—ln his own report as secretary, the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth said that progress had been maintained during the past year. They had now a membership of 55, and there had been no withdrawals. .THE WAR IN THE LIGHT OF RELIGION. In the course of his address, the President appealed for more members, and pointed out the aims and objects of the society. Referring to the war, he said be thought they ought to examine the present struggle in the light of religion. It had tried the faith of many people, who argued "How is it God can allow such horrors now?" and "Why does He not step in and prevent such horrors?" or "How can He be a just and merciful God to allow these things?" He (the speaker) was of the opinion that, instead of trying their faith, the war ought to make their faith stronger, because it was only another instance of the way in which God had always worked. Because they had been enjoying a large mea- sure of prosperity the people had become luxury and pleasure-loving and had disregarded God's commandments. The war was God's punish- ment. The people must turn away from being excessively pleasure-loving and resolve to he better iu the future. The war had been sent in mercy, for they had been prevented from striving amongst themselves and had to join to- gether in fighting a common enemy, who had yielded to ideas which were heathen and not Christian. When the war was over they would realise Christ more fully, to their own advan- tage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>VICAR AND _ %VAR-TIME MERRY-</head>
        <p>VICAR AND _ %VAR-TIME MERRY-</p>
        <p>MAKING.</p>
        <p>People Who Are Ready_ To "Dance</p>
        <p>Over the New-made Graves."</p>
        <p>_ Writing in the current issue of his Parish Magazine, the Vicar of Burtonwood the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) says : " Our people, the greater number of them, still need to be reminded of the Sunday and week-day intercessions on behalf of our soldiers and sailors exposed to so many and great dan- gers. How does it come to pass that there is so much indifference,, callous indifference in our midst: that our people sit 'down to eat, rise up to play with, an unconcern indicative of a dis- tressing heartlessness? Is it a time for dancing, whist drives, drinking, and miscalled merry- making? How can people find pleasure and sat- isfaction in amusements, frivolity, and even drunkenness at a time when they should con- sider their ways, humiliate themselves by fast,. big and self-denial, falling low upon their knees before the Almighty? We confess to a sense of tteep shame and intense pain at the selfish, heartless attitude of the greater number of our people at this time of unique distress and awful chastisement. May God be merciful to those who are ready to dance over the new- made graves a our Tommies,' to hold high revel in the midst of death, to throw the dice and play cards on the tombstones of the slain !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FOR SICK AND WOUNDED BRITISH HO</head>
        <p>FOR SICK AND WOUNDED BRITISH HO</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln spite of the excellent arrangeale„,3, and splendid work carried out by the An Veterinary Corps for the care of the sick lie wounded horses of the British Forces nt to. front,' the public has long felt a desire to cof operate in the humane and economic Work this department. ad, It is interesting here to mention that alre 4„4 some 23,000 horses have been drafted int° hospitals, and tended with such care that lars: numbers have been returned fit to the fr; The Army Veterinary Corps has already al,siby itself of the assistance of the R.S.P.C."' to drafting a large number of its inspectors 1,110 the ranks of that corps, and the society 'll now received the official sanction of the Arno Council to aid the cause in coping withct t', increased demand on its resources. This tion is conveyed in the following words: they will be grateful for your society's fora assistance being started by your society for the puroblee'l of hospital requisites for sick and ivuen' horses." • I have gladly accepted the position of Cbai man of a special Council to organise the full and I appeal with confidence to all for finaur assistance to enable the R.S.P.C.A. to assie'i coping with this admirable work. Cheques this special fund may be sent to the Secretfr R.S.P.C.A., 105, Jermyn-street, London, or to Mr. C. A. Phillimore, who has consented to act as honorary treasiret, Messrs. Coutts and Co.'s Bank, 440, 5."4 London, W.C.</p>
        <p>PORTLAND, Chairnian., Royal Society for the Prevention Cruelty to Animals. 105, Jermyn-street, London, S.W., November, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>THIRTY YEARS TREASURER' Interesting Presentation to Well. Known Oddfellow.</head>
        <p>THIRTY YEARS TREASURER' Interesting Presentation to Well. Known Oddfellow.</p>
        <p>or There was a large attendance of members r the Independent Order of Oddfellows (3.ltEpcllBoo ter Unity) at the " Constitution " I,°"°- 'on Saturday, the occasion being the presenter on behalf of the members, by the Grand Mfass, of the Warrington District (Bro. Harry '7O, rence), to P.G. Wm. Watson, of a gold wit, 50 chain: in recognition of his services duri"</p>
        <p>years' membership of the "Widows 1,00 Orphans Fund," for 30 years of which hews acted as treasurer. Amongst the raenic.S. present were Prov. D.G.M. Spann, Pros'. Webster, P.P.G.M.'s J. Massey, w. A. , s ,Boyle Ellis Clare, T. P. Bradshaw. ers Thuntith, and many Paslo,Grands and offic of other lodges. N.G. Davies presided. di After the business of the lodge had been ,A, posed of songs, &amp;c., were reralered by 13r°8,1, Boyle, W. Webster, J. Massey, A. Hare, 81' Ellison, Bro. Pemberton accompanying.</p>
        <p>OVER HALF A CENTURY IN THE Orw.,:,iee During an interval Prov. G.M. H. Law."-ile was called upon to make the presentation. „c 1 spoke highly of the valuable services rencle'w by Bro. Watson to the cause of Oddfellevillie in Warrington for upwards of 50 years' „. of fine gathering that evening was a testino%id, the high esteem in which Bro. Watson was ::.11 , Jy" and his 30 years' service as treasurer, n13%,;,0f of the Widows and Orphans Fund, but als'i„vt his lodge, was a record of which any Ocldfehillf might well be proud. As long as the lodges of retained, as many of them did, the service: of their elder brethren to assist the fine a's' gji young and capable officers now going thrno the various chairs, the Grand Master bld fear but that the Manchester lin,.;`% it maintain that pre-eminent position 'a" held in the Friendly Society movement.</p>
        <p>The War and OddfellowshiP• The great war would bring heavy fin—lhel responsibilities upon the members, but t re could he fully met, and in those cases n'h„eu- Oddfellows laid down their lives for their CO".; try, the mfmbers- must see to it that no child, or Aged parent should want in any for, by assisting them, the Order would be P 0 , ing a fitting tribute to their departed breth.ore; In conclusion, the speaker hoped that x`"r, Grand Watson would live many years to C°t, tinue the good work lie had so much at hear (Applause.) Aer P.D.G.M. Spann, Prov. C.S. Webster, I.°-"'ci Secretary Taylor, P.P.G.3l.'s Holbrook Bradshaw, P.G. Clare, the President of _ter, Widows and Orphans Fund, and P.O. Wall' to the secretary, also added their testimouY P.G. Watson's services.., iva In the course of a suitable reply, P.G. " 50 gave some interesting reminiscences of his les years' membership, and of the early str,u+gigi of the Widows and Orphans Fund. His the fon lie said, was one of the founders of, p 3 to and at the end of the first year they boa '" er their credit. The fund now stood with Olt's,. 042,1,000 invested. He heartily thanked o(1. members for their token of confidence and Oho' will, and said he had only done his duty t° fellow men. (Applausl.) uo g been After the National tiithem had ' collection was made for the South Lax • g. ie boa Tobacco Fund, the amount realisea £1 2s.</p>
