      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>PAYING THE "RING MONEY."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>llow Our Fighters' Wives Draw Their Allowances. A CANARD "SCOTCHED."</head>
        <p>llow Our Fighters' Wives Draw Their Allowances. A CANARD "SCOTCHED."</p>
        <p>or,here may"EXAMINER" SPECIAL. be people who, passing the Parr 4'l on Monday mornings or afternoons, feel rious as to the presence of the two burly, avereoated custodians of law and order who stitand at the portals, and more curious still at a h.le sight of the little knots, or—shall we say? tehes, ef women who. at brief intervals, !Inerge from the doorway, nod a "Good-day" 41 the officers, and vanish from sight down the int set on the opposite side of the road. The • rY—if mystery it should be to anyone—is t0"43" solved. The women are the widowed k°thers and the wives of our gallant fighting iosh-4-those Tommy has left behind him, secure ,6the knowledge that whilst he is doing his blci(l'2 to uphold the honour and prestige of the .; hag on foreign soil, a grateful country is geo4(ng to it that "me" and the " missus" are h -"lig a little bit of silver to go on with. May- : the " little bit" is rather smaller than the A:4l)n khaki could wish, but they have the leo:tection of knowing that it is regular—so they live. tirnii" Monday an Examiner" representative ill2Ped in at the Parr Hall and had a brief with one or two of these plucky wives of 110.8PlueltY men, and afterwards went on to the ▪ " Office to see for himself how this business servp.aYing the allowances to the dependents of Re leo men is carried on. He was impelled to ht and see for himself by reason of the fact that ei„..4 recent meeting of -the Trades and Labour a dolorouo picture of weary, waiting l4fss; of fainting mothers, of callous. nay, (141'41. red tapeism, had been drawn. If eon- th,:hns Were as they were said to be, then it was 1470 e the searchlight of publicity was thrown about n them, and an alteration was brought nformal " Conversazione." th, Man dropped into the Parr Hall casually; had been no dress rehearsal against his wo-jng—lie saw things just as they have been far,: after week. Instead of the weary, half- hinisdting, pale and underfed and scantily clad of Women that he had been led to half- el• ect, he found a large room full ofwomeno! s,ice6ges and sizes, chattering away vivaciously, atisb-hging tit-bits from the " boy's" or the and's latest missive from the front, corn- ing notes as to babies, cracking jokes to the terchnPaniment of judiciously subdued laugh- alhr soMehow the presence of a policeman has a toning-do,wn effect on femuune en ti• llY— and generally conducting themselves lhe aproved lines of a mother's meeting. ee..ra Were no fainting females, nor were any of undue delay on the part of the sO Officials; indeed, there were those who, CT,• :r from desiring to get away from the greo-,versazione," stood aside whilst others, with lit%rer domestic claims upon their time, went he,,eo Each mother or wife was handed a num- inlieb (Use prior to entering the hall. The Ptit er on the disc denoted her order of pri- it Z. in the matter of receiving payment, and Wftitts no infrequent thing to hear one of the 11115 :? Wives say to another something after, thse ``Look here, Mrs. —, I've got No. J htt„llbatch and you've got No. 32. • You've got elteeV(ill's dinner to get ready. You take my In ban,. d I'll have yours !" "Aitl 'hn,a'ellee, according to their numbers, the o7_,wornen took their leave—with cheery ," "See you next weeks." and the heioo,and wended their way to the Post Office, ke5,7„ last informed by a smart little telegraph arr. -hger that their turn to " draw " had ived. Seoni QC' 1-flying-out Arrangements at :}9•P .O. --41• 01,,--‘ng the Post Office the women were ki&amp;eie'lllll3lY directed into the spacious TOM, 4,3 i 3'. set apart for the transaction of their aess 'h-tot 4 ,-,17 to a few weeks ago the room was - .‘iyhsioll,othe telegraph boys; it was the room '"gtl "leY romped and wrestled—when the Me °facial told off to keep a semblance of ainon s e the., g them wasn't on the ce ne—what Waited the summons to deliver the unigoa little buff envelopes containing c the ttobi. words from the wonderful wires. orn- •Y Warmed and provided with plenty of</p>
        <p>seats, the room is admirably adapted to its temporary purpose. At one end is a long table, and behind it sit the clerks whose business it is to dip their hands into the tempting array of gold and silver coins with which the drawers are laden and single out therefrom the sums specified on the "ring papers" of the married women and the maintenance warrants of the mothers whose sons have made them allocations of their pay. The business of paying-out is con- ducted with expedition and courtesy—such courtesy as is due to the dependents of the heroes-all who are fighting so minfully the cause of us stay-at-home admirers. Maybe the busy clerk finds time for a pleasantry that sends a ripple of laughter among the waiting women, joke begetting joke, and the wives and mothers, the precious coins gripped tightly in their closed hands, pass out into the street once more with smile-Wreathed faces. One looked in vain for that harshness, that want of tact and com- mon kindliness that one had heard of—from those who, obviously, don't know! A Cbnclusive Disclaimer.</p>
        <p>It was alleged at the Trades Council meet- ing that women had been told with brutal bluntness, when they had presented their "ring. papers," "Can't pay that; he's dead !" where- upon, not unnaturally, if it were true, wives and mothers had fainted or had broken down in pitiful fashion. But, as Mark Twain once said, when reading obituary notices concerning him- self in the American Press, "The story is grossly exaggerated !" As a matter of fact, the woman unfortunate enough to lose her husband or her son-support would be made acquainted with the fact before the Post Office officials; indeed the only intimation that they get is that Private So-and-so's pay-book is re-called. For all they know the book may be required by the Army Pay Office for the adjustment of some clerical errors—several such cases have occurred, and it has been necessary to .tell the wife or mother that further payment could not be made until the book is sent back from headquarters. leeVerything is all right when the book comes back, not only is the current week's pay handed over, but 'all arrears are settled up. "We could not have been guilty of uttering that heartless He's dead!' phrase," one of the officials assured our representative, "for the simple reason that the woman would be in- formed of that sad fact from the War Office; we never get the intimation. That settles the story about women fainting when we have told them that their husbands or sons have been killed. We don't do the telling; it's not our de- partment. The War Office see to that! On the first day we started paying out a woman did faint, but that was not due to any information we conveyed, but to the exceptional rush and the pressure of other women round about her. We hadn't got the Parr Hall as a waiting-room then."</p>
        <p>Our informant added that there were eases in which the husband was reported as having been killed, but the wives still went on drawing the Army separation allowance, because the P.O. had had no authority from headquarters to cease paying it. A case in point was that of a poor womaa who had that day drawn her fourth week's pay since she lost her bread- winner. £6OO A Week Pahl Out.</p>
        <p>"It is absolutely untrue to say that we have occasioned a shock to any wife or mother," reiterated the official, by way of conclusion, ".because we do not know when a man is killed. All we tlo know is that the pay-book is re-called, and, so far as we are aware, the re-calling of it may be simply for the purpose of putting it right clerically !" Not all of the women come for their money on the Monday; some attend the next day, when the routine is precisely the same, and every effort is made to reduce the discomforts of the some- times necessarily long waits to a minimum. (Mem. : If somebody, or bodies, want to do the fighting man's loved ones a good turn, what about arranging for a cup of tea and a biscuit or two; it would be vastly appreciated!) The hours during which the Post Office is open for the purpose of payments to soldiers' dependents are from 8 to 8, and the busiest time is between 9.30 and 11.30. Altogether between 800 and 900 separation allowances are paid out, the total involved being over ,k600.'</p>
        <p>Tempting. Property insured in London is valued at :e1,320,000,000, according to an announcement made by Lord Peel last week. One can almost hear the Kaiser smacking his lips.—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>THE E)CAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Great Civic and Patriotic Demonstration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CANON WILLIS AND OUR JUST WAR.</head>
        <p>CANON WILLIS AND OUR JUST WAR.</p>
        <p>• As befitted the times, there was an air of the martial allied to the civio about the Mayoral churchgoing on Sunday. The presence in the procession of the serried ranks of those who have offered themselves for home service with the local Preparatory Training Corps supplied the main suggestion, and the music of the bands —the soul-inspiring national airs of the allied combatants, who, side by side, are fighting the common enemy, blending with the strains of the popular songs which Tommy Atkins has adopted-as his secular and spirit-raising war chants, and being punctuated, ever and anon, by the piercing shrill of bugle• calls and the ear-assailing throbs of the kettle-drum— heightened it. All the way from the Town Hall—where the procession was marshalled—to the distant Par- ish Church—huge crowds of people thronged the pavements, whilst yet more followed in the wake of the civic cavalcade.</p>
        <p>Constantinople, and at the moment, there was special significance in the fact.</p>
        <p>A CHRISTIANISED CONSTANTINOPLE—? They would doubtless recall—those of them who had dipped into the pages of history—that in the third century—three hundred years after Christ—the seat of the then greatest Empire was removed from Rome to the place that was now known as Constantinople, by Constantine the Great. A thousand years later—in the year 1453 to be precise—Constantinople Was taken by the Turks, and the Mohammedans settled there, with the result that for 460 years that great city, and the country of which it formed the capital, had been the seat of Molammedan worship. Now, as they knew, the Turks had joined arms with Germany, and by so doing, had, according to the Prime Min- ister, sealed their own doom, with the result that there was good cause for hoping and be- lieving that, when the present terrible war came to an end, Constantinople would again become the seat of a Christian Empire. Yes, verily the words of the text were the cry of the Church, and let them pray to God that the Anglican Communion, the Eastern Commitnion, and the great Roman Communion might again become one. Coming to the third aspect of the text, let them consider the German claim—the German: cry! Time was when Germany was Christian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>PROCESSION.</head>
        <p>THE PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>A touch of sunshine would have added to the amenities, but, perhaps, after all, the sombre skies were more in accord with the feelings of the assembled processionists as the news went round that Britain's grand old warrior, Lord Roberts, had passed away. Crosfield's Band, under the conductorship of Mr. J. C. Taylor, heralded the approach of the long queue as it proceeded from the Park along Sankey-street, Buttermarket-street, and Church-street. Imme- diately behind came the members of the War- rington and District Preparatory Training Corps, commanded by Sergt.-Major Sullivan. They marched as men who had been accustomed to military discipline for years, instead of only a matter of a few brief weeks. The local Cadet Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade contri- buted about 80 members to the procession, and they were in charge of Major Brooks, Capt. Bunch, Lieut. Morris, and Acting-Sergt.-Major S. Essenhigh. Their bugle band, like that of the Boy Scouts, who followed, added to the volume of martial strains which saluted the ear as the procession wended its way along the streets. The following troops of local Scouts were represented: Ist Warrington (in charge of Assistant-Scoutmaster Cartwright); 2nd War- rington (Scoutmaster Young)r and Ist Grappen- hall (Scoutmaster T. A. Edwards). Mr. J. B. Starkey (chairman of the local Boy Scouts' Association) walked with the troops. A detach- ment of ambulance men were in charge of Supt. S. Baldry. The Borough Fire Brigade (under the com- mand of Supt. Turner) preceded a detachment of the local police force, who were in charge of Mr. Martin Nicholls (the chief constable). The mace and sword bearers, in their quaint gowns, were followed by the Mayor, who was accom- Panied by the Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway), and on returning from churcli also by the Rev. Canon Willis (his chaplain). The Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph) and Mr. A. H. Cros- field followed, and then came members of the various local bodies.</p>
        <p>The Town Council was represented by Alder- men Bennett, Tinnion and James Evans, and Councillors Barton, Chantler, Chrimes, Craik, Cunnington, Dickenson, Gough, Halliwell, Haw- thorn, Henshall, jAshford, Locker, Osborne, Shaw, Skelland, and Wilson. Others figuring in the procession were:— Corporation ocials Messrs. T. H. Spinks (assistant town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), A. M. Irer (borough surveyor), F. G. Ruddock (analyst), W. T. Flood (chief inspector of nuisances), E. Place (markets inspector), R. Whitfield (head gardener), W. S. Haddock (gas works manager), J. Gray (water engineer), and W. S. Howard (cashier of the gas department). Magistrates: Messrs. J. Bailey, W. Furness, A. J. Jolley, T. Locker and G. Pilling. Education Committee: Messrs. G. Walker, J. Moore Murray (director of education), and G. Pendlebnry (school of art). Warrington Infirmary: Messrs. J. H. Smeth- urst (chairman of the Board of Management), H. Collin, W. Daintith, T. C. Wadsworth, and J. H. J. Hampson (secretary), together, with a number of wounded soldiers who are under treatment in the institution. P.S.A. Societies: Messrs. W. Clegg and B. Farrar. Insurance Committee: Messrs. S. Brockle- hurst. W. Bibby, P. Delaney, F. Dickens, J. Elwell, J. Seed, and A. Tilling (clerk). The Friendly Societies Walked in the follow- ing order, in charge of Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, the chief marshal: R.A.O. Buffaloes (accompanied by their band under the conductorship of Mr. R. Smith), Hearts of Oak, National Independ- ent Order of Oddfellows, Irish National Fores- ters and Hibernians, Loyal Ancient Order of Shepherds, Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), Rational Association, An- cient Order of Druids, Free Gardeners, Catho- lic Grand Friendly United Order of. Oddfellows. Society, Ancient Order of Fores- ters,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>IN THE CHURCH. "If God Be With Us, Who Can Be</head>
        <p>IN THE CHURCH. "If God Be With Us, Who Can Be</p>
        <p>Against Us?"</p>
        <p>Inside the church every available seat was occupied, and not a few of the worshippers found no seats .at all. The majority of the pews, both on the floor and in the commodious galleries, were reserved for those who had marched with the Mayor's entourage. His Worship, attended by his deputy (Councillor Joseph), the Assistant Town Clerk (Mr. Halls- way), and Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, was accom- modated in a front pew, the mace and sword, which symbolise the civic state of Warrington, being borne before him up the aisle and placed in specially prepared brackets by his side..</p>
        <p>The service was of a most impressive char- acter. As the processionists filed into their seats the organist (Mr. Darby) played the National Anthems of the Allies. The anthem, "Judge me, 0 Lord "—a specimen of Mozart in his most inspired vein—was beautifully ren- dered, both as to the delightful quartet-prelude and the grand massibg of the voices which leads to the glorious finale. The hymns selected were "0 God, our help in ages past," and "Stand up !—stand up for Jesus," the former being sung to a new tune, which lacks the rol- ling sonority of the older and more popular setting. At the close, after an offertory had been taken on behalf of the local Belgian Relief Fund, the congregation joined in singing the National Anthem, after which the proces- sion was re-formed and marched back to the Town Hall, where once again "God save the King" was sung before the crowd melted away.</p>
        <p>EAST IS EAST AND WEST IS WEST-WILL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EVER THE TWAIN MEET?</head>
        <p>EVER THE TWAIN MEET?</p>
        <p>The sermon in the Parish Church was preached by the Mayor's Chaplain ' (the Rev. Canon Willis). The Canon based his discourse on 'lf God be with us, who can be against us?" (Romans viii. 31). The little word " if" in the text, he said, was not hypothetical; it did not suggest a supposition: it stated a fact. The words could be taken in three senses—firstly as a cry of the soul, secondly as a cry of the Church, and thirdly as a cry. of faith. As applied to the individual it was the cry of con- fidence; of triumphant confidence. When St. Paul wrote the words he was over sixty years of age and had been a Christian for thirty years, so he spoke from experience. He knew what it was to have God on his side. For a thousand years after the Ascension of its Founder the Church of Christ was one, and then—nearly a thousand years ago—that great schism took place between the East and the West—a schism which had caused a breach that had never been healed. Five hundred years later came further separation—a separa- tion which they spoke of as the Reformation. Later still, came other sad separations—the falling away of so many great religious bodies, , as, for instance, the Baptists and the Quakers in the seventeenth century, and the Wesleyans and the Unitarians in the eighteenth. But he thought he saw signs of a healing of the breach. East and West' were approaching each other again. Russia was coming up from the East; great Russia, whose huge army was almost en- tirely composed of members of the Orthodox Eastern Church. Russia thought much of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>GERMANY'S FALL FROM GRACE.</head>
        <p>GERMANY'S FALL FROM GRACE.</p>
        <p>In the churches of this country they still had reminders of the fact in some of the most beau- tiful of their hymns, as for instance "Jesus lives! No longer now can the powers of death appal us!" "We plough the fields and scatter," "Who are these, like stars appearing?" &amp;c. The German people were Christian people, but the German professors—Nietzsche and others— had thrown over Christianity; thrown over, even, their belief in God; whilst the German Emperor appeared not, only only to have thrown God over, but also to. have taken Him under his wing. Britain had her faults, but, thank God, she also had her virtues. For one thing, she had her methods of government, which were so different from those of Germany. There was no Iron-Heel policy in Britain, but, in- stead, toleration and love, and the belief that everyone should do that he thought well, pro- vided he did right. The aim and object of this .country was to make all happy—not to .make them slaves, but to make them free men— free and happy. And in the present war the cause of this country was a just one—never was a war fought for a more just cause! As a' nation, as a Church, and as individuals they did feel that they had God and the Saviour with them:- Concluding, the preacher said "Our duty is to fight for our country. Thank God! things seem to be going well for us, but the real struggle, I bblieve, is yet to come. 'Twill come when we have driven the Germans back to their Rhine. Then shall we be face to face with the great German fortresses, and we shall need 'more men and still more men,' as Lord Kitchener has said. It is for every man to think 'ls it my duty to go, for the sake of my country, for the sake of my King?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>THOUSANDS SING THE NATIONAL</head>
        <p>THOUSANDS SING THE NATIONAL</p>
        <p>ANTHEM.</p>
        <p>At the close of the service the procession was re-formed, and, through crowded streets, wended its way back to the Town Hall, where the different units were drawn up in their appointed places on the lawn. The Mayor and other members of the Corporation took up their posi- tion on top of the steps, and at a given signal the massed bands played the National Anthem, the processionists and the large crowd of lookers-on uncovering and joining lustily in singing the grand old hymn. When the last notes of the final appeal had died away, the crowd quietly dispersed, and the aldermen and councillors and other civic dignitaries repaired to the Council Chamber, where, at the invita- tion of his Worship, they partook of refresh- ment, and where the toast of the Mayor and Mayoress was honoured, on the proposition of Alderman BENNETT, who said that in many respects that day had been unique. There had been a splendid processihn, a full church, a beautiful service, and then the final grand assembly on the lawn in front of the Town Hall, a sight which would remain in their memories for a long time. They thanked the Mayor for his public-spirit and the originality which had characterised that Mayor's Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>HIS WORSHIP AND LORD ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP AND LORD ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>The toast having been honoured, The MAYOR, in reply, thanked all those who had attended, adding: "Will you also allow me to thank you for the very kind manner in which you have toasted the Mayoress and my- self? I am sorry that there is one thing that has marred the proceedings to-day, and that is the death of that venerable and esteemed soldier, Earl Roberts: It was unfortunate that, at his time of life, he should have taken that journey to France, in order to encourage fur- ther the Indian Troops, who have fought so gloriously and so well for this country. (Hear, hear.) He went there to give them that cheer which would do them good." His Worship then' went on to refer to innovations in that day's procession; viz., the engagement of two bands, and also the new feature in shape of the massing of the Friendly Society members and th 4 Pre- paratory Training Corps on the I,awn in front of the Town Hall, remarking that the innova- tion was certainly an unusual one; but, then, the times were unusual and they must do un- usual things.</p>
        <p>--- - Mayor's Singleness of Purpose. He might add that anything he did in tbe direction of that day's procession, or in any other connection, he did with a sense of re- sponsibility, and with the thought and hope that he was pleasing everybody and doing his duty as a Mayor should do. It was that feeling, and that feeling only, which prompted him to do what he had done in connection with that day's proceedings, and Ile was glad that the innovatibn—the brief ceremony on the lawn— had met with their approbation. (Applause.) The company then dispersed, and the Mayor's Sunday celebrations were at an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MAYOR AT WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AT WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN PEACOCK'S PRAISE FOR FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>There was a most inspiring meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday afternoon, when the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) presided over a large attendance, including members of the various local_ Friendly Societies. The Rev. T. T. James, president of the society, delivered an address, and Miss Elsie Wilkinson earned hearty applause by her fine treatment of the solos " Out of the deep" and "Land of hope and glory," Mr. W. 11. Payton playing the organ accompaniments.</p>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP'S AIM AND OBJECT.</p>
        <p>Travis read the lesson, al;;;hich</p>
        <p>His Worship, who was received with applause. said that he was grateful and gratified to find in the congregation that afternoon so many of his fellow Friendly Society members, especially in view of the bad state of the weather. After their splendid rally of the morning, he had ha,rdly expected so large an attendance. They were all aware that he was a Friendly Society man, and he was trying to fill the posi- tion of Mayor to this ancient borough in a way which would give satisfaction to 'those, of all classes and creeds.</p>
        <p>No Friendly Societies in Germany. Friendly Societies had taught him to look at things in a broad manner, and he hoped that his conclusions had generally been right. In the old days, men belonging to such societies used to be put into prison, because they were found meeting in secret; yet now, the Govern- ment welcomed them, for they made men better in all respects. In Germany there were no Friendly Societies. If the people of that coun- try had been allowed to extend their brotherly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</head>
        <p>NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
        <p>feelings towards mankind more, there would have been no war.</p>
        <p>Since the introduction of the Insurance Act, he regretted to say that commercialism had entered into many of their Friendly Societies; however, they were doing all they could to pre- vent it spreading. They had decided that, whilst many of their brethren were away fight- ing, they, would keep them in benefit, so that when they returned they could go on with their membership as if nothing had happened. (Ap- plause.) ' Thanked the Societies for "Practically Everything." He thanked Friendly Societies for practically everything he had, because when he was quite a boy—he did not mind confessing it—he was very, very poor indeed. joining the Friendly Societies when he was 16, brought him some confidence to follow out their motto of asking for promotion when he deserved it. So that now, although State Insurance was with them, he urged men to join the Friendly Societies' voluntary section; they would maintain that " brotherly " feeling, besi&amp;s getting valuable assistance in times of need. Once again he thanked the Friendly Society membeia, without whose help he would not have been so success- ful during the troublous times of his Mayor- alty, and he advised all who could to join. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>P.S.A. MEMBERS AT THE FRONT</head>
        <p>P.S.A. MEMBERS AT THE FRONT</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, in giving the address, alluded to the opportunity they had of showing their sympathy with those who suffered at home as a result of the war, by contributing to the Prince of Wales' Fund. He was very.glad to say that a good many members of that society had gone to the front, and pointed out that the assistance of their dependents was not a charity but a duty. (Hear, hear.) They welcomed their Chairman very heartily; he had presided over many meetings, after which his present task was mere child's play. At least, he would not get into trouble that afternoon. (Laughter.) They wished him all success in his second year of office; his task was a great and difficult one— not so much on account of the multitudinous engagements involved as of the responsibility and anxiety. " Charms " That Grow Off One.</p>
        <p>It was wonderful how a town endeared itself to one who lived in it. Before he knew Lanca- shire as well as he did now, he was passing through it, on one occasion in a train, and pres- ently they came—he hoped there was nobody present who lived there=to one of the dirtiest and most ramshackle places he ever saw. And then a man in the carriage, who was returning from Blackpool, jumped up and exclaimed with great fervour: "Good old Wigan!" (Laugh- ter.) Of course, they who lived in Warrington could discuss its shortcomings amongst them- selves, but on no account must they admit them to anybody else. Warrington, A Garden City? - - - -</p>
        <p>When his friends came to stay with him he took them from the station up Wilson-Patten- street and Bold-street, and through the Queen's Gardens; and he took them,back past the Town Hall and through the Bank Park. Then he said: " You see, we have quite a garden city !" (Laughter.) But the true value of a town was measured by the lives it contained; and, believ- ing this, they would, on that Municipal Sun- day, go forward and try to make the town better and better. Friendly Societies had done a great deal to inculcate a moral character and ideal. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIOR'S HANDSHAKE FOR</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIOR'S HANDSHAKE FOR</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Farrar, secretary, also welcomed their Chairman, and referred to his own experiences of the valuable work of the Friendly Societies.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. T. Yeates, of Orford, a member of the P.S.A. Society, who had been invalided home from the Aisne, and .was to rejoin his regiment on Thursday, then entered the pulpit and shook hands with the Mayor, Mr. James and the Secretary, amidst applause. All wished him God-speed, and he made a brief, farewell speech.</p>
        <p>The collection realised ..tt, making a total two weeks' contribution to the local war funds of £7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FOR WARRIORS' WIVES.</head>
        <p>FOR WARRIORS' WIVES.</p>
        <p>Society of Friends Provide a Cosy</p>
        <p>Club-Room.</p>
        <p>The local members of the Society of Friends have decided to place a comfortable room in their Meeting House, in Buttermarket-street, at the disposal of the wives of the soldiers and sailors who are away fighting their country's battles. Thither the womenfolk may repair for a few quiet hours each day, taking their sewing with them, if they have any to do, or whiling away the time with a chat or a read, for it is hoped that sympathisers with the move- ment will see to it that the tables are liberally stocked with suitable papers and magazines. The room will be opened daily between the hours of three and nine, and a cordial welcome will be extended to the wife of any " Tack " or "Tommy" who cares to avail herself of the facilities offered.</p>
        <p>FORMAL OPENING BY THE MAYORESS. This informal club-room-,--to use a description applied to it by a lady speaker—was declared open by the Mayoress (Mrs. Peter Peacock) on Wednesday afternoon, in the Presence of a goodly company, which included a number of soldiers' and sailor? wives.</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes presided over the brief opening ceremony, and, in the course of a short speech, said that the members of the congregation which met in those ftemises had been pondering upon how they could best render assistance to the wives of the men who were engaged in the gigantic struggle which was in progress on the Continent, and, as a result, they had decided to lend that room. There the soldiers' and sailors' wives could meet and spend pleasant afternoons and early evenings, it being assumed that their household duties could be performed in the mornings and later in the evenings. It was to be hoped that those present would show their appreciation by visit- ing the room as often as possible, and also by bringing along their women friends who were situated similarly to themselves. She asked the Mayoress, who, she said, was always good- natured and willing to give a helping hand to deserving objects, to declare the club open. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>COUNTER-ATTRACTION TO UNDESIR-</head>
        <p>A COUNTER-ATTRACTION TO UNDESIR-</p>
        <p>ABLE RESORTS.</p>
        <p>The Mayoress, who was greeted with ap- plause, said that amongst the most pleasant of the many duties a mayoress in an important town had to perform was the opening of a social club like the one they were launching that afternoon. The provision of such a comfort- able room for the wives of the brave soldiers and sailors, a room wherein they could encour- age one another and attend to their babies and their sewing during the course of a friendly chat, was a very happy idea, and one which must coffimend itself to all good-thinking people. "We all know too well," continued the Mayoress, "how people talk about where our women spend their time, and it often occurs to me that those people, instead of talking and in many cases scandalising the women, could be better employed by doing fts you have done this afternoon, and by offering inducements which will lead to profit and enjoyment. I have much pleasure in declaring this women's club open." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE UNDERLYING IDEA. Miss Capper (of Manchester) said the idea underlying the establishment of those clubs was to help, to some extent, to relieve the anxiety which the wives of the soldiers and sailors must feel. A number of similar clubs had been opened in Manchester, and it was to be hoped that similar institutions would spring up all over the country. Mrs. Ransome said that the working arrange- ments were almost complete, and, if that ven- ture was successful, there was every possibility of inaugurating a similar one at the headquar- ters of the Bewsey Adult School. (Applause.) Newspapers and magazines were needed, but thol, it was expected, would be readily forth- coming. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>It Wasn't Al,:</p>
        <p>"Johnny," said his mother, severely, "some one has taken a piece of ginger cake out of the pantry." Johnny blushed guiltily. "Oh, Johnny !" she exclaimed, "I didn't think it was in you."</p>
        <p>"'lt ain't all," replied Johnny; "part of it's in Elsie."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%th9M%%l</head>
        <p>%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%th9M%%l</p>
        <p>If Warrington Backs Could Speak they would thank Mrs. Dooney, who has been kept well 6 years by Doan's Pills.</p>
        <p>On March 16th, 1907, Mrs. K. Dooney, of 54, Forster-street, Orford-lane, War- rington, said:—" I suffered for a long time with my kidneys. The pains in my back were most acute, and at times I was scarcely able to get up and down stairs. If I stooped they became much worse, and I found it hard to straighten myself.</p>
        <p>"I had bad headaches, and often came over so dizzy that I was afraid of fall- . The water was cloudy, and caused me great pain when relieving the</p>
        <p>ing. bladder:</p>
        <p>i "At last I made up my mind to try Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. They soon eased the pains and remedied the water troubles. I felt a different woman after taking Doan's Pills, and picked up wonderfully. I have told many of my friends what these pills did for me."</p>
        <p>On November 21st, 1913—ovEn six YEARS LATER—Mrs. Dooney said :—" I am pleased to say that my cure has been a lasting one, and I still keep well. I have recommended Doan's Pills to dozens of people." ' Thank You</p>
        <p>Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are successful</p>
        <p>because their curative properties are directed</p>
        <p>entirely to the kidneys and urinary system. They have a quick, soothing, cleansing and tonic action on the kidneys. They assist the urinary</p>
        <p>system just as a purgative assists the bowels.</p>
        <p>They discharge accumulated water and waste products left in the system by weak or diseased</p>
        <p>kidneys. They promote a natural activity of the kidneys and bladder, and so purify the blood and prevent the accumulation of the</p>
        <p>urino-:_s poisons that cause lumbago, rheuma- tism, dropsy, gravel, renal stone, gout, sciatica, inflammation of the bladder and irregularities in the flow of urine and disorders of the blood.</p>
