        <p>MAYOR'S SHARE IN THE NEGOTIATIONS.</p>
        <p>A MUCH APPRECIATED ACQUISITION._</p>
        <p>The Travelling Kaiser. It is semi-officially announced that the Kaiser's headquarters are now in France. His hindquarters were recently seen in Russia.— " Punch."</p>
        <p>" Mouchers' " Hard Task. "They had been quarrelling about who was the biggest moucher, and had gone there to fight it out," said Inspector Stuart, at the local Police Court on Tuesday morning, when giving evidence against Thomas Monks, a labourer, of 21, Ellesmere-street, and Robert Heaton, a labourer, of 22, Manley-street, who were charged with fighting in Wharf-street the pre- vious day. The Chief Constable intiniated that the men had set themselves a hard Ulla.— The pugilists were each fined 10s., with alter- natives of 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>" • • • - sary for the Council to make a further appli- cation to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>The Mayor—" who," Alr./Hallaway informed the Inspector, "has taken 'great interest in the project and practically carried it through him- self "—said the offer of the land to the Cor- poration, at what was considered a very low price, had given general satisfaction in the town. The acquisition of the land would, so to speak, complete the recreation ground scheme for the St: John's Ward, which was simply one mass of congested property, whilst the people of the Fairfield and Howley Wards could get to the ground very easily. Altogether, War- rington was not favourably situated so far as recreation grounds were concerned, and it had been long felt that something should be done in the matter; indeed, for years the Council had been agitated to do something to increase the play-accommodation for the children of the town, and recreation grounds generally. The proposed addition to Victoria Park was easily accessible from all parts. Alderman Bennett said that the acquisition of Victoria Park was regarded as one of the greatest boons ever conferred on the public, and it had been enormously appreciated, as this contemplated addition would also be. There was only one feeling in the town with regard to the acquisition and that was a feeling of unanimous approval.</p>
        <p>i</p>
        <p>Councillor Archer said he lived only a stone's throw from the Victoria Park. Ratepayers of the ward which he represented had often ex- pressed to him their thankfulness that such. a health resort had been provided. One Sunday evening last summer a matter of no fewer than 4,000 people passed his door on their way to take advantage of the particular spot with which that inquiry was concerned. The inquiry was then closed.</p>
