      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S LUNGS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S LUNGS.</p>
        <p>Projected Extension of Victoria Park.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LOAN OF £4,000 SOUGHT.</head>
        <p>LOAN OF £4,000 SOUGHT.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Council have asked the Local Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of £4,192 for the purpose of pur- chasing land for the extension of Victoria Park, and on Wednesday the Board sent down one of their Inspectors, Mr. Edgar Dudley, F. 5.1., to inspect the site and go into the de- tails of the application. The Inspector held his inquiry at the Town Hall, when the facts concerning the land which it is proposed to acquire, and what the Council purpose doing with it, were laid before him by the Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway) and the Borough Surveyor (Mr. A. M. Ker). There were also present at the inquiry the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Alderman ‘43ennett, Councillors Skella.nd and Archer, and Messrs. Piercey (assistant borough surveyor), and Jas. Houghton (chief clerk in the Borough Treas- urer's Department).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>THIRTY-SEVEN ACRES OF RECREATION GROUND.</head>
        <p>THIRTY-SEVEN ACRES OF RECREATION GROUND.</p>
        <p>At the outset the Deputy Town Clerk ex- plained that the application was for the pur- pose of getting sanction to a loan for purchasing 37 acres, 3 roods and 15 perches of land, to be used as a recreation ground. Victoria Park, to which the area specified was to form an exten- sion, was purchased by the Corporation under the powers of the Warrington Corporation Act, 1899. The area then acquired was 53 acres, and the price of £lO,OOO included an old manor house, which was still existing. The price worked out at about £2OO per acre, and the land thus acquired—the old Warps Estate—had been used as a public park. The manor house was partly used as a caretaker's residence, but several of the rooms were disused. The house was one of those old buildings that required a lot of money spending on them, if full benefit was to be got out of them, and it had been I suggested that it might be used as an art gal- I lery or something of the sort. Victoria Park, as one looking at its situation would readily appreciate, had been of great advantage as a breathing space, particu- larly to the people residing on the eastern side of the borough. The piece of land which it was now proposed to acquire abutted immediately on to the Park; in fact, a small portion of it—on the south side—jutted right into land already belonging to the town. Warrington was a large manufacturing town, and its manufactures, he was happy to say, were increasing every year, with a consequent increase of population. Under such circumstances sites like the one in ques- tion—seeing that it had a frontage on to a waterway which was navigable for barge traffic —were most suitable for works' purposes.</p>
        <p>An Opportunity That May Not Be Repeated. Indeed, there was every likelihood of the whole of the area in the neighbourhood of the land, which it was proposed to acquire, being developed for business purposes within a reason- able period of time. It would be in special demand for works, more particularly as there was a suggestion—which would probably materialise—that the Corporation should put a bridge over the river in this neighbourhood, an enterprise which would open up the whole of the neighbourhood. The Park was situated on the main road, and a footbridge gave access to the other side of the town. The Corporation felt, that in order to round off their boundaries and to preserve for the town—at a time when land was comparatively cheap—a much-needed open space, they ought to acquire this, addition to Victoria Park. If the land which it was now proposed to acquire was opened out for com- mercial purposes and works were put up cheek by jowl with the present recreation ground, it would not be conducive to The best interests of the amenities of the neighbourhood. At present there were in Warrington 93 acres of recreation grounds, but they were scattered all over the town, and some of them Were very small plots indeed. The Inspector: Only three of them are of any size.</p>
        <p>No Keep-off-the-Grass Notices. The Deputy Town Clerk said this was the only piece of land the Council had on which the children of the town and the young men could go and play unrestricted and unfettered by any .regulations. In connection with other of the open spaces, there were so many regula- tions—" Beep off the grass," &amp;c.—that children did not really feel "at home." This was a place where they could go and roll about and do just as they liked; that was why the Corpora- tion thought it was absolutely necessary that they should seize the golden opportunity and get the land at the extremely cheap rate at which it was, at present, procurable, viz., about £llO per acre. The land was freehold, but the mine and mineral rights were reserved. There was also a restriction as-to the buildings to be put on it; it being stipulated that the land shall be kept as a park, and that no manufac- turing buildings shall be put thereon, but Only such buildings as are compatible with thkplace being used as a public park.</p>
        <p>For the information of the Inspector, the Surveyor went into'the detaili of the plans of the proposed acquisition, explaining that it was intended to use part of the new ground as play- ing fields and part as an ornamental park' though it was not proposed to lay out that latter part at present. As it was at present the land was suitable, without preparation, for plaYing 1 fields. It was the intention, provided it be I found possible, to put in an ornamental lake. but, of course, if it was desired to go on with 1 that scheme, it would, at a later stage, be neces-</p>
      </div>
