      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WAR AND ITS COST</head>
        <p>THE WAR AND ITS COST</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George's Characteristically Shrewd Budget.</p>
        <p>THE GREAT PURPOSE FOR WHICH WE FIGHT.</p>
        <p>" Examiner" Interview with Mr. A. H. Grosfield.</p>
        <p>"First thoughts on the War Budget?" Mr. Crosfield said: " You will scarcely guess. No, they were not concerned with the huge cost of the war, nor with the manner in which we are to be asked to pay for it. My first thoughts, on reading the Budget, were of a very different character; they were concerned with the won- derful circumstance that this great Constitu- tion of ours should have made it possible for the most stupendous Budget of our time—or of any other time, for the matter of that—to be presented by one who was once just a village lad in Wales. That, to my mind, is one of the things that we should not overlook when we talk about this War Budget; it is indeed a great and inspiring fact. Other thoughts crowd along, but all through the remarkable speech of the Chancellor of the Exchequer the great- ness of his personal achievement compels your, admiration—the wonderful combination of lucidit3c and eloquence, the humour and tact, the courage and simplicity of it all. No wonder I he captivated the whole House—he richly earned that tribute of appreciation." " Then you endorse all that the Chancellor of the Exchequer had to say?" " Why, yes; and, to tell you the truth, I have not yet come across anybody who does not— who does not, at all events, endorse the Budget speech in all its essential features. It would be remarkable if it were otherwise; . the patriotic necessity is so palpable to us all. Of course, it is only on grounds of national neces- sity that taxation so onerous for all @lasses of the community could be defended, and that necessity we all recognise, and are ready, each in his way, to meet. The Chancellor of the Exchequer is quite wise in his decision to make known to us in plain language what the war will cost us, and he has chosen a method which will bring that fact home to everybody, in every class of life. But if the burden of extra taxa- tion is heavy—as it undoubtedly is—l for one am optimist enough to believe that it will not, be laid on the shoulders of our people for any great length of time." " Tell me whence your optimism springs?" I "It springs from „some words in Mr. Lloyd I George's speech:</p>
        <p>German Navy will be a thing of the past, and the Austrian Navy, too."</p>
        <p>OUR UNCHALLENGEABLE SUPREMACY. "And the British Navy---?" " For us, of course, whether in good times or in bad, the maintenance of our unchallenged and unchallengeable supremacy .at sea is the very breath of our nostrils; it is a vital necessity for our national and imperial existence; but, after all, it is a question of the relative strength of our own Navy, and of other Navies. Japan is our Ally. We have no Chinese Navy to build against. The aborigines of Africa do not build Navies. If our Allies, now co-operating so splendidly with us, are in the mood to re- trench in the interests of one and all of us; what is to prevent us (as I have already asked) from going back a decade, or even two decades, for our standard of naval expenditure?" "You would then drop from the fifty-odd million standard to a twenty-odd million standard?"</p>
        <p>"Exactly; it would mean a saving of -froth twenty to thirty millions e year." "But may not the cost of the war swallow all that, and a lot more, before it is ended?" "What the cost of the war will be, none of us know. We have Mr. Lloyd George's estimate that if it lasts a year it will cost 450 millions. Will the war last a year? Well, from the moment the Germans failed in their march on Paris many of us have been sanguine enough to expect, or, at any rate, to hope, that the war would be ended within twelve months. Per- sonally, I never believed—and do not now be- lieve—in the possibility of a two or three years' war, such as some people talk about. The pace is far too hot and the sacrifices too terrible for such a prolongation of the war to be possible. If we continue to strive with might and main to put our full strength into the field, I believe we have good reason to hope that the enemy will be crushed and peace signed before the anniversary of the date when war was de- clared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>COMPLETE SETTLEMENT.</head>
        <p>A COMPLETE SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>"He summed up in one admirable sentence the whole purpose before us and our Allies: 'You have got to settle once for all,' he said, the great questions which have been the cause of irritation in Europe for generations.' You see what is behind that. We have to achieve our object of defeating the enemy so decisively that we shall be able to secure an all-round reduction of armaments on a really large scale. 'I shall regard the war as having failed in one of its chief purposes (Mr. Lloyd George said) unless it led to an all-round reduction in the inflated cost of armaments!' It is in that direction that we must look for an easing of the burdens which this war has thrust upon us. That, however, is for the future. At the moment the thoughts of our people. are con- centrated, and rightly concentrated, in a very different direction."</p>
        <p>" You mean that we are straining every nerve to increase and strengthen our lighting capacity?" " Exactly ; and we are doing it; and not for one moment shall we relinquish our efforts till the mighty purpose we have in hand is accom- plished. Somebody said to me to-day: Sup- pose the war should fail to bring about that •blessed result foreshadowed in the Chancellor of the Exchequer's speech!' and my answer is 'Why should it fail?' It is our business to see that it does not fail, and then we shall be in a position to see that never again is the peace of Europe jeopardised, and the wealth of Europe squandered as it has been during the present generation. The frenzied competition in armaments, the increase by leaps and bounds during the last decade or two in the expendi- ture on the Navies of Europe, has led inevitably to this hideous war. Everybody knows who has been responsible for it all. It was Germany that set the pace and forced the pace, and now that the crisis has come we must see to it that the power of Prussian militarism is broken for all time, and that effective measures are taken to safeguard Europe from the arrogant and insolent cruelty of that puffed-up, jack-booted Prussian military caste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OUR NAVAL EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>OUR NAVAL EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>" That achieved, what is to prevent our hark- ing back to the standard of naval expenditure that served us ten or even twenty years ago? It is in that direction that I look hopefully for means by which we shall lift from the backs of the people the burdens which this inevitable ,War Budget now imposes." "I have heard men say that this war means the end of the movement for a reduction of armaments—that the German attempt to demonstrate that might is right Will compel the nations of Europe in the future to arm to the teeth rather than turn their swords into plough- shares?"</p>
        <p>"With such people I do not at all agree. And, mind you, I am not at all what is called a 'Little Navy man.' When I entered the House of Commons in 1906 our naval expendi- ture was, roughly speaking, thirty-one millions. In the succeeding eight and a half years—that is to say, up to the period immediately preced- ing the war—it had actually risen to over fifty millions. During my time in the House of Commons-1906-1909-4 voted for every penny of the increase that was made in those years, and I remember that during the crisis of 1909, when it seemed only too apparent that Germany had been not merely indisposed to respond to the offer we had made in the direction of saner expenditure, but had been trying to steal a march on us-4 remember I had a conversation with Mr. McKenna, who was then at the Admir- alty, a fortnight before he brought in his Esti- mates, and I told him I had absolutely made up my mind to vote every penny he asked for. That is my answer to anybody who may say that my desire to see a reduction in armaments is in any way unpatriotic. Further, if I may be per- mitted another personal reminiscence, I may recall that time after time, in conversation with Lord Haldane, I urged the advisability of more liberal expenditure on our Territorial Force, all, of course, with the view to strength- ening and encouraging it. Those of us who took that line have been well justified in the event for the measures we advised and took to strengthen our position and increase British power on land and sea."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EUROPE AT ITS WORST.</head>
        <p>EUROPE AT ITS WORST.</p>
        <p>"And yet you are hopeful of better and saner days to come in the not distant future?" " I am, indeed ; and for this reason: We have an old Lancashire saying, 'When things get to the worst they mend.' Are not things just about at their worst for Europe now? When this war is over, will it not be about time for mending.? What is to prevent us setting about a mending? Not our own people, surely! Does anyone doubt that after the experience of this war our people will not be anxious to put a stop to fruitless and extravagant expenditure in competition on armaments? And what about France and Russia? We have all heard of the heroism and self-sacrifice with which Russia is fighting in this great struggle, but, I do not think our people, as a whole, q'uite grasp the absolutely stupendous scale on which she is conducting her campaign against Germany and Austria (not to mention Turkey). She has a line of battle extending for a thousand miles, from the Baltic to the Carpathians, aid the problem of troops and transports and supplies is on such a vast scale that it means a gigantic cost to the nation. ' Well, remembering that, Russia will not want to throw away money when the war is over. What of France? After the terrible sacrifices which she, like ourselves, is making in blood, and in treasure, she, too, will not want to engage in a race for extravagant armaments. No; whatever else is in doubt as to the outcome of the• war, it is very certain that we need be under no apprehension that either of our ereat Allies is going to set the pace in naval con- struction."</p>
        <p>"There is still the enemy to be thought of?" "Oh, aa for that. it is .my belief that .the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PAYING FROM SAVINGS.</head>
        <p>PAYING FROM SAVINGS.</p>
        <p>"Now let u-s-as-s-upa-e- that for the interest chargeable on borrowed capital to pay for the war, the improved pay for those who fight our battles, and adequate pensions for those who suffer by them, we have to find something like fifteen millions; against that we shall save—if the hopes raised by the Chancellor of the Exchequer's significant remarks in his Budget speech are realised—something like 25 millions per annum. This is—obviously it can only be —hazarding a rough estimate, but it is well worth bearing in mind at this moment when the burden of new taxation is announced." " You are prepared, then, to relieve posterity of as much as possible of the burden of this war?"</p>
        <p>"Certainly; I should advocate that. If my rough estimate were approximately realised we should be making a very handsome present to posterity—such a present as we have never before been in a position to make. It was im- possible to make such a present at the time of the Napoleonic and Crimean Wars. The world had not advanced far enough then. Opinion was not ripe for it then: It will ripen fast before another single year has passed over our heads. This ripening of opinion is an all- important fact which will differentiate our position at the conclusion of this war from the position occupied by our fathers and our great- grandfathers. History, they say, repeats itself —but not always. America had her tremendous Civil War, and it cost her five years of appalling suffering. It will not be repeated; and in all jiuman probability this European Armageddon cannot be repeated either. Given a compre- hensive, thoroughgoing settlement, with due regard to the just claims of nationality and permanent progress, the overwhelming interests of the nations of Europe, big and small, and ,ollective European common-sense will take such steps as will for ever safeguard the nations against a repetition of</p>
        <p>Blood, sweat and tear-wrung millions</p>
        <p>upon such an appalling scale. That is the glorious legacy which, thanks to the boundless heroism of our men in the field, and the splen- did work of our gallant Allies, we may hope to hand down to posterity. It we do that, it may be asked: Is it reasonable that posterity should be spared a very ample contribution towards prosecuting to a successful issue this greatest of all wars? I cannot doubt that this question was in the mind of the .Chancellor of the Ex- chequer when he reminded us that it is easier to raise taxes in a period of war and to lower them in a period of peace than it would be to raise even lower taxes in a period of peace. But beyond this it is clearly in our present interests to shoulder as much of the burden of the war as possible. Our readiness to tax ourselves for the purpose of paying some part of the bur- den of the war will, as Mr. Lloyd George pointed out, strengthen our credit and give increased confidence that we are not going on by the easiest method of borrowing, but that we are going to carry on the very best traditions of the finances of this country by taxing ourselves for the time being.' Therein is wisdom which may be of advantage to us all, for the maintenance of our credit is the foundation of all our indus- trial prosperity. Mr. Lloyd George, with char- acteristic shrewdness and foresight, has taken care to bring in a War Budget which will not only provide the 'sinews of war,' but which will at the same time nourish and foster, in a time of exceptional stress and difficulty, the trade and commerce on which the well-being of the nation and the Empire depend."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEER HALFPENNY A GLASS MORE. Local Licensed Nictuallers' Decision.</head>
        <p>BEER HALFPENNY A GLASS MORE. Local Licensed Nictuallers' Decision.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association held yesterday afternoon it was decided, in view of the new Budget imports, to increase, as from Monday next, the price of,all beers, and stouts, whether on draught or in bottle. A half-penny extra will be charged on each half-pint, a penny on each pint, and twopence on each quart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>TEA TAX.</head>
        <p>THE TEA TAX.</p>
        <p>We understand that so far no concerted action has been taken by the tea merchants and grocers of the town in the matter of increasing the price of tea, although 'en inquiry yesterday we were informed that at some shops it is pro- posed to charge an additional twopence on every pound:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Retired Builder's Fortune.</head>
        <p>Retired Builder's Fortune.</p>
        <p>...... - --- The will of the late Mr. Joseph Twiss, of 27, Stafford-road, Warrington, a retired builder, whose death occurred on July 2nd, has been proved at ..e8,858 19s. 10d., the net personalty being sworn at £2,772 48. 3d. The executors appointed are Miss Ruth Twiss, of 27, Stafford- road, one of the deceased's daughters, and Mr. John Shepherd, of 12, Stanley-road, Aspull, near Wigan, grocer. The will is dated October 17th, 1912, and under it, and a codicil, the testator leaves to his wife, Mary Ellen Twiss, the use of his dwelling-house and household effects for life; to his daughter, Lydia Shepherd, house and shop, 6, Rylands-street, for life, and then to her husband and children; 2,4, 6,8, Thelwall- lane, and £3OO to his son, Joseph Twiss, for life on certain trusts. The residue of his pro- perty 10 leaves to his wife for life; with .e- -mainder as to 19 parts 2-19tlis to his daughter, Lydia, 2-19ths to his son Joseph, cind 5-19ths each to his daughters, Margaret, Ruth and Rhoda.</p>
        <p>After all, the dreamer may be having a better time than the fellow who is wide awake.</p>
        <p>The man doesn't amount to much who is known by the deeds of his ancestors..</p>
        <p>Our fat graveyards are due rather to green doctors than green Christmasse.s.—Dr. Lawrence Stacey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SITUATION.</head>
        <p>THE SITUATION.</p>
        <p>• There is evidently a lull in the South- West of Belgiiim, where, for the moment, the KAISER'S legions have tired of being slaughtered in droves. On our side, we are showing Berlin the kind of determination with which we have entered the struggle, and there is little doubt that before we have gone much further we shall have given quite another interpretation to the. learned German maxim that Might is Right. The right on our side will have attracted so much might to its aid that. might itself will be arrayed *against the unholy alliance that has been raised up against us. In Parliament, Mr. ASQUITII has asked for those mil- lions of men who will fill the ranks till Germany is exhausted; and the Dominions are coming to our assist- ance with feverish enthusiasm. Yes- terday we heard from Melbourne that Australia has promised unlimited men. Financial and other arrangements are being made to enable Australia to send contingent after contingent, as many as are needed, and more than have been asked for. That is the spirit of the whole Empire. Mr. LLOYD GEORGE has arranged to meet the • enormous cost—about £1,000,000 a day at the present time—by moderately taxing tea, beer and incomes, and arranging a war loan, all of which will be cheer- fully met and provided by a resolved People. The re-assembling of Parlia- ment has been notable for the an- nouncement made by the PREMIER that there have been no negotiations about prolonging the life of this Par- liament on account of the war. A week that has shown our people' at their best has been overcast by the death of LORD ROBERTS, while 011 a visit to the front to cheer on the men—as he said about the most useful 'work to which he could at the time put himself. To the end, Mr. ASQUITH said in a noble tribute to his memory, LORD ROBERTS was not only wishing, but working for the success of our arms, and he died where he would have elected to die, almost in sight of the trenches held by his old comrades in arms with the same dauntless heroism which he hirrOelf so often led and inspired. LORD ROBERTS rests, as is fit, in St. Paul's with NELSON and WELLINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WAR STORIES.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES.</p>
        <p>GERMANS KILLED lIIS BLACK " PAL."</p>
        <p>Warrington Marine Who Is Longing To Exact Revenge.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Gamble, of 336. Knutsford-road, Latchford, are a couple deserving of the honour of their fellow-townspeople, for they have given three of their sons to the service of King and country, the third of them having recently gone to the front. In addition three of their sons- in-law are also serving with the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SANGUINE SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>A SANGUINE SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>In a recent letter, one of their sons, Private Arthur Gamble, 41.M.L.1., who is borne on the books of H.M.S. Cumberland, writes that he is glad, in a sense, to hear that his brother Wil- liam is going to the front, adding "but I don't think he will see many Germans, as the war can't last much longer. . . . lam all right, having got over the lot of fighting that fell to our share. They have now put me guarding prisoners . . . . who are aboard their own steamer, which we captured. You can bet they don't get much sympathy from me, as they shot one of my best pals. Although he was a " blacky " he would have laid down his life for me, and I am waiting an opportunity to avenge his death. We are still in the Cameroon Islands, fighting side by side with the French soldiers, who are a good lot and will stop at I nothing.</p>
        <p>• SAYS THE GERMAN IS A COWARD. "My idea of the German is that he is a coward Out here one can't sit down and think too deeply, for if he did he would never be able to do what is required of him. Where I am we haven't to care for earth, nor hell! We are not only fighting Germans, but the fever as well. There are only fifty of us Marines here, and half of them are in hospital with the fever. .. . Tell Dad to cheer up. I will boozi be witli lkiga</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>TIIRILLING DRAMA AT TIIE COURT. '</p>
        <p>" Anybody's Wife "—a domestic drama con- taining a high percentage of thrills—forms the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre this week, and is being played to good business. The story revolves round the misdeeds of a heartless adventuress, who, by her abominable intriguing, temporarily mars the happiness of a home. There is no lack of incident in the plot, which contains some novel elements, and which is admirably calculated to rivet the attention of all who prefer their theatrical fare served up hot and strong. Mr. Kennedy Allen, who, with Miss Eva Elwes as pen-partner, wrote the play, and who runs the company that is presenting it, has charge of the principal comedy part, that of Silas Slammer, and extracts roars of laughter from his audiences, his drolleries affording a welcome relief amid so much that is pathetic and tragic. A vigorous reading of the part of Bill Blinker—a much-wronged man, who is out for vengeance—is afforded by Mr. Roy Fortescue, and Mr. Fred Ingleby scores heavily as Lord Langley—as undesirable a specimen of theatrical-nobility as ever roused the " Gods" to hisses. The role of the home-wrecking ad- venturess is in the capable hands of Miss Georgie De Lara, who makes the most of all the opportunities afforded her by the authors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Big Attraction for Next Week.</head>
        <p>Big Attraction for Next Week.</p>
        <p>The sporting drama, "The Gin-1:-Iler,!' will form the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre next week, and will be presented by Mr. Harry Foxwell's company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LADY BOXERS AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>LADY BOXERS AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A genuine and extremely clever exhibition of boxing is given at the Hippodrome this week by four lady champions. The turn is decidedly novel and proves to be popular with Warring- ton audiences. Several exciting rounds are 'fought by Mdlle. Carpentier (champion of the world), Miss Lucie Warner (champion of Eng- land), Miss Adele Neilson (champion of Nor- way and Denmark), and Mdlle. Alice Fleury (champion of Belgium). The ladies display great knowledge of the fistic art, and it is sel- dom that the referee, Mdlle. Jeanette de Lys, has to interfere. A smart ball-punching and club swinging performance is given by Miss Vera Caine. The other turns that go to make up an excellent programme are also very enter- taining and include "The Touch of the Child," a dramatised version of Tom Gallon's story of that name. The playlet is admirably presented by Archie Chappell and Henry Dea's Company. "His Face Is His Fortune!" This old phrase can be used with much truth concerning Ed. E. Ford, the Australian " sundowner," who causes roars of laughter with his comical facial expres- sions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Two Funny Sketches Next Week.</head>
        <p>Two Funny Sketches Next Week.</p>
        <p>The "top of the bill" turn at the Hippodrome next week will be provided by the Howard and Collinson Trio, in the screamingly funny sketch, " 'Enery's 'Oliday," whilst another laughable playlet "Jock's Invention, or the Humophone," will be presented by Walter Kemp and Com- pany. Other attractions will be the Kotenichi Troupe in a display of Japanese conjuring; the Eight Varsity Girls, in "Revue Revels"; the Lane and Dale Trio, on their unicycles and bicycles; Lena Bluette, the soprano vocalist; Haydn Mars, "the musical marvel "; and Neville Delmar, with a budget of comic sZings.</p>
        <p>LICENSEES ON THE WATCH</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The first of the two splendid programmes at the Star Kinema House this week included "Her 'Really' Mother," a pathetic film, which strongly appealed to the sentiments of the audi- ences. The war news contained in the " Gaumont Graphic" and " Pathe's Animated Gazette" was a very great attraction, and the special, "Our Cavalry's Magnificent Horseman- ship," was one of the most instructive military subjects yet filmed. The house rocked with laughter during the screening of "The Impor- tance of Being Another Man's Wife," with that celebrated comedian, Arthur Roberts, in the chief part. Thrilling adventures were shown in "Three Bags of Silver," an instalment of the serial. "Adventures of Kathlyn," and "A Canine Thief" illustrated a new crooks' ruse.</p>
        <p>The second programme, now showing, includes that great naval drama, "Orders Under Seal," and a grand selection of war pictures, which should not be missed. "Sign of the Cross" Next Week. • -- -</p>
        <p>A filmed version of the late Wilson Barrett's great play, "The Sign of the Cross," will form the chief attraction at the Star Kinema next week, when special vocal numbers will be ren- dered by Miss Marie Houghion and Madame Raf,Aone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>"In the Shadow of Disgrace," "The Crash," and "Lost by a Hair," are three of the engross- ing dramatic films to be shown to-night, and several productions in lighter vein send the "houses" into fits of laughter. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Next Week.</head>
        <p>Next Week.</p>
        <p>In addition to numerous Other varied attrac- tions the "star" films for next week will he "The Rival Railway Plot" and "Nina of the Stage."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>The programme for the second part of this i week should not be missed, f only for the sake of "The Suicide Club," a two-reel drama based upon the tale of that name in the New Ara- bian Nights," by the famous novelist, Robert Louis Stevenson. Another drama which is of absorbing interest is entitled "Blotted Out."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Net Week.</head>
        <p>Net Week.</p>
        <p>The two principal films to be exhibited next week are "The Tenth Commandment" and The Daughter of the Tribe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LICENSEES ON A WATCH COMMITTEE —continued.</head>
        <p>LICENSEES ON A WATCH COMMITTEE —continued.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peacock, as he then was known, was can- didating for Whitecross Ward.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—ln your report of the Watch Committee proceedings last week I find that another repre- sentative of "The Trade" has been elected, and this practically by Liberal votes. All honour to Aldermen Tinnion and Pierpoint, and Coun- cillors Locker, Shaw, Wilkinson, Skeriand and Lindley, who courageously -voted against this reactionary proceeding. The following Liberals, viz., the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), Council- lors Dickenson, Halliwell„ Cunnington and Gough, were found in company with four mem- bers of "The Trade" voting for a licensed vic- tualler on this committee, while three Liberals remained neutral, viz., Alderman James Evans, and Councillors Henshall and Barton. A very large number of Liberal electors naturally ex- pect that their own representatives, at any rate, are sound on a question of principle involving, as in this instance, disinterested action on the part of every member of the Watch Committee. This committee exists not only to hear charges against the police, but, as individual members, to prompt the police in the performance of their duty, a considerable ,part of their work having to do with licence-holders. - There are two gentlemen in particular (I refer to Alderman James Evans and Councillor Hen- shall) who, although neutral, puzzle me ,and a number of others. These gentlemen stand before the public of Warrington respected, honoured, and esteemed. We look to such in their public capacity to give a lead on ques- tions where a moral principle is involved, but what do we find? Not only silence of speech, , but failure to vote as well. Some time ago Councillor Henshall successfully contested a seat in the Council held by a publican. He was enthusiastically supported by a number of workers, who put temperance principles in the , forefront of their banner, and now when he has an opportunity to give effect by his vote to their natural expectattons he sits on the fence.' This attitude disheartens everyone whose aim is to free the town from the undue influence of vested interests.</p>
        <p>On the general question at election times Liberal candidates invariably tell you that the influence of "The Trade" is unsparingly used to scotch social reform. It is discouraging to see that when they have an opportunity they fail to give effect to their principles.7Yonrs._&amp;c.,</p>
        <p>iDETN- GRIFFITITS. Rylanas-street. Warrington, 19th November, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Strong Protests Against</p>
        <p>Council Action. RESOLUTIONS AND LETTERS' The action of the Town Council last vieekJ;r: electing Councillor Chrimes' who is "tot licence holder, to a position Oil the "to' Committee, has been much criticised; -01 obpelionlronwteo wgihviechsotmhee eloefcttiohne h eaxs p gr vs se if io nr</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE UNION cosnr*</p>
        <p>NATION. 'ttes ° At a meeting bf the executive CO dlO the Warrington Band of Hope Vni"' Cairo-street Schoolroom on Thursday, lowing resolution was unanimously Passe".37oo6 That this Union, representing ehile.ren, strongly condemns the action of I% • Ong on Warrington Town Council in apPoin „d s the Watch Committee a brewer a" publican. over „.1</p>
