      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>I,Tary e matron of the Warrington Infirmary and 'isMagary acknowledges with thanks the re- of twenty useful articles of clothing from "le South Lancashire Needlework Guild.</p>
        <p>For Infirma</p>
        <p>Patients,</p>
        <p>Christadelphian Lecture. in the Christadelphian Meeting Room, Bold- ttreet, on Monday evening, Mr. Andrew Morris, f Manchester, will give an address on "God's Plan for the Abolition of Every Evil—Political, +Social and Religious." P.T.C. Supper. sixty of the members of the Latchford Section :5f the Warrington and District Preparatory rraining Corps supped together off the succu- lent hot-pot at the Royal Oak Hotel on Thurs- (lay evening, Mr. W. H. Bellian presiding.</p>
        <p>- Successful Students. In the list of results of the recent Civil Ser- ./ice (2nd division clerkships) Examinations, Published yesterday, appear the names of two tartington students-131st, E. Jeffery; 140th, 7. C. Ealraford. There were 1,928 candidates. Shorthand Writers. There was a good attendance at the weekly ~leeting of the Shorthand Writers' Association, !;eld at Wood's College, Market Gate, on Tues.; ‘'tty evening, when, in 'addition to the usual !Peed practice, -a very interesting discussion took place on the advantages of advanced 'Phrasing. • No Use to "Davy Jones."</p>
        <p>On board the Clan Grant, one of the vessels itri.k by the notorious Emden, was a remark- able collection of plans got together by Professor Geddes, of Edinburgh, among them being the prize designs for laying out the two estates of the Warrington Garden Suburbs Company.</p>
        <p>A Quiet Wedding. At Aughton Parish Church on Tuesday the W edd n g took place quietly of Mr. Harley Roberts, second son of Mr. William Roberts, of Rarleydene, Latchford, Warrington, and Miss Mabel Leary, eldest daughter of Mr. Isaac 1'."17, of Aughton. '.l:lie Rev. T. Pickthall offi- 9ated. A large number of useful and valu- able presents were received.</p>
        <p>"Creation of Ideals."</p>
        <p>At the Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel on Sun- (l'l3: evening the Rev. Irvine Lister commenced a series of special addresses on the subject of The Influence of Religion upon Society." The first of these dealt with " The Creation of Ileals," and in it Mr. Lister showed that Chris- tian ideals had had, and would continue to have, a tremendous influence on society. In the 91ristian religion there was an ideal for the pdividual, for society, and for the whole world. that for the last-named, he said, was Peace, and the Prince of Peace had given a glorious niDle of peaceful progress and development. -1-t Was in the eternal realities that they should always place their faith.</p>
        <p>Music Master's Lecture io Musicians. Mr. W. S. Woods, music master at the War- Vhgton. Training College, gave a lecture on it.tturday afternoon in the Royal Institution, lverPool, before the local members of the In- corporated Society of Musicians, on " The Prac- tical Usefulness of the Tonic Solfa Method to the Professional Musician." The four subjects treated were melody, rhythm (by means of Preach time names), harmony and change of kl,e3*. The points were illustrated by a class of from the Fonthill-road Council School. he lecture was followed with the keenest in- terest, and at the close a very cordial vote of „.thanks to the lecturer was proposed by Dr. C. Reynolds, and seconded by Dr. A. W. Pollitt.</p>
        <p>Councillor airlines and the AVatch In our Committee. Council report of the discussion in the Town Chri Preceding the election of Councillor mes on the Watch Committee, we gave pub- licity to the following:— Councillor CHRIMES : I thant you for your Pluck.</p>
        <p>This comment was made upon Con ncillor lArcher's speech defending Councillor Chrimes' ?; Pos it ion . It was made by Councillor Cunning- ')ll and not Councillor Chrimes, who did not I,,llterverie at all in the discussion. It was ' ‘-:ouncillor Lindley and not Councillor Barton who seconded Alderman Tinnion's amendment ' Ito Posing to substitute Councillor Craik for " ouncillor Chrimes.</p>
        <p>Mayor and Mayoress "At Home." The Mayor and Mayoress made this year's At Home," held in the Museum on Thursday, serve a dual purpose—that of meeting their ltiluerous friends and of helping to•make the hristmas of many a poor child very much r"ater, for it 'was stipulated that the "At Ile" would only be held on condition that rsitors should bring a doll or a toy, the col- trion thus acquired to be distributed amongst le Door little ones at Christmas. The re- . Eponse was splendid, and the following ladies fled a very busy time in collecting the presents LindleyllS they were handed in : Mesdames Osborne, Bebbington, Down and Locker, and the Misses I. and E. Wilkinson. and Short. A Pathetic note was struck when Mrs. Delooze, of Behool-brow, informed the Mayor that her hus- band had been killed at the front quite recently and asked him to sign her papers.</p>
        <p>3 Special sleltry7(icSeisein GcolniiiiiecrtSioilitNaviltlri *the annual of the Wesley Guilds in the Warrington elrenit were held on Sunday at the Bold-street W eB.-eYan Church, the proceedings being con- in the same church on Monday evening. 11 Sunday the Rev. W. Thompson Derry, the "Perintendent minister of the circuit, was the Meacher morning and evening, and his dis, Z),llTses were eagerly followed by large congre- -70-"one• The same gentleman also presided ,ver the rally on Monday evening, and in the crourse „ of an opening address made sympathetic I.leilerence to the death of Lord Roberts, a report , T which will be found in another column.— he e Guilds represented at the rally were: Bold-1 Teet, Bewsey-road, Padgate, Penketh, Sankey 4d,ges, Latchford, Stockton Heath and Moore. lle various Guild secretaries and the general '':eretary (Mr. Kenneth Bishop) all presented r,l'S gratifying reports, showing that the il3ours of their respective Guilds are not being 41Jried on vain.—Miss Bessie Dutton was the io`l_,tllc,i,Pal speaker, and her address was listened ''h• rapt attention by the large gathering.— Iv] re* to a vote of thanks—the seconder of „ (Dr. Bennett) described her as a Inrularch of local Methodism "—Miss Dutton sttil_algY remarked that that was the first time 'new that she occupied such -a lofty posi- .o°ll, but if she really were a monarch she ”ght that she would have the public-houses eXed a little earlier and so reduce to some 'lent the temptation of drink.</p>
        <p>Silver-Toligued Orator7Preacher. Locai Wesleyan Methodists are looking for- Withgreat interest to the visit of the Rev. 101118 dale T. Young, the president of the W.M. ti°leaference. Mr. Young is regarded as one of ,finest preachers, not only in his own con- but throughout the Nonconformist kii,n;es of to-day. "Upon this supreme gift," Wei me' writer of an appreciative character wipeb in "The Methodist Recorder," his fame Probably rest. This gift [of preaching] Res his other gifts. But it would be a at Mistake to imagine that the other gifts ,re Dre ilat there Mr. Young is a born ranr• Everything in his build, everything • is deportment, spells the public orator. tl voice compasses the whole gamut , , the w human emotions—passion. pathos, irony, "-the 43aing appeal, the thundrous declamation q. 0,1 se are all stops in that wonderful organ. i Fivi gave him that voice for the people, and n g it to the people he has given it back to • He has probably done more than fY Other public man to keep alive the ideal t at 11a sermon-lecture." Mr. Young was born tral nrbridge-on-Tyne, and underwent his '711411g for the ministry at Ifeadinglev College. 1, e reverend gentleman is visiting Warrington t2aneetion with the anniversary celebrations 13old-street Wesleyan Church. He is due Ittthla evening of November 26th. when he will The"-iscl On "The Church and the Present War." Riiod anniversary celebrations commence on a„.V. when the special preacher will be the ' Lefroy Yorke.</p>
        <p>li.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>"Christianity and War." , At the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket- street, to-morrow (Sunday) i evening, Mrs, Agatha Watts, of Manchester, will deliver a public address on the above-named subject, a cordial invitation being issued to all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>"How To Understand Pictures."</head>
        <p>"How To Understand Pictures."</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Warrington Educa- tion Committee the second free lantern lecture on the above subject will be given in the School of Art, on Tuesday evening next, by the Head- master (Mr. Clarence Mawson).</p>
        <p>Missionary Anniversary at Wycliffe</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>Missionary anniversary celebrations commence at the Wycliffe Congregational Church to-night, when a meeting will be addressed by the Revs. A. H. Caren (South China) and R. Griffith (Madagascar). To-morrow evening the Rev. R. Griffith will be the special preacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Electric Light for Outlying Districts.</head>
        <p>Electric Light for Outlying Districts.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from a detailed Parliamentary notice in to-day's Examiner that the Warring- ton Corporation intend to apply in the Session 1915 fOr power to supply electricity to Stockton Heath, Latchford Without, Thelwall, Grappen- hall, Lower Walton, Higher Walton, Appleton, Penketh and Great Sankey. The notice will be read with deep interest by ,the districts con- cerned.</p>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyans. A successful meeting in support of Wesleyan Missions was held at the Padgate Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening, Mr. F. Hickman, of Warrington, presiding. The speaker was the Rev. T. H. Wainman, who has had twenty years' experience as a missionary in South Africa, and who went through the Boer War as a chaplain to H.M. Forces. The collection amounted to .t 8 15s. 3d.</p>
        <p>Fatal Sequel to an Accident. Whilst following his occupation as a brakes- man on the Dallam clown branch of the London and North-Western Railway line on Monday, Thomas Preston Taylor (57), of 9, Lexden- street, Warrington, was knocked down by a railway waggon, and received such injuries that he died in the Infirmary the same day. At the inquest on Thursday the Coroner's Jury re- turned a verdict of "Accidental death." Mr. J. Longland represented the family of the deceased.</p>
        <p>Bowling Club Supper. A goodly company of the members of the King and Queen Bowling Club assembled for their annual hot-pot supper at the hostelry from which the club takes its name, on Thursday evening, when the President (Mr. T. S. Steel) occupied the chair.—Mr. J. Wood, the secretary of the Warrington and District Bowling Asso- ciation, responding to the toast of the associa- tion, said that, despite the troublous times through which the country was passing, the past Beason was a highly successful one from the bowls-man's point of view.—Prizes, of the total value of ..t22, were presented. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>From Soldier to Town Councillor.</head>
        <p>From Soldier to Town Councillor.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we announce the at the age of 67, of Mr. Joseph Shillcock, licen- see of the Mulberry Tree Hotel, Stockton Heath, which sad event occurred on Monday morning. Upon completing a term of service in the Army, Mr. Shillcock became a police officer, first at Halifax and afterwards at War- rington, where he was promoted to sergeant and drill instructor. Upon quitting the force he took the licence of the Bay Horse Hotel, later on moving into the White Hart Hotel, Sankey street, and later still to the Mulberry Tree Hotel. For several years he represented the Town Hall Ward on the Warrington Town Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Late Mr. Ernest Clayton.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Ernest Clayton.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Monday afternoon of the late Mr. Ernest Clay- ton, of 13, Orford-avenue, who for 23 years uas connected with Messrs. Arthur Waring and Company's Tannery, Winwick-road. The de- ceased gentleman was for many years a sidesman at St. Peter's Church and actively identified himself with the Young Men's Club in his parish. Friendly Society work also claimed his attention, and he was a popular member of the Orford Cricket Club and the Warrington Bowl- ing Club. Politically he was a Conservative. He passed away on Friday of last week at the early age of 37, leaving a widow and a little daughter to mourn their loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>" BOBS" A MODEL ENGLISHMAN.</head>
        <p>" BOBS" A MODEL ENGLISHMAN.</p>
        <p>AVesleyan Minister's Tribute.</p>
        <p>A tribute to the memory of the "Grand Old Man of the Army," the late Lord Roberts, was also forthcoming at the annual rally of the ' Wesley Guilds in the Warrington Circuit, held at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Monday evening, when the Rev. W. T. Derry. presided, and, after making a brief reference to Wesley Guild work, said he would like to say a word about that model Englishman—Lord Roberts. Fortunately when a man died his spirit did not die with him, neither did his influence, which, in the case of this great man, would be even stronger now than perhaps it would have been if he had been permitted to remain amongst them. Lord Roberts was one of the first men in the nation. tHear, hear.) He had courage that was indispensable to a soldier, and, although it was common to most of the men in the forces, there was something, an added attri- bute about Lord Roberts, that was not quite so common in the soldier, and that was sympathy and tenderheartedness. That joint attribute was what Lord Roberts possessed in a marked degree; it was a distinguishing feature in his character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Beloved .By His Men.</head>
        <p>Beloved .By His Men.</p>
        <p>It was said that one reason why the British were holding the Germans at bay was because. the German officers treated their men, not as men, but as machines. Lord Roberts never treated his men as machines, and proof of that was found in the affection shown to him by his soldiers. He was certainly tenderhearted, and if a man did make a mistake through his ten- derness of heart it was a mistake which every- one would surely pardon. Another want in the dead soldier's character was his modesty; a thing, which, as a rule, was not to be found in a soldier. He had warned the nation about the great war which was in progress, but when his words came true he was too modest to say "I told you so!" During the last five years 50 per cent. of the men enlisting in the Indian Army were total abstainers. That was chiefly owing to Lord Roberts's influence and example. It would be difficult to find a correspondingly large body of working men in which there was such a per- centage of total abstainers. (Hear, hear.) An- other point in Lord Roberts's character was his splendid patriotism. When Lord Lansdowne sent for him to go to South Africa the grand old Field Marshal said: "For 19 years I have been living temperately and taking regular exercise in order to be fit for such an oppor- tunity as this." Old Warrior's Undaunted Spirit.</p>
        <p>" Whether you agree with him or not with regard to his views about National Service," concluded the speaker, "you cannot but admire the undaunted spirit of the old man, who de- voted the last years of his life to bringing before his countrymen the subject which he regarded as of paramount importance—the safety of the Empire! Last week he said: lam going to visit the Indian soldiers. . . . . It seems to be the most useful thing I can do</p>
        <p>now ! Lord Roberts had been interesting himself in the soldiers and had been asking for field glasses, &amp;c., for their use. The day had come when he rendered his last service, and he died out in Prance, within sound of the guns, and, as he himself would wish to die, close to many comrades with whom he had served in 'past years. He was a great soldier, he was a great gentleman, and he was •a great Christian. (Applause) A SAD SIGN. _ _</p>
        <p>On all the public buildings on Thursday the flags were flown at half-mast, as a token of respect to the great warrior whose remains were interred thai aa:: in St. Paul's Cathedral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERALS' DRAW FOR</p>
        <p>AMBULANCE.</p>
        <p>The draw promoted by the local Women's Lib- eral Association in aid of the fund being raised by the Women Liberals of Lancashire and Che- shire, to provide a motor ambulance for the front, has resulted in a substantial sum being forwarded to headquarters. The doll was won by ticket No. 417, the owner of which should communicate with Mrs. Osborne, 22, Bold-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LORNE HOUSE SCHOLARS' HELP.</head>
        <p>LORNE HOUSE SCHOLARS' HELP.</p>
        <p>In aid of the fund for providing comforts for the local Territorials who are serving at the front, a bazaar was held at Lorne House School, Froghall-lane, on Thursday, the sum of £6 58. 11. d, being realised as a result of the efforts put forth by the scholars. The bazaar was opened by Mrs. Gillen, and teachers and pupils were in charge of the stalls.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE SCHOLARS' CHRISTMAS BOXES FOR OUR FIGHTERS.</p>
        <p>The boys and girls of the Wycliffe Council School have set a splendid example to War- rington scholars, by contributing, out of their own pocket money, the sum of £2 2s. to Princess Mary's Soldiers' and Sailors' Christmas Box Fund. This sum has been forwarded to the authorities during the week. Ar.</p>
        <p>£29 FROM WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE. The whist drive and dance at the John Cros- field Memorial Hall last week, given by Mrs. Firth Lindley and Mrs. T. Locker, quite apart from the Women's Liberal Association, realised £29. This handsome sum has been divided as follows :—To the mine sweepers' fund, £l2; to a local fund for providing comforts for our soldiers, £l5; and to the Mayor's tobacco fund, £2. Mrs. Lindley and Mrs. Locker thank the many ladies and gentlemen for attending and helping to make the event the success it was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ATTRACTIVE PATRIOTIC FAVOUR.</head>
        <p>ATTRACTIVE PATRIOTIC FAVOUR.</p>
        <p>Mrs. G. S. Pendlebury, 88, Bewsey-road, wife of Mr. Pendlebury, assistant master at the School of Art, designed for her own use, at the outbreak of the war, a patriotic favour, paint- ing on a broad satin ribbon the flags of the five Allies. This was so much admired that she determined to paint others and sell them for the benefit of the War Funds. So great has been the demand that Mrs. Pendlebury has been kept busy producing them ever since, and, although the price is only 3d., she has raised ..t4 for the object named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>RED-LETTER DAY.</head>
        <p>A RED-LETTER DAY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Edelsten reports :—Thursday was a red- letter day in the history of the War Clothing Committee. The ,Borough Treasurer arrived like Father Christmas, laden with parcels, con- taining 26 excellent second-hand suits of cloth- ing, besides 15 pairs of trousers, overcoats, &amp;c. These were received with the greatest joy and dispatched to Fazakerley, as well as a large bundle of shirts, socks, and warm jerseys from St. Paul's Mothers' Meeting and G.F.S.; 2doz. mufflers from the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform League; and knitted braces and face washers from the Hamilton-street Schools, and gifts from other friends. Letters of thanks were received from the Indian Soldiers' Clothing Fund, and from Major R. D. Willcocks, warmly thanking the commit- tee for their gift of clothing for the hospital ship for the Indian wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LECTURE FOR RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>LECTURE FOR RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>In aid of the funds of the temporary Red Cross Hospital established at Raddon Court, a lecture on "Florence Nightingale" will be given at the Parr Hall, Warrington, on Monday even- ing, December 7th, by Mrs. Ridgway, of Lymm. The lecture will be under the patronage of the Mayor and Mayoress and many other influential ladies and gentlemen. It was at first arranged that the proceeds of the lecture should go to the Belgian Red Cross Fund, but as it was reported in the papers that the Belgian Red Cross Fund had been seized by Germany, it was decided that as Haddon Court was now in use, and a number of Belgians were receiving attention there, to give the money to the Raddon Court Hospital instead. It would have been difficult to choose a more appropriate lecturer than Mrs. Ridgway, and all who are able to do so should seize the oppor- tunity of hearing her upon a subject that will claim her fullest sympathy. Particulars are advertised in to-day's "Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>VICAR ASKS FOR CHRISTMAS BOXES FOR</head>
        <p>VICAR ASKS FOR CHRISTMAS BOXES FOR</p>
        <p>" TOMMY."</p>
        <p>Writing in the current issue of the Burton - wood Parish Magazine, the Vicar (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) says:—" We have some forty or more local ` Tommies' mostly in training in the South. ' .... We wish them to know they are not forgotten, not one of them, in prayer and kindly thought. Christmas will enable us to show our appreciation of our young fellow-parishioners in khaki, and to assure them that out of sight they are not out of mind, that we are with them in spirit if far apart in body. Would it not be kind to send each one of OUR khaki-men a little remembrance at the festival of Peace and Goodwill? Some small gift, some small token of affectionate interest sent from the old parish and the old church would do much to cheer the absent soldiers and sailors during a Christmas-tide which must, in any circumstances, be depressing for them. The gift need not take the rather over-done form of chocolate or cigarettes. Some other token'of re- membrance can be easily provided. . • • A suffidiency of money is the only need . . • • a pound or two!" The Vicar adds that a con- tribution box has been placed in the baptistry of St. Michael's for the purpose of receiving subscriptions, which, it is urged, should be made by the first week in December.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FOR THE WAR FUNDS. Successful "Fun Taft" at the Secondary School.</head>
        <p>FOR THE WAR FUNDS. Successful "Fun Taft" at the Secondary School.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Secondary School on Sat- urday evening was held the fourth scholars' social in aid of the war funds, the arrange- ments this time being in the hands of the Bur- sar Form. There -was a splendid attendance of pupils and parents, and the many entertain- ments provided shadowgraphy, bagatelle, cake's weight guessing competition, fortune- telling, hat-trimming, humorous art gallery, and disc-throwing—were all well patronised. The toffee stall proved a specially strong attrac- tion, running short of supplies at an early hour. A draw for framed engravings of the Prince of Wales and Princess Mary realised the respect- able sum of 295., and the total proceeds amounted to £4 10s.</p>
        <p>The teachers and pupils have now collected over•X37 towards the vaious war funds; and, in addition, the girls under the superintendence of the senior mistress have promised to supply 200 pairs of mittens for the local Territorials. They have already forwarded to the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Fund a consignment of useful garments; comprising woollen cuffs, Bala- clava helmets, scarves, body-belts, tobacco and cigarettes, and other comforts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WORKHOUSE HOSPITAL FOR WOUNDED. •</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE HOSPITAL FOR WOUNDED. •</p>
        <p>The committee of the Runcorn Board of Guardians appointed to consider the question of provision for the reception of wounded sol- diers at the hospital reported to the Board on Tuesday that at a meeting they had resolved that steps be taken for making provision for wounded soldiers, subject to the permission of the Local, Government Board and to the War Department being prepared to pay 2s. per head per day for maintenance. The committee in- spected the rooms and resolved that the re- quired number of beds with bedding be pur- chased; that the necessary lavatory accommo- dation be provided; that two anthracite stoves be purchased, and that the Chairman, with Messrs. Morris, Pilling, Mclntyre, Woodward and Spruce, the Master and the Medical Officer, be appointed a sub-committee to complete the arrangements. On the proposition of Mr. Woodward, seconded by Mr. Morris, the committee's recommenda- tions were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>"DISMISSED" COMMITTEEMEN.</head>
        <p>"DISMISSED" COMMITTEE- MEN.</p>
        <p>Councillors Resign as Protest.</p>
        <p>Councillors H. Roberts and J. G. Craik, who were voted off several important committees last week by the Town Council, have, as a protest, resigned from other committees. They give their decision in letters to the Town Clerk as under:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>Kingsley, Hill Cliffe, Stockton Heath, 16th November, 1914. Dear Mr. Town Clerk,—l am obliged for the notices of the meetings of the various com- mittees upon which I was appointed on 'Mon- day, the 9th instant. In view of the fact that my re-election upon the Watch and Gas Committees was prevented by the reactionaries of the Council I regret that I cannot serve upon any committee except the Education Committee during the current year. The action of the individuals concerned was undoubtedly intended to cause me personal an- noyance, but they have overlooked the fact that they have not only outraged public opinion, but they have also grossly insulted the electors of Whitecross Ward, who will resent-the indignity thus gratuitously imposed upon them in the person of their oldest representative. The Corporation Gas Undertaking, owing to the large amount of ratepayers' capital in- volved, and .the volume of business transacted, requires in its management the help of the best commercial brains and experience which the Council possesses. Ido not think there is a man in Warrington so itted as to suggest that the committee has been strengthened in this important particular by the addition of some, at least, of the individuals who were placed upon it last Monday. The whole circumstances attending the pro- ceedings in question constitute a deliberate breach of the "truce," owing to which the municipal skins of certain members—who would never have been re-elected on their merits— were saved, and is a sorry exhibition of the con- temptible state of mind which enables a certain class of persons in this great crisis of our nation's history, to forget all the decencies of public life.—Yours faithfully, H. ROBERTS. J. Lyon Whittle, Esq., Town Clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>COUNCILLOR CRAIK. Manehester-road, Warrington,</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR CRAIK. Manehester-road, Warrington,</p>
        <p>-November 16th, '1914.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—As a protest against the disgrace- ful and un-English conduct the members of the Town Council have meted out to me, after 12' years' service, I beg to resign my membership- of all the committees the Council in their "wisdom" thought fit to elect me to serve , upon.—Yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>JAS. G. CRAM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>VICAR'S SON VOLUNTEERS FOR BOTHA. Mr. Bernard Mitchell, the eldest son of the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwood, who went to South Africa in 1912, has volunteered for active service under General Botha. In the current issue of the Parish Magazine the Vicar asks his congregation to remember Mr. Mitchell in their prayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>RUNCORN GAS COMPANY'S AID.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GAS COMPANY'S AID.</p>
        <p>_ The Runcorn Gas Company has given 100 tons of coke in one cwt. bags for use as required by the local Distress Committee. Those employes who have enlisted are receiving from the com- pany half-wages, and will be reinstated at the conclusion of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>READY FOR ACTIVE SERVICE AGAIN.</head>
        <p>READY FOR ACTIVE SERVICE AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Private Yeates, Orford, who has been at home seven weeks recovering from the effects of shrap- nel wounds in both thighs and hips, received in the Battle of the Aisne, left home on Thursday morning, to report himself at Exeter (the head- quarters of the Devonshire Regiment) as fit for active service again,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WOUNDED AND A PRISONER.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED AND A PRISONER.</p>
        <p>We understand that Vernon Owen, of the 4th King's Own Lancasters, of 48, Chester-road, is now wounded and a prisoner in Germany. He took part in the Battle of Mons, and answered the roll call on August 25th, but did not respond when his name was called the following day.</p>
        <p>MORE AMBULANCE MEN FOR THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FRONT.</head>
        <p>FRONT.</p>
        <p>Four more members of the local corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade—Corporal Hank in- son, and Privates Looker, Rigby and Tanner— have volunteered for service at the front, and left for Shoreham Camp, Brighton, on Wed- nesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>The number of wounded soldiers—Belgian and British—under treatment at the Infirmary yes- terday was 37. _ _ _</p>
        <p>There arrived at Raddon Court on Saturday eight more wounded soldiers, which brings the number of recuperating warriors now there up to 33-15 Belgian and 18 British. To-day it is expected that seven or eight will leave, and their places will be filled by another batch of the same number, coming from the Western Hospital, Manchester. _ _</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon Miss Etta Balmer, of Newton-le-Willows, visited Raddon Court, and kindly entertained the wounded soldiers with a varied musical programme. The songs she rendered in the French language were specially appreciated by our foreign friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>STILL MORE RECRUITS WANTED.</head>
        <p>STILL MORE RECRUITS WANTED.</p>
        <p>During the week 165 men have been accented at Orford Barracks as recruits for Lord Kitch- ener's Army, whilst 70 have offered themselves for service with the Territorials. Under a new arrangement the men who offer themselves are drafted to Blackpool for training immediately upon acceptance, not a day being wasted at home.</p>
        <p>More men are urgently needed for Kitchener's Army, especially in Infantry regiments. In- tending recruits—who must stand sft. 3in. or over, and have a minimum chest measurement of 34fin. and must be between the ages of 19 and 38—should apply at the Central Police Buildings, or the Beamont Schools. The Army Ordnance Corps still requires blacksmiths, clerks, foremen, assistant foremen, hammermen, saddlers, storemen and wheelers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>OUR BELGIAN GUESTS.</head>
        <p>OUR BELGIAN GUESTS.</p>
        <p>Black Bear House, Latchford, in which the local Baptists have undertaken to maintain a family of Belgian refugees, is rapidly being got ready. With the object Of raising a small sum to swell the fund required for the maintenance of the Bold-street hostel of refuge, Mr. A. Clay, the manager of the Liberal Club, gave a duck as the first prize in a draw which took place on Saturday, the fund being benefited to the extent of 16s. as the result of his gift and efforts. Mr. George Miller, wholesale fruit merchant, realised £l7 for the Mayor's Belgian Relief Fund, from the box of fine Belgian pears which was to be seen last week on his stall in the Market. A number of the Belgian refugees who have been staying in Lymrn returned to France last Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>BURTONWOOD MEN AT THE FRONT,</head>
        <p>BURTONWOOD MEN AT THE FRONT,</p>
        <p>i News is given n the current issue of the Burtonwood Parish Magazine as to the where- abouts and the doings of two of the gallant " Tommies" from the village, who left these shores for France soon after the outbreak of the war. "One, John Fenny," writes the Vicar, "is a prisoner in Germany, which fact ensures his temporary safety. He is allowed to write and receive letters; indeed, he would seem to be kindly treated. "John Riley took part in the battle of Mons, being one of 60 who returned with a whole skin out of a company of 1,200! What carnage! The good Hand of our God was upon our soldier friend, who was, however, wounded and inval- ided home, but has now returned to the front again.. May he enjoy the same good fortune as before—better fortune, coming out of the fiery ordeal unscathed, without scratch or bruise!"</p>
        <p>If some people had a strong sense of humour they would die laughing at themselves.</p>
        <p>If ignorance were really bliss, there would be far less misery than there is in the world.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BEER OR COFFEE?</head>
        <p>BEER OR COFFEE?</p>
        <p>Guardians Discuss Inmates' Christmas Beverage. YULE-TIDE GRANTS FOR OUTDOOR POOR</p>
        <p>The Christmas allowances to inmates in the Workhouse constituted the chief matter dis- cussed at the fortnightly meeting of the War- rington Board of Guardians on Wednesday. At a meeting of the House Visiting Committee the Master's estimate for supplies for the next fort- night and for Christmas extras was approved. The extras included a certain allowance of beer to the inmates, and this was taken exception to by three members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DRINK-WRECKED LIFE.</head>
        <p>A DRINK-WRECKED LIFE.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dale moved as an amendment to the resolution: "That the allowance for intoxicants be deleted from the Christmas fare, and that a substitute which would be equivalent in value be given instead," He did that, he said, for various reasons. He hoped they would not think that the feast would be incomplete if the intoxicants were absent. For a number of years he had assisted in providing a Christmas treat for 600 or 700 persons, and there had not been any intoxicating beverages there, and he ventured to say without fear of contradiction that there was as much joy round that board as round any other board at which sat a simi- lar number of persons. It fell to his lot, on the summons of the Clerk, to make a special visit to the House last week, and to his sur- prise he met an individual whom he had not seen for twenty years. That man had been a slave to intemperance, or he would not have been in the institution that day. His wife and family had turned their backs on him because of his drinking habits. The man had admitted that he was now considerably better than he had been for some time. That was only one type of a number the Guardians had under their control, and he (the speaker) was of the opinion that it would be more cruel than kind to present temptation to men of that description at Christmas time, because he be- lieved it would only stir up an almost uncon- trollable desire for more. He hoped the Board would reconsider the matter. It had been an old custom, hut there was no necessity to con- tinue with it if they thought it was harmful to the inmates -of the House. (Heir, hear.) The Bishop of London declared at a recent meetinr, that lie had been accustomed to being invited" to dinners, and expressed the opinion that two-thirds of those who attended dinner parties took non-intoxicating beverages. The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): Funny dinners!</p>
        <p>Mr. Dale: That is the statement he made, and I believe it. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Hear, hear. The Chairman : •I don't doubt it. The Rev. A. Ist. Mitchell: A splendid man! Mr. Dale: I don't think we should be doing any good to the inmates by offering them in- toxicants after they have been so long under our regime. We should consider this for the sake of the inmates, especially those who are brought here through their intemperate habits. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell seconded the amend- ment, and said he was glad that Mr. Dale had emphasised—though not as much as he might have done—the fact that it was not the wish of total abstainers that the inmates should be deprived of any pleasure or enjoyment. What was proposed was to remove that which was worse and substitute that which was better—to take away the lesser and give the greater. He would suggest coffee and hot milk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MISTAKEN KINDNESS.</head>
        <p>MISTAKEN KINDNESS.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent said that she had been un- decided on the subject, but would support the amendment. It was no kindness to the inmates to give them a craving for something which they had been trying to overcome. She did not think anyone could accuse her of trying to de- prive the inmates of their pleasure. Mr. Billinge said he was absent from the House Committee meeting and did not know what the extras were. The Chairman: That the usual amount of beer be allowed! Mr. Billinge : What is the allowance? The Chairman: As in previous years. Mr. Billinge : What is that?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PINT FOR MEN; GILL FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>PINT FOR MEN; GILL FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Mather: A pint for the men and a gill for the women. Mr. Billino'n pointed out that non-intoxicants were provided for those who desired them. The inmates had the option of having what they desired. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Mr. Mitchell said he would like to take away the lesser and give the greater. It is like taking a threepenny bit and giving a halfpenny. Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I would make it up in whatever way it could be done. Mr. Mather: After all the strong remarks made, our great man, the Chancellor of the Exchequer, has made-no distinction be- tween tea and ale Let us put them both on the same scale and give the inmates tea or ale, whichever they like. Mr. Ritchie: I propose that they have the beer as usual. I have a glass of beer myself, and I should enjoy my Christmas dinner all the better knowing that the inmates were hav- ing a glass of beer, or a pint. If there is one thing I like better than a glass of beer it is a pint. (Laughter.) Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It is a halfpenny per glass more, you know.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WORTH THE BUDGET RISE, SAYS A MEMBER.</head>
        <p>WORTH THE BUDGET RISE, SAYS A MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ritchie: I don't care if it is a penny; it is worth it. (Laughter.) These people can have "pop" if they want it. Mr. Hughes supported Mr. Ritchie's proposal. Mr. Bent said that, being in the Trade, he was rather backward in intervening in the dis- cussion of such matters. However, he would be sorry to ,see a repetition of what occurred at the feast last year—one-half of the glasses filled with water and the other half with beer. He had the glasses of water emptied and re-filled with beer, and there was greater rejoicing. It was a great pity to object to those who wanted beer having it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dale: May I be allowed The Chairman: No. On a vote being taken three voted for the amendment, ten against, and two members did not vote.</p>
        <p>It was reported that at a meeting of the Padgate Homes Committee the Superintendent's estimate of supplies and ,Christmas extras was approved and the Board confirmed the fiction of the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EXTRA MONEY FOR OUT-RELIEFERS.</head>
        <p>EXTRA MONEY FOR OUT-RELIEFERS.</p>
        <p>At a later stage in the Board meeting the Rev. A. M. Mitchell moved: "That the extra Christ- mas allowance to out-reliefers granted last year. be renewed this Christmas, viz., adults Is. 6d., and children ninepence," and said that one reason why the allowance should be made again was that the winter had commenced early and promised to be a very severe one. Further than that, there were so many demands that some of them had never got their hands out of their pockets. He thought the Board would not object to the proposal. Mr. Seaton : It is a great pleasure to the poor people in Newton. I second the proposition. Mr. Dale supported, and said that he thought the allowance was properly spent. The out- reliefers required assistance as much as those in the House, for many of them would have been in the House if they had yielded to the pressure which had been brought to bear upon them. _</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I opposed . this for .several years, but after the experience of last year I shall not do so. Mr. Billinge : Give the Vicar a chance this time. (Laughter.) The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WAR LOAN.</head>
        <p>WAR LOAN.</p>
        <p>An advertisement appears in to-day's "Ex- aminer" which will interest investors of all classes. Those who cannot go to the front, but are willing to help with money, have now an op- portunity of contributing to the success of the Allies and at the same time of getting a very good rate of interest for themselves on the best security in the world. The amount asked for is et 350,000,000, at a price which yields 4 per cent. to the investor. If necessary the lists will be kept open until next Tuesday, but those who want to make sure of allotments will go well advised to apply long before then. Any of the banks, money order offices, or principal stock- brokers, will supply the necessary forms. The success of the War Loan is a foregone conclusion, .great enthusiasm having been shown by applicants all over the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>DRINK IN WAR 11ME.</head>
        <p>DRINK IN WAR 11ME.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden and the Magistrates</p>
        <p>and Watch Committee.</p>
        <p>PAINFUL STREET SCENES.</p>
        <p>At the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening the Rev. H. Ogden delivered a pointed sermon upon " War and the Public- house," taking for his text Acts xix. 28: "Great is Diana of the Ephesians."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SELF-PRESERVATION.</head>
        <p>SELF-PRESERVATION.</p>
        <p>Self-preservation, said the preacher, was the first law of human life. The natural instinct tended to the safety of number one, and was extremely pronounced in the male creature. Drummond, in his " Ascent of Man," said there was another law, warring in their members, which was concerned for the preservation of others. The maternal instinct for the preserva- tion of the race was an absolute necessity, but unfortunately the law for the preservation of self worked in all the schemes and affairs of men. A dog would preserve a shocking collection of bones, burying a hideous heap and gorging himself until his stomach resented more. Man might not gorge his digestion in the same sense, but yet would spoil his appetite for the best things, by his concentration on his own desires and passions and fortunes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MORAL DANGER.</head>
        <p>A MORAL DANGER.</p>
        <p>But the public-house was more than a dan- gerous industry; it was a moral danger, and a moral danger, without the question of bread and butter, immediately bound up with it. He would not quote the utterances of any ministers or clergymen that night, for the lay-mind com- monly regarded the ministerial mind as suffer- ing from pious delusions. But such an unimpeachable world-preacher as Tolstoy would better serve their end; and, in one of his letters, lie said: "When a man is sober he is ashamed to piirsue an immoral life; ashamed to steal; ashamed to kill. . . . Thus conscience is deadened by drink." The late surgeon-in- ordinary to Queen Victoria had declared that "alcohol was Satan's chloroform, and that he slayed both body and soul." Judges were rarely fanatics; their utterances were grave and their judgments balanced—as became a judge. Lord Coleridge, in charging the Grand Jury at Swan- sea, took a grave view of the nature of the . calendar of charges before him. A hundred prisoners and an "excessive proportion of sexual character is enough to impress any : judge," he said; and declared that he was al- ' most tired of expressing his opinion that the greater part of the offences were due to drink. In a great many cases the criminal had been supplied with drink in disregard of that section of the licensing laws which said that drink must not be supplied to a person under the influence of drink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MILITARY AUTHORITIES AND DRINK.</head>
        <p>MILITARY AUTHORITIES AND DRINK.</p>
        <p>The noble Judge had urged the local author- ities to do their duty fearlessly in the preven- tion of crime, and his utterances were a delight- ful commentary upon the Watch , Committee appointments in their own town. Public bodies were not always tactful, and the present time was not a normal time. They needed more strictures upon the public-house in times of peace; in times of war they needed a stiff, stern hand. The soldier and the sailor had to be as fit as any conditions could make them. Lord Fisher had no delusions about war; to him it was pitilessly cruel. That was his method of conducting it, and his instruments and agents had to be fit. Lord Kitchener had pub- lished and re-published his famous order—a German soldier carried his copy of Nietzsche in his knapsack; a British soldier carried his commander's desire for him to forsake drink. Lord Roberts appealed, whilst living, for tem- perance; surely his voice would haunt them from the grave ! Sir John Jellicoe had spoken from the sea, and believed that coffee was more efficient than rum on the quarter-deck and in the fo'castle. Their military and naval leaders were anxious that the men should be St, and fit all the time; and one great agent they believed to be total abstinence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>IN THE WARRINGTON STREETS.</head>
        <p>IN THE WARRINGTON STREETS.</p>
        <p>Sir Thomas Barlow pointed out two solutions of the problem—restriction of hours and amel- ioration of conditions. They must, he said, make the local authorities use their- powers, and purify the Watch Committees. These, too often, were made up partly of men interested in the liquor traffic. A good Watch Committee could do an immense amount with the powers they had.</p>
        <p>These words were not the utterances of a temperance fanatic, but of the King's physician! The words of Sir Thomas Barlow had been quoted just to emphasise what' could be seen in local streets any night. With more sadness than words could express, one had seen men in His Majesty's uniform leaping and dancing as though mad—rolling along the highway, a menace to themselves and a danger to the pub- lic. One had noticed four pals, with a good deal of chivalry, conducting a helpless pal into the barracks. If a casual observer could see 1 that, what would a systematised search reveal? Patriotism was a word on everybody's lips just then, but was it patriotic to supply drink to a soldier until he was helplessly drunk? Kitch- ener, Roberts and Jellicoe had urged the nation to assist in keeping a sober Army; and the friend who " treated " a soldier already in a state of drunkenness was as bad as a spy. He certainly was a foe in disguise! But what of a trade that was collecting war funds, and assisting in their distribution to the needy, and yet thwarting the wishes of the military leaders? There was certainly a strange irony in their succouring soldiers and sailors, and yet being the head and forefront of a trade that was rendering the soldiers and sailors so unfit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"PASSING STRANGE."</head>
        <p>"PASSING STRANGE."</p>
        <p>It was passing strange that no action had been taken to close public-houses earlier in the town. Sheffield, which was no worse than War- rington from the drink point of view, had decided that no woman should be served with drink before 11.30 in the morning, and that liquor should not he sold after 9.30 in the even- ing, even to bona-fide travellers. It had even taken action, according to report, to make the law applicable to clubs. What was good for Sheffield could not be bad for Warrington. The Chief Constable" had been petitioned by the Anglicans, Roman Catholics and Free Church- men. Their leading representatives had laid their petitions before the Chief; yet, whatever the reason, nothing had been done. One deeply deplored that so beloved a personality as their Mayor could see no reason why the hours of sale for drink should be reduced. The streets, the interior and the exterior of the public- houses were surely the best evidence ,that they needed action—and immediate action!</p>
        <p>"TONE" OF THE COUNCIL. But evidently the tone of Warrington's Coun- cil was revealed in their action over the Watch Committee. A councillor of immense value had been displaced, and a seat found on the com- mittee for a licensee. When every progressive town was curtailing the power of the trade, in this borough it must have more room and more power. The Socialists' gibe at the Churches Fag too pointed not to find the weak- ness of their armour. Were Anglicans, Roman Catholics and Free Churchmen not only to write in concert, but also to act in concert, no one would dare to oppose the restriction of the licensing hours. Might the enemies of religion be for .once confounded, and see the churches acting for the rights and benefits of a common humanity!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON JUSTICES' ACTION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUSTICES' ACTION.</p>
        <p>Vicar of Burtonwood's Lament.</p>
        <p>Writing in the current issue of his Parish Magazine, the Vicar of Burtonwood (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) says The Temporary Re- striction (Licensing) ,Bill of August last has practically been nullified in its effects by the provision that the assent and recommendation of the Chief of Police is necessary for the ap- plication of the measure to any particular locality—' and experience,' says a well-known Temperance reformer, proves that the police do not as readily recommend as they 'might, but in some cases have thwarted the local Licensing Committee in their desire to take advantage of the Act.' The Police Chief has proved the crux in a number of districts, but this cannot y said of Warrington, where the magisteria Bench point-blank refuses to curtail the *ll. ities for the sale of intoxicating liquors. Oh, ei Warrington, when wilt thou learn to tread the pathway of sobriety:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WAR FUNDS.</head>
        <p>WAR FUNDS.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. WAR REFUGEES' FUND. s. d.‘ Employes Richmond Gas Stove 4 , Meter Co. Ltd. Draw for Belgian Pears, per George Miller l7 0 0 J. S. Earle, "Brooklands," Result of Draw........... Mrs. Eliz. Pidduck, Miss Mary Green- ing, and the Women's Co-operative Guild Social (~t5 each) Proceeds of Sale of Celery given b4r Mr. Wm. Dandy - Proceeds of Draw for a Doll, per M 4.8. Sheath and Mrs. Fleming Pupils Secondary School, 3rd c0n...1 Henry Collin, " 8.N.W., Per Mr . Fulton-Smith," and "Nemo Funcç (...t1 Is. each) Mrs. James Fairclough J. P. Buck F. Bate and Employes, and H. A. Sii- cock (10s. each) lEmployes Harry Rogers, Ist con. Mrs. E. Atkinson Children's Lantern Lecture on Bel- gium, per Rev. H. Ogden</p>
