      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>DAILY NEED.</head>
        <p>A DAILY NEED.</p>
        <p>after dusk to travel with blinds closely drawn. Those of us who have noticed the effect of this new regulation have been struck by the com- pleteness with which it ensures the invisibility of a moving train the occupants of which have observed it to the letter. Unfortunately, how- ever, this has as yet been achieved but rarely. Regular travellers naturally co-operate with the officials in all such matters and things without question or difficulty; but there are others who are a constant cause of anxiety and troublo owing either to ignorance, carelessness, or down- 'right stupidity, and so, while as a rule the order as to drawing the blinds after dark is being obeyed by most with commendable loyalty and intelligence, there are isolated cases in which it is persistently disregarded, notwith- standing the vigorous remonstrances of station officials and the appeals of guards afraid of being reported. Some of these offenders, who act as if they considered their judgment supe- rior to that of the authorities issuing the in- structions, might possibly change their defiant attitude if they could be shown that otherwise they may personally suffer. Well, they of all in the darkened train offer a mark for any irresponsible maniac who might yield to a" sud- den impulse to take pot-shots at the carriages. I Enough said!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>ETERNAL CITY.</head>
        <p>THE ETERNAL CITY.</p>
        <p>Popular Lecture on Rome by a Popular Lecturer.</p>
        <p>BLED TO DEATH BY WAR.</p>
        <p>Yet another winter, Warrington is having the privilege of a series of popular lectures by Mr. J. E. Phythian—already so well known in the town. This year's course, which is again given under the auspices of the Museum Committee, deals with "Rome, the Eternal City: Its Story and its Monuments," and all the lectures are to be illustrated with excellent lantern slides.</p>
        <p>The first of the series—" The Making of Rome and Classical Rome "—was delivered in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Saturday even- ing. Mr. John Upson presided over a large audience; and, in a brief speech, stated that a list of library books dealing with Rome had been made out, and the committee would be ade- quately rewarded if they found them in de- mand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>AIM OF THE tECTURES.</head>
        <p>AIM OF THE tECTURES.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said he had given two series of lectures upon foreign cities before in Warring- ton, viz., on Florence and Venice. The present course had been arranged before the start of the war, and he was glad that he had not been asked to change the subject to one more closely con- nected with the war; for there were some things, in this war, which could only be under- stood if they knew the history of Rome. In these-lectures he would try to say briefly what were the significant achievements of Rome. and so find out, something of the character of the men who played a part in them. To get a know- ledge of what was done in those days, was to get a clue to what was going on to-day. Nobody knew when Rome began to be: lie had seen there the bodies of men who died at least 1000 ir.c.—a fact which could be ascertained from the rate at which soil accumulated. The Romans, themselves thought it was' 753 B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Supreme Consideration.</head>
        <p>The Supreme Consideration.</p>
        <p>After briefly outlining the old legends as to the foundation of the city, the lecturer went on to speak of its ancient religion. Many virtues, lie said, marked the Romans when they were really at their best. The individual, for in- stance, had to be prepared to sacrifice himself for the good of the community. "None was for a party, But all were for the State." And they not,only had to have all those virtues which might be of service to, them in this re- spect, but they also had to be unsurpassedly good to win the favour of the gods. All this was the real Roman religion, and he believed that they of the present day could do with more of that spirit among them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>More About World-Power.</head>
        <p>More About World-Power.</p>
        <p>The Romans came to think that they were the proper people to rule the whole world. There was a person somewhere on the Conti- nent of Europe—perhaps they would be angry if lie called him a gentleman—(laughter)—who seemed to have the same idea to-day! But the Romans did keep the world in order; and tley ruled it well, for they allowed people to retain their beliefs, and only persecuted the Christians because they did not think them good citizens. Britain had followed the same policy of tolera- tion with regard to the Mohammedans in India, who would not join their co-religionists in Tur- key because they knew their religious prac- tices were safe in the British Empire.</p>
        <p>Ca Jeer's Tribute to the Belgians. Describing Ciesar's conquest of Gaul. the lec- turer pointed out that this great soldier-em- peror said the Belgians were the bravest people he had ever had to face. (Applause.) But the Roman Empire fell ! One of the reasons for its decay was undoubtedly the fact that so many of its young men lost their lives in the wars. A war like the present one, he thought, might enfeeble the life of to-day, for in it were perishing many of Europe's finest young men. It had been stated that the French were small because thousands of their finest men were lost in the Napoleonic wars. - That was so in the case of Rome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Forum.</head>
        <p>The Forum.</p>
        <p>A large number of fine illustrations were thrown on the screen, dealing especially with the arrangement of the great city and its beauti- ful architecture. Referring to a picture of the Forum, Mr. Phythian said that be had stood before these ruilig; and felt that building to have been the milSt wonderful tribute to the P°lver, of the Romans.</p>
        <p>Why Britain Rules the Waves.' An officer a the cruiser Undaunted told his friends of his experiences in a recent fight with German destroyers. He was asleep when the Germans were first seen, having been on duty just previous, and his servant knocked at the door and said: ."‘"Beg pardon, sir, four German destroyers have just been sighted. Will you take your bath before or after the action?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Respecting Aldermen. Certain things said in the vourse of last week's Town Council meeting set me thinking of piti- able displays of human vanity and childishness which I have witnessed in k,onnection with elections of aldermen in various parts of Lan- cashire and Yorkshire. Petty jealousies and heartburnings constantly arise out of such pro- cePlings, and in some cases result in bitter ,uds amounting almost to a sort of vendetta. in one place that I have particularly in mind as I write, the "balance of power" was sum that members of the political party having the slight preponderance, when aspiring to vacant seats on the Aldermanic Bench, were reduced to the humiliating expedient of voting for them- selves, and wondering onlookers were treated to many a sorry exhibition. From what I have ' heard from time to time, that sort of thing has been common enough in different parts of the country. Speaking in the Warrington Chamber on the occasion to which I referred at the out- set, Alderman Tinnion observed, with marked disapproval, that it had been "urged that these honours ought to go round." That is precisely what it came to in one borough where I lived some time. The situation had become so scan- dalous that at last it was arranged that as aldermanships became available, councillors should be elevated automatically according to seniority. The effect may well be imagined. Fancy a big firm promoting their clerks or other employes, not by individual merit, but by a cast-iron rule of that kind! Na, na; it winna dae.</p>
        <p>Origin of "Hip, hip—." At the Mayoral luncheon the principal toast was received with musical honours and cheers for the Mayor and the Mayoress. _The explan- ation of the term, "Hip, hip, hurrah !" by-thee bye, has nothing to do with the anatomy. Some time ago a writer in an American paper sent to me by a relative in New York gave it thus : During the stirring times gf the Crusades, the chivalry of Europe was excited to arms by the inflammatory appeals of Peter the Hermit. And while he was preaching the Crusade, this furious zealot was accustomed to exhibit a banner emblazoned with the letters H. E. P., the initials of the Latin words Hierosolyma eat perdita (Jerusalem is destroyed). The people in some of the countries that he visited, not being acquainted with Latin, read and pro- nounced the inscription as one word, "Rep." The followers of the Hermit were accustomed, whenever an unfortunate Jew appeared in the streets, to raise the cry, "Rep, liep, hurrah!" to hunt him down and flash their swords on the defenceless Israelite in token that by his death was typified the result which they desired more than to be charitable. Along with this I set the following from "Chambers' Twentieth Century Dictionary" : "Hip, hip: a cry said to come down from the Crusaders' time, often the cry of the mob in an outrage on the Jews—more probably an abbre- viation of Hebrew than formed from the initials of Hierosolyma est perdita : Jerusalem is destroyed." The conclusion is that the modern exclama- tion, corrupted in its vowel, suggests both Jew I persecution and a bit of Middle Ages fanaticism. Concerning Toasts. To be sure, we have nothing of this sort in mind when, sometimes uproariously and per- haps not very melodiously, but always heartily and sincerely, we unite in chanting "He's a jolly good fellow," and, after formally chal- lenging ,denial which is never forthcoming and is of course no more expected than a forbidding of banns, we proceed to shout ourselves hoarse in the terms quoted in the preceding para- graph. Moreover, we are no longer cynical in our toasts. Many of those that have come down to us from the so-called "good old times," which were really bad old times, are of a de- cidedly cynical character. On a previous occa- sion I gave some examples affecting the ladies which, fair on the surface, had really a bitter sting at the end where the sting is usually to be found. These old toasts, generally put in the form of doggerel, convey the impression of scepticism as to friendship, treating it as a rare jewel and the world as shallow. Even the pretty well-known one "To contentment" is not altogether free from cynicism— "No one bulldog yet could eat Every other bulldog's meat; If you have a good-sized bone, Let the other dog alone." This might be commended to the Kaiser, eh? The Scaremongers. As specimens of this woebegone class have come under my notice, I have referred to their dismal prophecies for my readers' entertain- ment. I have discovered another this week. After the manner of the Fat Boy in "Pickwick," he has been trying to make timid ladies' flesh creep by declaring with emphasis his convic- tion that the Germans will presently be in our very midst. He has made up his mind that they are on the point of invading Englfind by landing their fighting men on the Yorkshire Coast, and that they will march thence to Manchester first thing. one who had appar- ently been impressed by the wondrous tale re- peated it to mile and invited an expression of opinion upon it. I suggested that the alarmist How can the Germans, should be asked—a) already pressed to the limit to their resources, afford to detach from their armies the men and equipment that would be needed for such an enterprise? (2) Even supposing they could spare these, how could they transport them and effect a landing on our shores without first dispcmin„ of our Fleet? When these two questions have been answered there are certain others in re- senre to the benefit of which my perhaps moreperplexed than actually frightened young friend will be welcome.</p>
        <p>Precautionary Measures. I take it that a raid by airship is all we have to fear, and that it is for this reason that the railway companies are requiring passengers</p>
        <p>110 ER FECT LY healthy children are generally happy; but a very slight stomach trouble is often enough to make them miserable. That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no poison or narcotic.</p>
        <p>fla.t7</p>
        <p>• I</p>
        <p>i-.7f'::-;,-'''.: 4.,.,.:-.';'''.-,'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>TILE</head>
        <p>TILE</p>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Costly Building Scheme Despite the</p>
        <p>War.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley presiding.</p>
        <p>NEW CATHOLIC INSTRUCTOR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Padgate Homes Commit- tee it was reported that Father Walmsley, the Roman Catholic instructor at the Homes, was leaving the district for St. Andrew's, in Scot- land, and it was decided that, subject to the consent of the Roman Catholic Bishop, the Rev. Father Miller be appointed his successor, at a salary of £lO per annum. It was also recommended that the Superin4 tendent be authorised to engage temporary assistance on the teaching staff whilst Mr. Moly- neux was awv with the South Lancashire Regi- ment.</p>
        <p>At the same meeting the Superintendent was instructed to prepare a report showing the num- ber of attendances made to the Homes by the Rev. Ir F. S. Collier since his appointment as chaplain, and also the number of attendances made by the Roman Catholic Instructor during the same period. It was further recommended by the commit- tee that the whole question of the provision of accommodation for children between the -ages of three and five years at the Homes be referred to the original sub-committee appointed to con- sider the Local Government Board Inspector's report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>LETTER OF CHEER FOR A SOLDIERSERVANT.</head>
        <p>LETTER OF CHEER FOR A SOLDIER- SERVANT.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent said they ought to thank Mr. Molyneux for his patriotism in joining the forces, and she would like them to send a mes- sage from the Board to the effect that they were pleased he was serving his country. Mr. Mather: That is all very well, but there are thousands and thousands who do not get thanks from anyone. He is only doing his duty. The Chairman: Expression of approval is sufficient without a formal vote. Miss Broadbent: That is what I meant. I only •brought it forward because he was one of our officers.</p>
        <p>_ Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It is a great encourage- ment to have a message of that sort, and we ought to send it.</p>
        <p>ISiiss Broadbent: They ought to get encour- agement and thanks for going. They make great sacrifices.</p>
        <p>It Was decided to send a letter.</p>
        <p>"THOUSANDS OF POUNDS ON A SCHOOL</p>
        <p>FOR TWENTY."</p>
        <p>. . Mr. Mather referred to the recommendation with regard to the building accommodation, and said that, in view of the increased prices of materials and the finances of the country, the Board ought to consider whether it was worth while spending thousands of pounds on a school to accommodate twenty children. - The Chairman said he would move that the sub-committee be constituted a committee of the Board to save time in the matter. The Guardians had accepted the scheme and it must be borne in mind that they would not be able to have the children of three and five years in .the present schools after March 31st. The idea of his motion was that the whole thing should be got through on economical lines and without delay. Mr. Brooks seconded and said the new build- ing was only part of the scheme, for there were other things, affecting the administration of the institution to be considered.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said it was because of the Chairman's suggestion that the matter was referred to a sub-committee. A better plan would be to get the sub-committee to report at a special meeting of the Schools Attendance Committee. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>POSTPONEMENT WOULD BE ADVANTAGEOUS. ,</head>
        <p>POSTPONEMENT WOULD BE ADVAN- TAGEOUS. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Billinge : It would be advantageous all round if anything could be 'deferred. . _</p>
        <p>INA% A. M. Mitchell:, The sub-committee could report to the next meeting. _ . _</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The report ought to be pre- sented this morning if the committee had acted as they should. Mr. Bent: We shall not be doing any build- ing this year. The Chairman: We have not got any plans, and the committee were asked to get them. Mr. Bent thought they should be as economi- cal as they could. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Bennett: The Chancellor of the Exche- quer has said that public bodies ought not to urge forward any schemes that were unneces- sary for the time being. The Chairmall : But this is necessary! Several members agreed with the Chairman on this point. _</p>
        <p>- Mr. Mather: We could not get the building ready by March, it you shifted Heaven and earth.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I don't think we can, but it will show that we intend business. Hey. A. M. Mitchell: There is no reason to daily about it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley's motion was carried and the minutes of the committee were confirmed.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MASTER SURCHARGED. __</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Local Govern- ment Board auditor had summoned Mr. Brown, the late Master of the Workhouse, to appear ,before him the previous day and pointed out to him the letter which he (the Clerk) had sent claim- ing certain sums for food received by Mr. Brown over and above what he was entitled to, and food for his children, to which they were not entitled. The Auditor informed Mr. Brown that he would be surcharged with the whole sum. Mr. Brown promised to forward a cheque the next day. Mr. Billinge : What was the amount? The Clerk: There was £4 65., made up of £3 2s. for chickens and beer supplied to Mr. Brown, and £1 4s. for calves' foot jelly and eggs supplied to the children.</p>
        <p>KEEPING HIS SEAT OPEN. The Chairman read a letter from Mr. P. Garnett, who has enlisted in the Sportsmen's Battalion, in which he acknowledged the good wishes of the Board. • In answer to Mr. Mather the Chairman ex- plained that the Standing Orders with refer- ence to a member not attending for six months, and the question of his retirement then, left the action at the discretion of the Board. In the present case the Board would be fully satis- fied.</p>
        <p>It was decided, on the proposition of Mr. Mather, to send a letter of encouragement to Mr. Garnett, and assure him, so far as the Guardians were concerned, of his seat when he returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>FOR DESTITUTE ALIENS.</head>
        <p>FOR DESTITUTE ALIENS.</p>
        <p>1 The Local Government Board forwarded a cheque for -4:.22. for the maintenance of desti- !tut° aliens up to September 30th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>AFTER YOU ARE FORTY.</head>
        <p>AFTER YOU ARE FORTY.</p>
        <p>Forty years is middle age, or should be. People who squander their youth are old at forty; everybody needs to give a 'Mile more attention to health and careful living after passing this. fortieth "milestone."</p>
        <p>The reserve of strength is not what it used to be. Nature needs a little help if we are to continue the work and worry of modern life. To' most people, especially those who are at middle age, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are a supporting tonic and give valuable assistance. These Pills make thin blood rich and red; they carry nourishment to weak and inflamed nerves; they increase the appetite and make digestion normal; also they overcome sleep- lessness and most forms of headache. Try them for any trouble caused by thin blood or weak nerves, such as anEemia, rheumatism, neuralgia, sciatica, lumbago, indigestion, sick or nervous headaches, or St. Vitus dance. It is surprising how many diseases are due to thin and impure blood, and how quickly these yield when, by proper treatment, the blood is built up and the starved nerves nourished. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills contain no habit-forming drugs and cannot do harm. Begin Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People to-day; most dealers sell them (accept nothing else); or send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, , 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d.. for six,</p>
        <p>FREE to men readers, The Book of the Nerves: to lady readers, "Plain Talks to Women!! Send postcard for either to Card Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London.</p>
