      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>EXAMINER</p>
        <p>~ SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914</p>
        <p>Furness presided over a fair attendance and again explained. the scheme.</p>
        <p>-Councillor Fair proPO-seci-the following reso- lution: "That in the opinion of this parish meeting a committee, or committees, should be formed at once for collecting money for the Prince of Wales' Fund by means of entertain- ments, hotpot suppers, teas, &amp;c., and, if thought desirable, that collection boxes be put up in the licensed houses in the parish. That the moneys so collected shall be paid into the district war fund account, 15 per cent of it being reserved for any Special purpose which the Parish Com- mittee think fit."</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. R. Jones mentioned what was being done in the parish by difterent organisa- tions for various causes, and said that he thought the district was working very well in- deed. He would like to know if they could not request those organisations to consider the claims of the Prince of Wales' Fund. There was money from Sankey and Penketh Schools which they did not know what to do with. They were all working very well, but not for the Prince of Wales' Fund. Perhaps the committee would endeavour to focus the effort into parish work. He was sure there ought to be collecting boxes in the public-houses and also on the foot- ball fields. He seconded the resolution.</p>
        <p>Why Not Centralise Local War Efforts? Mr. G. Clark gave many instances of how well the children in the school had worked in making garments for the soldiers.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We-Zonlcf-t-aie the respon- sibility on our shoulders as a parish committee, and endeavour to have the various efforts which are being made in different directions centra- lised, so that the whole parish would have the credit for it.</p>
        <p>Representative Committee Appointed. Several members took part in the discussion, some suggesting that the amount reserved for local use from the sum collected should be 25 per cent.</p>
        <p>- Eventually, with the substitution of the figures 20 per cent., instead of 15 per cent., the resolution was carried..</p>
        <p>It was unanimously decided that Mr. Littler be appointed treasurer and Mr. Smith secretary. The following gentlemen were elected on the committee: The Rev. J. R. Jones, and Messrs. F. Newbold, H. Bingham, W. Webb, R. Forster, R. I. Partridge, F. Bradburn, A. Appleton, J. Bardsley, G. Clark and W. Knowles. The mem- bers of the Parish Council, it was further de- cided, will be ex-officio members, and the com- mittee were given power to add to their nunZer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>to I M !STRICT EWS</head>
        <p>to I M !STRICT EWS</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>Prompt and Plucky Rescue.—Captain W. Tindall Bell, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, saved the life of W. Bramwell, who is employed on the extension of Messrs. J. Fairclough and Sons' mill, on Monday. Bramwell fell off the quay into the river Mersey, and was drifting away, when Capt. Bell divested himself of his coat and vest and dived in. He kept Bramwell floating until a boat came along and picked them up.</p>
        <p>Fleeing Before the Germans.—The daughters of Mr. J. H. Fletcher, Holly Dene, Whitefield- road, have received a most interesting letter from a lady who was formerly their companion at Tournai, and who is now at Bethune. The letter recounts many pathetic incidents and stories of refugees leaving their all to escape the Germans. "Many of the poor mothers," says the writer, "were struggling along with their</p>
        <p>little ones, who had died-from- cold and hunger." The privations .of niany of the wounded sol- diers are also vividly described, but the writer is very optimistic in her outlook on the war, although she strikes a pathetic note in. the sentence, "We have not heard from father for over six weeks. I wonder if he is a prisoner still." She hoped, however, that she would be able to return to Lens, where she resided before the war, in a few days. -</p>
        <p>Training Corps Members at Rifle Practice.— The local members of the Preparatory Train- ing Corps attended Appleton Rifle Range on Saturday for instruction. Mr. A. Shepherd, the musketry instructor, was in charge. Ten shots were fired at a six-inch bull target at 100 yards by each member, five with the miniature rifle and five with the service rifle. The possible 'score was 50 in all. The individual scores were: A. Shepherd, 42; C. J. Smith, 41; W. Skelton, 31; E. G. White, 30; J. Richardson, 30; A. Wil- son, 29; H. Turner, 28; J. Swinton, 28; C. E. B. Sutton, 24; R. Amos, 23; H. Newall, 20; A. S. Wright, 19; W. A. Thompson, 17; S. Rustage, 17; J. W. Robertson, 12; F. G. Pidduck, 10; F. H. G. Semmens, 9; J. Perks, 8; A. Philp, 4. In the evening there was a most successful parade, when about 80 members attended.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming War Lecture.—An illustrated, non-party, and educational lecture on "Why we went to war" will be delivered on Friday evening next in the Church Room by Mr. Henry Lancaster (late joint secretary of Lork Kitchener's Recruiting Bureau for East Anglia). Lieut.-Colonel Charles Lyon will preside. The lecture will be interspersed with patilotic and recruiting songs and recitations. There will be a collection in aid of the Belgian Relief Fund. A feature in connection with this lecture is the amicable and harmonious relationship which exists in the parish between the two great poli- tical parties—a relationship which finds expres- sion in the fact that Mr. H. J. Westbrook, the local solicitor and agent of the Conservative party, is collaborating with Mr. S. Albinson, the local agent of the Liberal party. This is surely as it should be at this time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Concert and Conversazione.—On Saturday the members of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church held a very successful concert and conversaziofte. The musical portion of the programme was provided by Mr. Levi Richard- son and a party, each of whom rendered their selections with such effect as to secure an encore. Miss Edith Sherburn sang with sweetness and ability "Bird of love divine," &amp;c. Mr. J. Dakin, who possesses a good bass voice, entertained the audience with "Land of hope and glory" (the choir lustily joining in the chorus), and Mr. Richardson himself, a rich, melodious tenor, secured rounds of applause by his ren- dering of "I gave you a rose" (Wright), "At early dawn of day" (Schumann), and several. old ballads. The humorous recitals of Mr. A. Appleton were cleverly rendered, and enthusi- astically receivedi—Prior to -the interval, a pleasing ceremony took place in the presenta- tion to Mrs. Dodds of a framed photograph of tlie organ she has generously given to the church, also of a literary souvenir to Miss Payne in recognition of her earning, for the extension funds, the sum of £3 from a modest penny.—The chair was occupied by the Rev. H. T. Dixon in his usual genial manner, and the ladies of the church helped the funds by the free provision of the refreshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>FOR THE PRINCE OF WALES' FUND. Committee Appointed to Organise Local Efforts.</head>
        <p>FOR THE PRINCE OF WALES' FUND. Committee Appointed to Organise Local Efforts.</p>
        <p>The people of Sankey have already given tangible proof of their admiration of our gal- lant soldiers, and of their sympathy for the dis- tressed refugees from Belgium by raiWrtig funds to provide the former with those little comforts that do so much to soften the rigours of cam- paigning,and by helping to feed,clothe and shelter the latter, and now they are turning their atten- tion to swelling the coffers of - the Prince of Wales' Fund. With the object of forming a committee to organise social functions, &amp;c., the proceeds of which shall go to the fund, a special meeting of the Parish Council and a parish meeting were held in the Council Schools on Monday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Chairman Suggests Ways a-nd _Means.</head>
        <p>Chairman Suggests Ways a-nd _Means.</p>
        <p>The Council meeting was presided over by Mr. W. Furness, who said that some time ago he received a letter from the Lord Lieutenant for Lancashire asking what Sankey was doing as a district with the object of organising for getting money for the war fund. He replied that the Rural District Committee had no authority for organising the various parishes, but suggested that the Lord Lieutenant should get the Preston authorities to suggest to 'other committees the advisability of working up Par- ishes, so that each parish could do its level best and get the credit for what was done. He (the chairman) thought that by such means they would get the bet results. That had been suggested to the County Committee, and the Rural District Committee had adopted the sug- gestion and approached the parish councils with the object of getting them to call parish meet- ings and arrange for the organisation of small committees, who would adopt ways and means for raising money for the Prince of Wales' Fund. Teas, hotpot suppers and entertain- ments could be held, and a dozen different things could be clime to get money, without having to take the hat round.</p>
        <p>Part of the Money for Local Purposes. Councillor Fair: I think you will get a lot of money that way. Are the proceeds going to the Prince of Wales' Fund, or can we create a local fund?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I suggest that 15 per cent. of the money sent be ear-marked by us for our own committee's special objects and purposes. Councillor Fair: I think there will be a great many objections to the Prince of Wales' Fund. The Chairman said that was a national relief fund, and whilselocally they had had no occa- sion to draw on the fund, there had been enor- mous calls. made upon it from all parts of [the country. There were no people out of employ- ment in Sankey, and the whole of the district was in a healthy condition from the employ- ment standpoint. That was a matter for, con- gratulation. (Hear; hear.) Mr. Smith: It may not remain like that. If the war goes on, say, for two years, we shall have to .draw on the fund. - -</p>
        <p>Councillor Fair Ti quite agree with the 15 per cent. being deducted.</p>
        <p>Christmas Tea for " Tommy " Suggested.</p>
        <p>The Chairman. if our local contingent have a holiday at Christmas we might give them a tea out of that 15 per cent., or else help in the finding of materials for ladies' working parties. There are many channels into which we could divert the money. Mr. Littler, he added, had consented to act as the local treasurer, and Mr. J. Smith was willing to take over the duties of secretary. He had confidence in Sankey, and the public spirit which permeated Sankey, that when occasion required the people would not ! be found wanting either in providing the where- withal, or the time and trouble, to assist all object of the character mentioned.</p>
        <p>Hdtpots and Cups of Tea. At the subsequent parish meeting Councillor</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—The magistrates at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday were Vice-Chan- cellor Stewart Smith (in the chair). and liressrs. S. Davies, John Coxon, S. W. Gould, H. Holden, T. H. Warburton, and T. J. Ridgway. Yuletide and the Shops.—lt has-been decided that the shops in Lymm will be open all day on the Wednesday in Christmas week, and will close for Christmas Day and Boxing Day. There will be the usual half-holiday on the Wednes- day previous to New Year's Day, whilst the first de: ol 1915 will be regarded as a full holi- day.</p>
        <p>Citizen Sunday.—To-morrow will be observed as Citizens' Sunday at Lymm. A special ser- vice will be held in the Parish Church in the afternoon, and will be attended by members of the Urban District Council, the Tradesmen's Association, and representatives of various local societies. A collection will be made in aid of the District Nurse's Fund.</p>
        <p>Missionary Sermons and Meeting.—Missionary sermons were preached at the Broomedge Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday by the Rev. R. W. Ferguson, of Lymm. On Wednes- day evening a public meeting was held, when Mr. Ferguson gave the local report, which was of a most encouraging character. Mr. A. B. Edwards, of Lymm presided, and the Rev. G. R. Southall was the principal speaker.</p>
        <p>Why the Case was Dismissed.—At the Lymni Petty Sessions on Monday a letter was read from the father of a defendant against whom a summons had been issued for failing to ha v—e a red rear light attached to his car whilst in Warrington-road, Lymm, at 10.30 p.m. on Octo- ber 18th. The letter stated that defendant was serving in the 6th Battalion of the Manchester Territorials, and upon Supt. Sutherland stat- ing that he did not wish to press the case, the summons was dismissed.</p>
        <p>Lymm Wednesday F.C.'s Defeat.—The Lymm Wednesday F.C. were defeated on their own ground this week in a Manchester and District Wednesday League engagement by Pendlebury and Swinton F.C. In the first half of the game the home team were unable to break through, but the visitors, showing better form, netted a couple of goals, an advantage they held ;A the interval. On changing ends the local team put plenty of vigour into their attacks u the visitors' citadel, with the result that 11. Jackson and F. Guest equalised the scores. Afterwards the game was even more keenly contested, and it was lot until the last few minutes that a penalty to the visitors enabled them to secure the lead and run out winners by three goals to two.--Next Wednesday the Lymm eleven are due to play the Manchester P. 0., on the latter's ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>FIVE THOUGHTLESS BOYS.</head>
        <p>FIVE THOUGHTLESS BOYS.</p>
        <p>-- Fined for Throwing Stones at Tele- _ _</p>
        <p>graph Foles-:</p>
        <p>The small fine which the magistrates at a Juvenile Court, sitting at Lymm on Thursday morning, imposed upon five youthful defend- ants, who were summoned for breaking insula- tors on telegraph poles, must not be taken as indicating the seriousness of the offence. The evidence given to the magistrates (Mr. Alfred Watkin and Mr. J. Holden) showed that six lads went out blackberrying und later corn- menced to throw stones at the telegraph poles which run along the towing-path of the canal at Lymm. Through their acrioa a number of insulators were broken, art' it was !Anted out that in consequence ther3 was the dwnger of important messages being held up. Two of the, boys pleaded guilty and the remainder "not guilty."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Might Have Delayed War Office Messagss.</head>
        <p>Might Have Delayed War Office Messagss.</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Robinson said three of the boys admitted the offence when he called upon them and incriminated the others. All tne boys were of respectable parentage:, • The mothers of some of the boys told the Bench that their lads had been thrashed, whilst others remarked that their sons " had had a good talking to." The Chairman, in imposing a fine of Is. each upon five of the defendants and discharging the sixth, who was only nine years of age, said that the boys had heard of the danger of doing that sort of thing, especially at the present time, when war was in progress. It was possible tllat owing to damage done.by the throwing of stones a message on War Office matters might not be able to be transmitted. That kind of thing must be stopped, and if any of the defendants were brought up again for a similar offence,. or if any other boys were charged with throwing stones at the telegraph poles, the unisment would not be so light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>DE 1R DRINKS.</head>
        <p>DE 1R DRINKS.</p>
        <p>T‘%o Male-lid i Travellers Fined.</p>
        <p>Two casa in which men falsely stated that they were bona-fide travellers in order to obtain refreshments from public:houses during pro- hibited hours, were heard at the.Lymin- Petty Sessions on Wednesday, by Vice-Chancellor Stewart Smith and other magistrates. In the first case taken, Frederick Lockyer, licensee of the Star Inn, Statham, summoned James Bate, a labourer, who resides in Thel- wall-lane, Latchford, for falselY representing himself to be a bona-fide traveller and thereby obtaining intoxicating liquor during prohibited hours on October 23rd. Defendant was also summoned by the police for being found on licensed premises during prohibited hours, and</p>
        <p>for giving a false address. Defendant pleaded guilty to all three offences.</p>
        <p>Landlord_ Says, "Make An _Example."</p>
        <p>Frederick LoclZyer said the defendant visited his premises at 11.45 a.m. on Sunday, October 25th. Witness asked him where he came from, and he replied "Warrington." As the whole of that town is outside the three-mile limit, wit- ness was satisfied that defendant was a bona- fide traveller.</p>
        <p>Witness asked the magistrates to make an ex- ample of the defendant in that case, pointing out that it was hard lines on licensees that they should be subjected to such trouble. They tried their best to carry on their businesses in a proper manner, and he thought a stop should be put to the practice of men making false representations in order to get drink. He asked for his expenses..</p>
        <p>Sergt. Davies- said that, in company with Acting-Sergeant Robinson, he visited the Star Inn on the morning of October 25th, and found several men on the premises, and took their names and addresses. Defendant gave his name as "James T. Bate," and his address as Buck- ley's-yard, Warrington. This information was found to be incorrect, and it was discovered that defendant was really living within two and a half miles of the hotel.</p>
        <p>Defendant, who had nothing to say, was fined ss. on each of the police summonses, and is. and costs on the licensee's summons. The Chairman said that the Bench desired that for the future in cases where the licensee took out the summons first, the police should not take out another summons, or vice-versa. There ought to be. one summons.</p>
        <p>gupt. Sutherland stated that the police were the first to take out a summons in this case.</p>
        <p>RIGHT _NAME-WRONG ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The second case was against Arthur Ford, a foreman painter, of 15, Rock-road, Latchford, who was summoned by Benjamin George Little, licensee of the Pickering Arms, Thelwall, for making a similar false representation. The police had also taken out summonses against defendant for being found on licensed premises during prohibited hours on October 25th, and for giving a false address. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, Warrington, appeared on behalf of the licensee, who, he said, kept a book in which he made every per- son who visited the house during prohibited hours sign his name and address. Mr. Little asked defendant where he came from, and re- ceived the reply, "Longford-street, Warring- ton," which was a long way over the three-mile limit, and the entry defendant made in the book in the presence of the licensee, was "A. Ford, 17, Longford-street, Warrington." When the police visited the premises defendant again said that he lived in Longford-street, but inquiries showed that he came from Rock-roaT, which was considerably under three miles away. Con- duct Jike defendants endangered licensees. Even after Mr. Little had gone to the extreme trouble of making everybody sign their names and addresses this defendant gave a false ad- dress.</p>
        <p>Benjamin George Little gave evidence bear- ing out his solicitor's statement. Sergt. Davies said that defendant told him that he had written his correct address in the book. However, from inquiries, it was found that defendant did not live in Longford-street, but in a house which was just two miles away. Defendant said that he was taking his eldest boy for a walk on the Sunday morning in ques- tion, and could have called at houses which were over the three-mile limit.</p>
        <p>Defendant was fined ss. on each of the police summonses and is. and costs on the licensee's summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>PENK ETH.</head>
        <p>PENK ETH.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The Wesley Guild held a de- votional meeting in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Tuesday, when short papers were read by the lady members, and Miss Johnson sang two solos. Mrs. Gunnell presided.</p>
        <p>Tannery Bandsmen's Success.—Penketh Tan- nery Band Quartet competed last Saturday in Messrs. Rushworth and Dreaper's Fifth Annual Quartet Contest for Brass Instruments, held in Rushworth Hall, Liverpool. Sixteen sets com- peted for the prizes offered, one being a silver challenge shield. The adjudicator, Mr. Wil- liam Halliwell, of Wigan, commented upon the keenness of the competition, and also upon the high standard of the playing. The result was as follows: 1, Skelmersdale Old; 2, Litherland; 3, Penketh Tannery; 4, Gossage's Soap Works. Messrs. H. Carman, H. Glover, A. Bowyer and D. Massey were the Penketh instrumentalists.</p>
        <p>From Waifs to Warriors.—On Monday even- ing at the Friends' School a lectuie on "Dr. Barnardo's Boys in Canada" was given by Mr.l W. Hind Smith. The lecturer first spoke of Dr. Barnardo's life, work and death, and then went on to describe the aetion taken by the boys during the present grave national crisis. The majority of those in the homes, the lecturer said, were too small for military service, but their brass band had been placed at the disposal of the War Office, and had paraded the London streets with the idea of helping further recruit- ing. The band employed a motor 'bus adorned with two striking placards bearing the words, "Free Trips to Berlin and Back, with Plenty to Eat Both Ways." Those lads who were old enough had joined the Army, and Lord Kitchener had been asked if boy buglers were required. Upon an affirmative reply being re- ceived, sixteen of the lads from the homes offered their services and were accepted. A number of Boy Scouts from the homes had also been placed at the disposal of Scotland Yard. Out of 3,000 Canadians who had left the "Land of the Maple Leaf," 400 were Dr. Barnardo's old boys. One of them called at the home in Ply- mouth to say that he was forwarding £1 per month out of his pay towards the home funds,</p>
      </div>
