      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>GOOD BYE TO THE LANCASHIRE CUP.</head>
        <p>GOOD BYE TO THE LANCASHIRE CUP.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>Wa,, • itllo,4lngton said "Good-bye" on Saturday to tea anees of winning the Lancashire Cup this t11:-11, being knocked out in the semi-final of tnateentupetition by Rochdale Hornets. The tzeoetfahtlz a decidedly interesting one from the Point of view, and the big woe tujr, eund their way to Wilderspool thoroughly )311t-Yed everything about, it—except the result., et tl as local supporteils were chagrined le d t Nf defeat of their champions, they o "8 that, judged on the day's play, the kid 'earn won. It was a match of busty Dia °DPortunism, rather than a high-class dis- t°to of Rugby football, but what boots that, 41,1 kg as the excitement was kept up to con- liteg?Pitell throughout the whole eighty min- iorB Among the throng of interested specta- tow eeenPYing prominent positions on the front t 4 B,.ef the grandstand were a party of wounded Rtish h, and Belgian soldiers, who watched the —e with evident relish from start to finish. FIRST BLOOD TO THE VISITORS. The 48t Hornets opened in promising style, the itleio few minutes yielding numerous exciting th,4"ents, arising mainly from hesitancy on I),a:it of those players entrusted with clear- lio,„Niehs. The struggle was carried into the tipur,e half, and the defenders were earry kr-111.,t° give proof of their mettle. They proved q•,..,"in nonce to be thoroughly alive to all that toenuired of them, and presently set their it) arcis on the attack, when Jolley tried hard tra:4lProve from a mark, but had no luck. It 0t until the game had been in progress ilia'tcY minutes that the first points came, fall- 'e visitors' share. Two of the Warring- "ll'eectuarters mis-timed a clearing kick by eNt , and Carter and English being on the theih att the psychological moment, between tve—,..lianded the ball on to Prudence, who got ki4 ine line in tricky fashion. Jones took the and added the goal points.</p>
        <p>IN ARREARS AT HALF-TIME. the iLing ensuing play in the neighbourhood of teje,alf-way line Bowers Was ordered off the thq "IT'cir, a foul charge so near the touch-line ij ley, the victim of the charge in clues- 'B banged into the touch-judge, who rvas ieta:d over like a. ninepin. The sentence of tY expulsion met with a mixed recep- liii the Warrington supporters applauding, 8t the men from Rochdale were anything 13tr Pleased. Despite the reduction in the tilj:llgth of their forces, Rochdale continued to tao the lion's share of the attacking, and than once the home team were hard put tiaalit to hold them in check, the impetuous 44it es of their forwards giving the defence in- -4102 trouble, to say nothing of many anxious thel,etits. Towards the close of the first half . and "arrington men pulled themselves together daie,4lade a determined incursion into Roch 7 l)qt 8 territory, but they were unable to hold th„ against the stout opposition of the Hornets' to4e(loarters, and the onslaught had no story aehltt. on the score-board. When the interval qti d it found the visitors leading by five °all. Points, and leaving in the minds of the h but a the impression that, had fortune F 441, a little kinder to them on one or two occa- 4'ntile margin, on their merits, should have '4l even wider one.</p>
        <p>ta WARRINGTON DRAW LEVEL. on in the second half Warrington thejq high the hopes of their supporters for, to eocceeded in equalising. Robinson failed. -Qeelit a pass f;om Jones, and Cox, dashing ball 44;:eabled his fellow forwards to rush the baj.,3"; Pinally, after a bright passing display, Ivllo-`eth handed the leather on to Cunlifle, Rot ' ettocessfully dodging a bunch of opponents, ,11'hiRrer the line and completed a good try, ix : t $l, ?ritchley further improved with a well h , . This success greatly ear g o treociledt at goal the home team. They put even. qpi,'er energy and spirit into their play an tlt,oottleid gamely for the lead. But fate—and 4`,4eir -4e----was against them, and gradually tt,lley. efforts grew weaker and weaker, until "le Were once again driven to act mainly on 4ttantefeusive. But not before a really spirited thei7. 011 their part had forced the Hornets to Iv44' line, an effort which, unhappily, was hiB pe'clAwing to Bradshaw knocking forward in 'llooavour to send Tranter over.</p>
        <p>poi BUT ONLY FOR A TIME. krt °wing on this came a big rally on the ,t4t tni the Rochdale men. There was then u.ft. minutes to go, and victory had yet to ,kr.°11..,• With a dash that was irresistible their Wir-rus got going, and well inside the time left the,ae,, ,s, y for the accomplishment of the task; Plgt,:lacl Put on the points necessary for War- ,4ll ts'll'a defeat. In a measure the fact was an unaccountable falling off among the t Mgt, On forwards; they seemed suddenly to Ne,lneces• Jones got away from a serum- ear the line, and evading the unwelcome ‘,l°llB of half a dozen would-be tackler!, over the line, completing a fine inch- °,,Wo a;,effert by adding the goal points to his 'talal:`3% Robinson figured in the next out• 441 Mg incident. He was placed in possession Iql:ae Making splendid headway on the righti When he hesitated, and, like the man n ,j• L'l'averb, was lost. A little later on Critch- a great attempt to place a goal, but struck the goalpost and rebounded into lest, with time close at hand Schofield, R. ob- e:/sIO-r,'; 3.unee, Corsi and Prudence joined in a L'u3 Passing movement, which enabled the N'e;;`,llled, after a splendid run, to get over, `colc the goal kick, but failed to pilot the between the uprights. When time was the score-board read: Rochdale Hor- Ro'al'i goals 3 tries (13 points); Warrington, try (5 POWs), • 46W 114 15LAVE ht PLAYED. f - the • Nised lnterests of candour it has to ble con d is P that Warrington were surprising y 441'lliting o • n Saturday. As tacticians, their o 111,d llitheu were anything but brilliant succeges, ;41 a forwards were decidedly below par, ‘ll(lged by previous exhibitions this season. Crit hl were the pick of the front ir Men in the `Lkt -rhe. but tle eY want of cohesion about lh tie °t-er f kere was atl first row which ren- rN deter= rve the most air cl determined efforts of t ort ?4elt'lel.resourceful players named. At r -half- alhteth proved, as usual, a useful man I;tteteci ePecial sphere, but he somewhat de- onfranileili B s vaa l y b his proneness to for- -1 li radshaw and Tranter were u ,I';kaliiek laof the home threequarters, but Jolley allailli!tilY himself at full-back, though occa- Ths.c.-ane caugl t glimpses of his old form. \ph:‘l)" 41:(Ulletsdteealrad-incluvdiezd, one the coun- Railant Robinson,., tilitnille_e,cl a defenders, ferfsUpough from his regi- Vio Corq.er to figure in the ranks of his old la. Wei's' and Prudence put in hard and and Schofield, in the half-back tl always sound and reliable. Of their -I;',C)lq es.nB. Slade and Carter were, perhaps. the -11sPiellous of the bunch, though all did til.ets have now beaten Warrington vv ilderso 1 this season, playing only qtelt- :Ill" on each occasion. In the League lilltesuel7 lost their full-back in the first few through an accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MANCHESTER • AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER • AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. W.EASTE.</p>
        <p>-- This match, in the First Division of the above League, was played on the Gorsey-lane enclo- sure before a fair attendance. Rylands' were short of Stretford (their captain), who has enlisted in the local Reserve Battalion of the Terriers," and Warren, who was under sus- pension. Weaste, who won the toss, played the Brothers Brown, of Warrington -" A." Brand- weed kicked off for Rylands', the ball being returned. Weaste took the offensive, but Hor- rocks relieved. Gallop fed his backs well, but Weaste defended soundly. Rylands' forwards, who were playing a grand game, gave the half-- backs every opportunity to open the game, and tries were scored by Povey, Horrocks and Row- linson, Shaw placing one goal. Half-time arrived. with the score: Rylands', one goal three tries kl 4 points); Weaste, nil. At the opening of the second half Weaste kept Rylands' on the defensive, but failed to get over the line. Half-way through this half Rylands' again began to force matters, and Povey, intercepting, raced over, the goal-kick failing. Other tries were scored by Potter, Penn, Horrocks and Rowlinson, the game ending in favour of Rylands' by 1 goal 8 tries (26 points) to nil. Gallop, Povey and Horrocks outshone the Weaste backs, whilst the Rylands' forwards commanded the scrummages. J. Brown, Hard- ing° and Alfleck were the pick of the visitors' team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>DEATH OF WELL-KNOWN N.U. FORWARD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF WELL-KNOWN N.U. FORWARD.</p>
        <p>Supporters and players alike of the Warring- ton Club—in fact, followers of the "Rugger" code the country over—will hear with regret of the death of that well-known Oldham player, "Bert" Avery, which sad event took place on Saturday, after an illness extending over 22 weeks, the cause of death being that dreaded disease, consumption. Avery was a great favourite with the Wilderspool crowd, and their sympathy will go to the Oldham Club in the great loss they have sustained. Avery, who was only thirty years of age, and came North from Devonport Albion, had been with the Old- ham Club for ten -years, and had done that club yeoman service. His was a record to be proud of. When only 16 years of age he made his first appearance for Devonshire County, when he was playing under the Rugby Union code. He was selected in four International trial games by the Rugby Union, and, in all, played 23 times for Devonshire. Since joining the Oldham Club, Avery, on a number of occasions, has played both for Lancashire and England, and was a member of the first Northern Union combination which was sent out to Australia. At one period he was the record-scoring forward of the Northern Union; indeed, he was a great "emergency" man anywhere on the field, and at different times did duty in every possible position. Avery was born at Buckfastleigh, and stood sft. 104 in., weighing about 14st. He played his last match for Oldham in the con- cluding game of last season, against Barrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Table up to Saturday, November 14th. P. W. D. L. For. Ag't. Ps St. James' 0.8. Parochial 0.8. Bolton C. 0.8. Wycliffe 0.8. Beamont U. 0.8. H'thside Froghall 0.8. 6 3 0 3 50 23 6 St. Mary's 0.8. Silver-street 0.8. Arpley-street 0.8. Sti Ann's 0.8. Sacred Heart 0.8. Beamont Ath. 0.8 4 20 2 16 70 4 St. Barnabas' 0.8. St. James' Rm. 0.8 5 1 0 4 34 129 2 St Alban' "B" 0.8 6 1 0. 5 5 24 137 2 Heathside Bewsey 0.8. 60 0 6 2 195 0 St. Alban's "A" 0.8 6O. 0 6 0 287 0</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S GAMES. At W, ilderspool. Kick-off 2 p.m. St. James Juniors 0.8. v. Silver-street 0.B• .; referee, J. Beardsell. At Victoria Park. Kick-off 3 p.m. No. I.—St. Barnabas' 0.8. v. St. James' 0.B.; W. Bibby. No. 2.—St. Alban's 0.8. "A" v. St. Ann's 0.B.; G. Crozier. No. 3.—St. Alban's 0.8. "B" v. Parochial; R. Williams. No. 4.—Heathside Bewsey 0.8. v. Wycliffe 0.B.; J. Flynn.</p>
