      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>WARRINGTON AWAY TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AWAY TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Team To Meet Widnes.</p>
        <p>Warrington journey to Widnes to-day, and the team to represent them on the Lowerhouse-lane ground was selected on Wednesday night as follows : Jolley. Taberner, Tranter, Brookes, Bradshaw. Daintith, Young. Cot, Clare, Cunliffe, Mather, Critchley, Fairhurst. . .</p>
        <p>It will be noted that Green's accustomed place on the forward line is allotted to Fairhurst, the reason being that the first-mentioned player is on the sick list.</p>
        <p>"A" TEAM AT HOME. The Warrington "A" team entertain the Widnes second string at Wilderspool this after- noon, and the following team has been chosen to represent the home club: Bramhall; Davies, Sherratt, Steel and Baker; Laws and ,Nicholls; Harmer, Thornton, Logan, Worrall, Fletcher, and Jordan. Both matches will commence at 2.0. This will be the first meeting between War- rington and Widnes this season.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. ABRAM RANGERS. At Gorsey-lane; kick-off 3 o'clock : Rylands': F. Horrocks; J. Griffiths, A. Povey, A. Horrocks and A. Rowlinson; J. Gallop and A. N. Other; J. Whittaker, J. Brandwood, F. Potter, E. Smith, W. Shaw and J. Baddely. Re- serves, A. Penn and J. Dumbell.</p>
        <p>If we could deceive others as easily as we deceive ourselves, what reputations we'd all have.—John Pomerene.</p>
        <p>When a woman is abstracted and silent a man always feels guilty; his only doubt is as to what particular thing she has found out.</p>
        <p>A man can interest a woman by telling her he is going to be married. But he cannot interest her by telling her that he is married.</p>
        <p>To make an enemy you have to possess a personality. To make a friend you only want the usual cake-making ingredients—lots of butter and sugar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Tributes After Forty Years of Poor Law Work.</p>
        <p>NO CHANGE IN MEETING DAYS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding.</p>
        <p>-APPOINTMENTS APPROVED.</p>
        <p>. The Local Government Board wrote approv- ing the appointment of Messrs. W. H. War- burton and J. Pearson as relieving officers of the Frodsham and Budworth districts respec- tively, with salaries at the rate of £lOO per annum each. The Board consented to the re- lieving officers holding the offices of registrars of marriages on the understanding that their duties in such capacity would ordinarily be confined to their districts, and subject to their giving a written undertaking to resign the ad- ditional office if at any time the Board or the Guardians should think it desirable. The officers could commence their new duties on the 16th inst. "SEND-OFF" TO AN OLD SERVANT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they ought to --giN;E: a hearty send-off to Mr. De Prez, who. for the last 40 years had rendered such valuable ser- vices, latterly as relieving officer of the Bud- worth district. The Local Government Board had complimented him on his work. They all wished him long life and prosperity, and a well- earned rest.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said he had known Mr. De Prez for 20 years, and he had always found him a loyal and conscientious servant of the Board. He had always shown sympathy with those who were dependent on the Board. Mr. Wright said Mr. De Prez was one of the most perfect relieving officers a Board could have.</p>
        <p>Alderman Norman and Messrs. Morris, Spruce, Hallows and Pilling also paid their tributes to the services of Mr. De Prez. Mr. Hallows said Mr. De Prez had always looked after the business of the Board, and at the same time had been sympathetic with the poor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling said he had known and admired Mr. De Prez when he was connected with the old Board at Warrington, and also since he came to the Runcorn Board. He was a most honourable and straightforward officer. Mr. De Prez suitably responded, saying that he had tried to do right, and he thought he had succeeded in most cases. PREMATURE'! Mr. Mclntyre was about to offer a welcome to Mr. Pearson as relieving officer for Stockton Heath, when the Chairman mentioned that that matter would come before the Board at the next meeting._</p>
        <p>M.P.: IN ACCORD WITH THE BOARD. The Local Government Board wrote formally acknowledging the receipt of a letter of the 4th inst. with reference to the pensions of soldiers and sailors.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. F. L. Brunner, M.P., wrote agreeint with the resolution, and said he was pleased to see in the newspapers that details of a pen- sion scheme would be published in a White Paper.</p>
        <p>WATCHING OVER THE CHILD-LIFE. The National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children wrote stating that the practical sympathy hitherto shown by the,Board in the work of the Society's local inspector in- duced them to lay before the Board the follow- ing facts: In the course of the past 12 months in this district 166 children had been dealt with, and the supervision visits to their homes numbered 412. The benefits secured to the children, the change in the habits of life of the parents, the improvement of home conditions, affected not only those directly concerned, but brought great advantage to the whole commu- nity. They hoped the Board would again show their appreciation of this work by renewing the contribution which was formerly given. It was decided to make the annual allowance of £2 2s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>SERVANTS' SUPERANNUATION.</head>
        <p>SERVANTS' SUPERANNUATION.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice given, Mr. Thos. Wright moved that a gratuity of £2l be granted to Miss E. E. Cunnah, the late assistant matron at the Workhouse, under the Poor Law 'Officers' Superannuation Act, 1896.</p>
        <p>tilt' MOOQn Wati A GRATUITY FOR THE MASTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris moved that the sum of £5 be paid to the Master of the Workhouse for services rendered at the Children's Home during the illness of the Superintendent.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce chairman of the Children's Home Committee) seconded, and the motion was carried. THE MEETINGS "AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gibbs moved "that in future, when the meetings of the District Council are not held the meetings of the Children's Home Com- mittee be held at 10.30 a.m.' the House Visit- ing Committee at 11 a.m., and the Board meet- ing at 11.30 a.m." In doing so, he said that that was the unanimous decision of the Board at the last meeting. He hoped that after the experi- ment had been tried the Union would have their meetings at that time every fortnight. Of course, that was a matter for the future, but he hoped they would all Support the resolution before them thlt morning. Alderman Norman expressed the opinion Mat some of the Runcorn members would not be able to get to the Board room in time. After further discussion Mr. Wood said he could not see the necessity for the alteration. The resolution made it practically impossible for the Runcorn members to attend. Mr. Brach moved an amendment to the effect that the Board of Guardians sit at 2.o'clock, and hold the committee meetings beforehand. Mr. Morris: That will penalise us more than ever. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling•seconded the amendment. Mr. Gibbs said that on the express wish of the Chairman of the Rural District- Council he wished to withdraw the motion which he had Put forward. It was agreed that Mr. Gibbs be allowed to withdraw his motion. The Board will therefore meet as usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Club Whose Members Have Nearly All Enlisted.</head>
        <p>Club Whose Members Have Nearly All Enlisted.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY'S AUSPICIOUS START.</p>
        <p>Owing to Saturday having previously been set apart for the first round of the Junior Medal Competition, and this having to be postponed on account of insufficient entries, there were not the usual number of matches down for decision in the local League, but of the matches played one of the most interesting was that in which Fairfield United strengthened their position at the head of the League chart by defeating the Central by 3 goals to 1. The victory was well merited, if only for the fact that the United were without the services of Ditchfield, Poniter, Payne and Roberts, who have trans- ferred their services to the Great Sankey Club. _ .</p>
        <p>Fairfield Athletic visited' Hollins Green and had to return defeated by five goals to nil. Longford United were disappointed by the non-appearance of the Latchford Baptists' team, and the matter is to be reported to the League Secretary, to be dealt with at the next League meeting.</p>
        <p>The Great Sankey Club are to be congratu- lated upon a splendid victory in their first engagement in the Widnes and District Alliance, their opponents being the Ball-o'-Ditton eleven. For a, to all intents and purposes, new team to get the ball through the uprights seven times— four goals being allowed and three disallowed— is a great performance, especially so when the feat is performed on foreign territory. It is all the more to be regretted that the Great Sankey Club were not allowed to play their team in the Second Division of the local League, when it had become impossible to obtain suffi- cient clubs to run the First Division. However, what has happened is that the Warrington League have lost a very good club with a very attractive team of players, who can be relied upon to give a good game and-always command a decent-sized crowd when on opponents' ground. The Widneg\Alliance seized the op- portunity of regaining the membership of the Great Sankey Club, which also means that they will retain their membership for next season as well-1915-16—as time goes a long way ahead. The Stockton Heath Club have also had to Seek re-admission to the North Cheshire League under very similar circumstances to those just mentioned.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman.”</p>
        <p>Roberts centred from the right and Ditehfield rushed in and headed the ball between the up- rights in splendid style, thus putting the visit- ors two goals up. The Sankeyites continued to have the best of the exchanges, and were all over the home team, whose goal had several narrow escapes. Aided by free kicks the home defenders managed at length to clear their area, but the forwards could not make much impres- sion on the visitors' defenders, and Brindle had little to do. Poulter and Houghton, by smart foot work, led another attack on the home quarters, which ended in .Whitfield scoring the third goal. From the centre kick the home- sters made several raids into the visitors' quar- ters, but they failed to pierce the defence. After a spell of even play in mid-field, the visitors again laid siege to the home goal, and Wigglesworth had to concede a corner. The flag kick was taken by Roberts without result, but shortly afterwards Poulter received the ball and with a grand shot beat the home cus- todian. From this on play was chiefly in favour of the visitors, and the home goal was nearly captured again, the ball just going past the post. The result was: Great Sankey, 4 goals; Ball-o'-Ditton, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. WARRINGTON CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the former, the home club having to re-arrange their team owing to the migration of four of their men to the re-constituted Great Sankey Club. The following players .turned out :</p>
        <p>Fairfield (fitted : Potter; Cox and Bebbing- ton; Gleave Lewis and Woodhouse; S. Wood- house, Hunter, Scott, Millward and Watkins. Central Hope; Glover and Williams; Goul- den, Brown and Williams; Mason, Hands, Farrill, Emson and Collins. The referee was Mr. B. Howell. The home team won the toss and Scott kicked off before a fair crowd of supporters. Nice combination by Millward and Hunter carried play into the Central goal-area, but Williams relieved with a timely kick. The home team, not to be denied, came again, and Scott opened the score after eight minutes' play. This reverse roused the visitors and they rushed towards the home goal, but finished in very erratic fashion. Gradually the home team gained the advantage, when offside against Scott sent them back. Millward and Watkins put the visitors' goal in danger, and a centre from the latter was promptly netted by Hunter. The Central made efforts to get on terms with their opponents, but were not successful. The half-time score was: United, 2 goals; Central, nil. On resuming, the visitors at once became the aggressors, but Lewis cleared at the critical moment. Watkins, gaining possession, raced away on the left, and, from his centre, Mill- ward was yards wide with his shot. The visi- tors' outside-left was doing well, when Gleave intercepted the ball and transferred. to S. Wood- house, who banged in a grand centre, from which Millward added a goal. The visitors made desperate attempts to open their account, but Emson shot badly when in good position. Goulden, at right half, plied his forwards with good passes, and Mason broke away, and centred well from the touch-line. Farrill was waiting for the ball, and, accepting the oppor- tunity, scored for the Central. Woodhouse tried a long shot at the opposite end, but the ball hit the upright. No further scoring took place, and time was called with the result : Fairfield 'United, 3 goals; Warrington Central, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN V. FAIRFIELD ATHLETIC This match was played 011 the ground of the first-named club, in ideal weather and before a fair muster of spectators, Mr. A. Holt having charge of the game. The visitors' captain had choice of ends, and, straight from the kick-off, the home team assumed aggressive methods, with the result that the visitors' defence was severely tested. Martin was successful in putting on the open- ing point at the end of five minutes' play. Owing to the visitors not being fully represented the home team had much their own way, and Mellor added a second point. After that the visitors showed an improvement, but it was not sufficiently marked to seriously trouble the home defenders, who easily held the upper hand. Martin scored again before the interval, which arrived with the scpre 3-0 in favour of the home players. On the resumption, the visitors defended very well and occasionally tested the home goal- keeper with long shots, which, however, were easily cleared. Hereabouts the home half-back line played very strongly and penned the visi- tors in their own half, a continual bombard- ment being kept up on their goal. Billington scored a fourth goal and Martin added a fifth before the final whistle blew. Result: Hollins Green, 5 goals; Fairfield Athletic, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>BALL-o'-DITTON V. GREAT SANKEY. The Great Sankey players opened their cam- paign on Saturday in the above competition by paying a visit to the ground of the first-named club. They met with a hearty reception from the spectators. The teams were as follows : itto n Wigglesworth; Currie and Mather; Lamb, Bride and Berry; Brandreth, Yeosley, Mellia, Baldwin and Chorley. Great San key: Brindle; E. Glover and Kelly; J. E. Glover, Millington and Payne; Roberts, Whitfield, Ditchfield, Poulter and Houghton. The referee was Mr. S. G. Essenhigh. The visitors won the toss and Mellia set the ball rolling. After an easy start the visitors settled down to their game, and the home de- fenders were called upon to clear their lines. But they could not hold the visitors, who pres- ently opened their score per Poulter. The home team tried hard to get on equal terms, but in E. Glover and Kelly they met a pair of stout defenders, and were driven back. After Ditchfield had missed the target, the game was evenly contested for a few minutes. The home team gained an advantage from a free kick, but off-side spoiled the effort. Kelly, being at fault, nearly let the home forwards through, but he soon made amends by a fine clearance from the attacking forwards, after which the game was transferred to the home quarters, where Currie and Mather played a fine resolute game and ! kept their lines intact. The home players gradually forced their way to the visitors' end, and tried strenuously to equalise. Off-side against them spoiled another good opening, and at the interval the visitors were still leading by one goal to none. . On changing ends the visitors made the pace and quickly put the homesters on the defensive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>RUNCORN AND CHESTER LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND CHESTER LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WESTON POINT B.C. v. BALFOUR-ROAD OLD BOYS.</p>
        <p>Played at Weston Point. The visitors won the toss and played with the sun at their backs. The opening exchanges .were very !quiet, and faulty kicking by the visitors' backs allowed the home forwards to get to close quarters, when, following good combination on the home right. C. Prince sent in a beauty, which the visitors' goalkeeper failed to hold, the ball going through off the upright. The visitors attacked, but could not defeat the home backs, being repeat- edly driven back. The home team returning to the attack, Reece, their centre-forward, scored a second goal. Play in mid-field was followed by raids on both goals. Offside against the 'visitors spoilt several good efforts. Grounds notched a third point for the home club. At half-time the score-board read: Bible Class, 3 goals; Old Boys, nil. In the second half .the home team shoved better combination than their opponents, and C. Prince again scored. Even play followed and then the visitors' centre had a splendid opening, but he failed to make use of it. The home team continued to play the better game, and Grounds and A. Reece added further points. Result: Weston Point Bible Class, 6 goals; Balfour-road Old Boys, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>MATCHES THAT DID NOT COME</head>
        <p>MATCHES THAT DID NOT COME</p>
        <p>OFF.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Latchford Baptists should have played Longford United. The latter turned up at the club's headquarters, but they were met by only three of the visiting players, and consequently no match took place. It appears that the Baptists' Committee have been taking drastic measures, owing to the lowly position their club occupies in the League, and with a view to improving their position they have sought to introduce outsiders in the club's playing strength. On the occasion when they gained their first point they had two or three players from Earlestown included in their eleven, and in the team that had been selected to oppose the United they were expecting eight players from Earlestown to figure in their ranks, but they failed to put in an appearance. The committee would do a wise thing to reinstate their own players at once, and thus avoid friction with the Church authorities with which the club is connected. Stockton Heath should have played their first home fixture of the season, but on Friday the secretary received word to say that the Hale- barn Lads, who were to have been their oppo- nents in a North Cheshire League tie, were un- able to fulfil the fixture as practically all their members had joined the Army during the week. This certainly was hard lines on the Stockton Heath Club, as they have played their first three engagements away from home, and were looking forward to this home fixture. The Halebarn Lads made it known three weeks ago that they would probably withdraw from the League. At that time they had not won a match, and their last defeat was 11-0 against them. _ .</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans failed to send a team to fulfil their engagement with the Elmwood Con- gregationalists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>A MATCH "BETWEEN THEMSELVES."</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Paddington League eleven , played their reserve team, and much to their surprise were soundly whacked by three goals to one. True, the League team were not at full strength, but, nevertheless, the reserves played stirring football, and their win was by no means a light thing.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY AL,BION v. BEWSEY ATHLETIC. These teams met in a friendly encounter on the Arpley ground on Saturday, a good game ending in a draw of.-two all.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY v. CRONTON.</p>
        <p>At Cronton, in the Widnes and District Alliance; kick-off 3 p.m.:— Great Sankey: H. Brindle; E. Glover and B. J. Kelly; J. E. Glover, 11. Ditehfield and H. Payne; T. Roberts, T. Whitfield, T. Hawthorn, S. Poulter and A. Houghton.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD-AVENUE v. BANK QUAY RECS. On the ground of the last-named:— Elmwood-avenue: James Phoenix; Jos. Phoe- nix and W. Hall; Jos. Pritchard, E. B. Mellor and W. Finch; W. Phoenix, Jos. Smith, A. N. Other,, T. Wells and A. Lamb. PADGATE UNITED v. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</p>
        <p>At Latchford Padgate United: Unsworth; Warburton and Potter; T. Woods, Gilbert and Williams; Woods, Ricketts, Ball, Higginson and S. Hard- man (captain). Reserve, H. Pass. Team meet at Warrington Bridge at 2.15 p.m. prompt. STOCKTON HEATH v. BROADHEATH LADS At West-avenue, Stockton Heath, in the North Cheshire League Stockton Heath: Hooper; Chesters and Mor- timer; Noden, Gosling and nylance; Lloyd, Pearce, Vernon, Macartney and Starkey. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. NEWTON FARM SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At Newton : Emmanuel : G. Bailey; E. Crozier and S. Hughes; A. N. Other, S. Banner and W. Ayres; T. Hughes, Walter Eaton, S. Roberts (captain), A. Oakes and William Eaton. Team meet at antral Station at 1.20; 'bus leaves 1.30.</p>
        <p>PADDINGTON A.F.C. v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>At Paddington. kick-off 2.45 : Paddington: G. Harrison; E. Holmes (cant.) and J. Chamberlain; W. Holmes, J. Taylor and W. Spencer; W. , Garnett, S. Taylor, T. Wood- head, J. Darbyshire and C. Smith. PADDINGTON A.F.C. RESERVES V. ELM- WOOD-AVENUE RESERVES.</p>
        <p>—..— On Elmwood's ground, Manchester-road:— Paddington Reserves: T. Knight (capt.); J. Dwerryhouse and T. Vickers; B. Chadwick, J. Brown and J. Adcroft; T. Wainwright, H. Wil- kinson, J. Holcroft, S. Summers and J. Picker- ing. Reserves: C. Forrest and Warburton.</p>
