      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>;TEENTII</head>
        <p>;TEENTII</p>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF YPRES.</p>
        <p>THE GREATEST BATTLE IN IHSTORY.</p>
        <p>The battle of Ypres, which has not yet ended, will rank as the greatest battle in history to date; certainly for Great Britain it is a second and greater Waterloo. Our troops have with- stood the shock of attacks by " droves " of German troops. They served all-comers alik?., and when the Kaiser selected the corpa of giants known as the Prussian Guards, thousands of men, none under six feet high, they anni- hilated the giants as they had annihilated the young students sent against them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>BAD WEATHER; MEN COMFORTABLE.</head>
        <p>BAD WEATHER; MEN COMFORTABLE.</p>
        <p>Snow has fallen in the northern corner of France. It is not expected that the winter will be severe enough to put a stop to fighting alto- gether. Attacks and counter-attacks will go on as usual, though often under the most trying conditions.</p>
        <p>I have talked, says a "Times" correspondent, to a few British soldiers who have been inval- ided back from the fighting line. They are victims of the severe November weather. The overcoats of these men are plastered with mud. They have fought in trenches sodden with rain. Their boots have sunk ankle-deep in the mud; they have stood nearly waist-deep in water. Rheumatism has developed. They are but a few. __</p>
        <p>_ Generally speaking, the health of our troops at the front is excellent, notwithstanding the cold and the rain. For this much credit is due to the fine work of the Army Service Corps in the field. Their organisation is wonderfully complete. Their convoys of motor-lorries are continuously at work between the railhead and the trenches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>LULL.</head>
        <p>A LULL.</p>
        <p>An Eye-witness present with General Head- quarters, in a dispatch dated November 16th and issued to the Press on Thursday night, carries on his account of the British operations in South-Western Belgium and Northern France. In a few days following the desperate assaults made on the Ist Army Corps by the Prussian Guard the enemy, although always active, did not attempt any decisive move. Hard and close fighting by British Infantry led to local advances. Eye-witness describes the dar- ing shown by German scouts and spies. The enemy's agents, it would seem, constantly find their way through the British lines to cut the telephone wires from the trenches. ALLIES FIRM. - -</p>
        <p>German attacks have been repulsed and the Allies have advanced south-east of Ypres. Several vantage points lost to the enemy some days ago are now once more held by Allied troops. Dixmude, whose capture delighted all Germany last week, has not much benefited the enemy. The inundations that ended the at- tempt to break through on the coast have spread past Dixmude towards Ypres, denying the Germans passage. On Tuesday Paris reported that during the last two days more or less marked progress had been made at every point attacked—at Hetsas on the -Veer, between Armentieres and Arras, in the Vailly region, in the Argonne, and on the heights of the Meuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>"MAINTAINED ITS FRONT."</head>
        <p>"MAINTAINED ITS FRONT."</p>
        <p>"The British Army maintained its front" was the laconic announcement in the French official communique issued on Wednesday afternoon. A British Press Bureau statement explained what this meant.</p>
        <p>The 3rd Division, we were told, was subjected yesterday to a heavy attack, first by artillery and then by infantry, the brunt of both falling upon two battalions of the division. These were shelled out of their trenches, but they recovered them after a brilliant counter- attack, which drove the enemy back in disorder some 500 yards. During the day an attack was also made on a brigade of the Second Division. In this the enemy were repulsed with heavy loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>ARTILLERY ACTIVE.</head>
        <p>ARTILLERY ACTIVE.</p>
        <p>Renewed activity was shown by the German artillery on Thursday along the front in Flan- ders, especially to the north, next the sea. There were no signs, however, of any immediate in- tention of the enemy to renew the infantry attack.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>NORTHERN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>GERMAN BOASTING; FRENCH SUCCESSES.</p>
        <p>The German Wireless News claims a success in the Argonne.</p>
        <p>The French have issued an official statement denying the assertions made by the Germans that the Allies have been driven out of the Argonne Forest. The fighting front of the Allied Army in this quarter has not varied ap- preciably during the past two months. In the Argonne and beyond the Meuse the enemy have suffered several repulses. The French have captured part of a village on the outskirts of St. Mihiel. The village is the only "point d'appui" still held by the enemy on the left bank of the Meuse. The bombardment of Reims by the Germans has been resumed—a sure sign that the enemy have been punished again in that locality. On the left bank of the Meuse, where the Germans hold the village of Chauvoncourt, there has been more fighting, but no decisive result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>HEAVY GERMAN LOSSES.</head>
        <p>HEAVY GERMAN LOSSES.</p>
        <p>The'French communique reports that between the Oise and the Aisne the Germans have been expelled, with heavy losses, from the village of Tracy le Val, which the French troops recently won from them. Further east there was no noteworthy change.</p>
        <p>RUSSIAN CAMPAIGN. GERMANS TURN AT BAY. ,</p>
        <p>Towards the middle of last week, while the Russians were progressing with their invasion of Poland and inflicting the customary punish- ment upon the Austrians, a "German offensive" was noticed in the direction of Thorn and on both banks of the Vistula towards Wloclawek (20 miles from the frontier), and further to the west. It was ascertained that the enemy had brought up some of the troop ek from Lyck (about 160 miles N.E. of Thorn). Russian troops occupied Tarnow and were gathering to the siege or investment of Cracow. Strong German armies, gathered to prevent an invasion of East Prussia, advanced along both banks of the Vistula. Temporarily at least they were successful. Berlin reports the capture of nearly 30,000 with 70 machine guns. Although both Berlin and Petrograd were sparing of news about events on Germany's eastern frontier, it was clear that between the Vistula and the Warta great forces were in con- flict. Russian official reports, while admitting a withdrawal of the advanced guards operating against the enemy, gave no details. Neither side has yet been broken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>BERLIN DECORATED:.</head>
        <p>BERLIN DECORATED:.</p>
        <p>—-.- - We were informed, however, via Copenhagen, that on the 17th inst. Berlin was decorated in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>RIDGE STREET, GrAnd elothinq H B all WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>RIDGE STREET, GrAnd elothinq H B all WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 21, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>lc A TIPPS !</head>
        <p>lc A TIPPS !</p>
