        <p>Summary of Authentic News From Ail</p>
        <p>Involved Areas.</p>
        <p>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>WEEK.</p>
        <p>honour of General von Hindenburg's victory. The total Russian losses were estimated at be- tween forty and fifty thousand, and a consider- able number of field cookers were taken.</p>
        <p>It was explained by Russia that the Germans had succeeded in concentrating suddenly in one region, that the Russian advance parties had been withdrawn, but that the strategy of their campaign as a whole was unaffected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>TEMPORARY CH.ECK.</head>
        <p>A TEMPORARY CH.ECK.</p>
        <p>The repulse of the Russians by General von Hindenburg on the front between the Vistula and the Warta does not appear likely to prove more than a temporary check. The Russian Headquarters Staff on that part of the front reports that the advance guards of our Ally were on Thursday falling back and that the Germans had advanced as far as Piontek.</p>
        <p>BELGIUM'S FATE OVERTAKING EAST PRUSSIA.</p>
        <p>In East Prussia the Germans are giving way before the invaders, whose progress, although rot rapid, is steady. Many thousand fugitives who have fled from their homes in East Prussia before the threat of a Russian invasion are arriving in Berlin. The Russians have imposed on conquered East Prussian towns war contributions correspond- ing approximately to the war contributions which the Germans imposed on Belgian towns of a corresponding number of inhabitants. The Prussian military authority has ordered all houses to be burned to prevent the Russians from getting shelter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>CRACOW THREATENED.</head>
        <p>CRACOW THREATENED.</p>
        <p>Russian and Austrian reports agree in stating that an action on a large scale is developing in the south towards Cracow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>TURKEY</head>
        <p>TURKEY</p>
        <p>HAS NOT MADE A GOOD START.</p>
        <p>Turkey has not begun the war well. British warships have damaged the forts on the Dar- danelles, at Akaba near the Egyptian frontier, and at Fao on the Shatt-el-Arab in the Persian Gulf. Now the Navy, with military help, has driven the Turks with loss from the defence works at Sheikh Said which watched the further entrance to the Red Sea, while an official an- nouncement concerning navigation OR the Shatt-el-Arab implies that a British expedition has advanced a considerable distance towards Basra. On the Russian frontier things go no better for the Ottoman Empire. Despite vio- lent efforts the Turks have failed to drive the invaders from their territory. East of Erzerum their own cavalry have been vigorously repulsed. An official account of the Sheik Said opera- tions shows they were completely successful. Three battalions of Indian infantry were landed under cover of fire from the cruiser Duke of Edinburgh. The Turks were utterly defeated and most of them captured, together with much material and six field guns. Aden is not far from Sheik Said, which, says the "Manchester Guardian," would provide an excellent base for an attack on the valuable British possession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>ENTRENCHED POSITION STORMED.</head>
        <p>ENTRENCHED POSITION STORMED.</p>
        <p>• A smart success has been scored against the Turkish forces on the Shat-el-Arab River in the Persian Gulf. The General in command reports that on November 17th his force stormed an entrenched position, strongly occupied, and took two guns and many prisoners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>AKABA BOMBARDMENT.</head>
        <p>AKABA BOMBARDMENT.</p>
        <p>The "Times" correspondent at Cairo reports that the Minerva has again bombarded Govern- ment buildings at Akaba and has landed a party which has driven out a hostile force officered by Germans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>UNITED STATES CRUISER ATTACKED.</head>
        <p>UNITED STATES CRUISER ATTACKED.</p>
        <p>. . A launch belonging to the United States cruiser Tennessee has been fired upon by the Turks while entering the port of Smyrna. The captain has reported the matter to Washington, whence instructions have been sent to the United States Ambassador in Constantinople.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>AFRICA. HEAVY FIGHTING AGAINST ODDS</head>
        <p>AFRICA. HEAVY FIGHTING AGAINST ODDS</p>
        <p>IN THE EAST.</p>
        <p>• Statements on the employment of Indian troops were made in Parliament on Wednesday. Indian soldiers are now doing duty in France and Belgium, Egypt, on the coast near Aden, in East Africa, on the Persian Gulf, and at Tsinc,'- tau. Lord Crewe, who spoke in the Lords, made it plain that the East Alrican position was not satisfactory. When war began the German colony had a much greater military strength than had the neighbouring British possessions. The balance has since been re- dressed, but the fighting has not gone entirely well. During two months the British forces suffered 900 casualties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>BOTIIA'S PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>BOTIIA'S PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Official dispatches from South Africa describe operations against sporadic bands of rebels in the Transvaal and Orange Free State provinces. General Botha's work of suppressing the South African rebellion goes on steadily. Beyers, with some 1,500 men, was met by loyal troops in the north-west of the Orange Free Sta,te on Novem- ber 15th. The rebels were attaclted on a front many miles long, put to flight, and pursued for several hours. Rifles, waggons, carts, amrnuni. tion, and horses fell into the loyalists' hands, as did 100 prisoners. A distinctly good effect was produced on the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>GOEBEN DAMAGED I PRINCE OF RUSSIAN BLACK SEA SUCCESS. I GOES TO TIIE FRONT AIDE-DE-C.AMP.</head>
        <p>GOEBEN DAMAGED I PRINCE OF RUSSIAN BLACK SEA SUCCESS. I GOES TO TIIE FRONT AIDE-DE-C.AMP.</p>
        <p>The Russian fleet meeting the Goeben and Breslau on Wednesday in the Black Sea attacked them. By use of her high speed the Breslau avoided an engagement, but her super- Dreadnought consort was compelled to fight and was damaged. A series of explosions are stated to have taken place in the Goeben, which took flue.</p>
        <p>The Goeben, German built and German manned, was much more powerful a vessel than any of her opponents, of whom the chief, the Sviatoi Evstafi, displaced only 12,000 tons against 22,000, and carried as a main armament only four 12in. guns against her ten llin. pieces.</p>
        <p>GERMAN NAVAL LOSSES. CRUISERS INTERNED. I</p>
        <p>BATAVIA, Tuesday. Commander Lubcke and the wireless operator Voltz, of the German warship Preussen, which is interned at Sabang Bay, have been arrested by the naval authorities. The communication between the antenna of the wireless rooms had</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales crossed from Fo , nee' to Boulogne on Monday. Tuesday night's "London Gazette" annt'oeB that Second-Lieutenant Edward, ',.,ipited of Wales, Grenadier Guards, has been aPi P'veld' under Monday's date, an aide-de-camP to' Marshal Sir J. French.</p>
        <p>PRINCE RANJITSINILII FOR FRONT. --Anavr•</p>
        <p>FOR WARM I 7 oVERCOA7</p>
        <p>_Aid</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>Worth orq._y</head>
        <p>Worth orq._y</p>
