        <p>LEATHER IS DEARER! Owing to the enormous quantity of Boots required for the Armies of the Allies, practically all of which have to be made in England, there is no possible doubt that Boots are certain to be MUCH DEARER. The Manufacturers demand an increase of 10 per cent. to 20 per cent., and, in certain kinds of Boots, up to even 30 per cent. Since the outbreak of War the price of LEATHER has risen by nearly one-half. It is evident that the Public will have to pay largely-increased prices, or accept an inferior article—one or the other. ) WE ARE, HOWEVER, IN THE FORTUNATE POSITION TO BE ABLE TO OFFER -LARGE STOCKS OF BOOTS, SHOES AND SLIPPERS AT PRICES RULING BEFORE THE WAR. We strongly advise our friends to take advantage of these Stocks, which represent the best possible value. R. ROBERTS &amp; SON 19, BRIDGE STREET.</p>
