        <p>DESERTION CASES. ABSCONDING ARTILLERYMAN. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, John Mills, who had previously been charged with deserting from the 27th Battery of the Royal Field Artillery, was ordered to be handed over to a military escort. ABSENTED HIMSELF OUT OF PIQUE. Edgar Smithson, a private in the 3rd Batta- lion South Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Great Crosby, pleaded guilty at the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday to the charge of being an absentee since November 14th.—Mr. T. S. Steel said he was instructed by the family to appear for the prisoner; although, of course, the Bench had no alternative but to hand him over•to an escort. The family desired him to explain that they were very sorry and disgusted at the prisoner's arrest as an absentee. The family was well known in Warrington, and desired to assure the Bench that their brother had not absented himself on any grounds of " funk" or lack of desire to serve his country; lie had been in the Army a number of years, and went through the South African War. Tie was also a member of the National Reserve. He desired to go to the front, and had, on two, occasions, voluntarily offered to go; but his age was against him. There was an idea in the Army, continued Mr. Steel, that, if they did such a foolish thing as prisoner had done, it would help them to get to the front earlier; and he had simply acted out of pique.—Prisoner was remanded to await an escort.</p>
